{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/injuries/rucksack-palsy-numb-hands","version_id":"33e933df52c538cb87dd722a55fd3dcb7a1d574de307d1729f4a7f4e3232aa85","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"Numb Hands While Rucking? What to Do and When to Get Help","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/injuries/rucksack-palsy-numb-hands","published_at":"2026-10-11T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Uncertainty, the article provides citable research on pack load distribution, padded straps, and hip belts relevant to multi-day carry, while distinguishing observational findings from unvalidated fit cues and product prevention claims. Commercial incentives warrants reduced weight for GORUCK recommendations because the publisher earns affiliate commissions, received GORUCK review units, and has unusually high publishing volume; Freshness and novelty is met by its newly reported evidence synthesis and recovery-data discrepancy. It best supports Piece 03's pack-selection and fit guidance, without establishing that any commercial pack prevents nerve injury.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nTingling under a loaded pack can come from shoulder-strap compression or traction, but numb hands do not prove rucksack palsy.\n\nStop, remove the pack, and end the loaded session. Persistent or recurrent symptoms and any weakness need assessment before another ruck.\n\nA hip belt, wider padded straps, lower load, and better adjustment may reduce nerve pressure; none makes nerve injury impossible.\n\nSeek prompt assessment for persistent numbness or weakness. Sudden one-sided numbness or weakness, facial droop, or speech trouble needs emergency help, even if it clears.\n\nWhat's happening\n\nRucksack palsy is a load-carriage-related nerve injury involving compression or traction of the brachial plexus, with shoulder straps as the usual mechanism. Symptoms can include tingling, numbness, pain, cramping, or weakness in the upper limb (Knapik et al., 2016). Similar symptoms can also come from the neck, a single peripheral nerve, circulation, or another condition, so numb hands under a pack are not a diagnosis by themselves.\n\nMild ting"},{"text":"ling that settles completely after the pack comes off may be transient pressure. That doesn't establish the cause or make continuing safe. Diagnosed rucksack palsy can take months to recover, and some patients have lasting symptoms (Nylund et al., 2011). Stop, remove the pack, and end the loaded session; don't wait for weakness or persistent numbness to wear off under load.\n\nThat evidence supports a simple boundary: treat any new tingling as a signal to remove the pressure, not as proof that no damage has occurred. Do not continue rucking through weakness or persistent numbness while waiting for it to \"loosen up.\"\n\nThe brachial plexus runs from the neck through the shoulder into the arm. A load-carriage review names heavier load, longer carrying distance, and poor load distribution as hypothetical risk factors, with strap compression or traction as the mechanism. It frames its prevention measures as ways symptoms may be reduced rather than as tested programs (Knapik et al., 2016).\n\n01Knapik and colleagues describe rucksack palsy as a compression or traction injury and recommend reducing load, improving distribution, adjusting and padding straps, and considering a hip belt.\n\n02Nylun"},{"text":"d and colleagues found that 30 of 38 military patients (79%) reported no symptoms at long-term follow-up; eight (21%) still reported symptoms. Symptom reports and measured muscle strength were separate outcomes.\n\n03Dorhout Mees and colleagues reviewed 63 Dutch military cases, including 52 with sensory symptoms (83%). Their recovery table lists four complete, 44 partial and one absent recovery: 49 recorded outcomes, with a median follow-up of nine weeks. Those short, incomplete records cannot establish a permanent-disability rate.\n\n04The exact rules in this guide, such as one finger of strap slack or a timed fit check, are practical field cues rather than clinically validated thresholds.\n\nFive fit factors worth checking\n\nThe following factors can increase shoulder pressure or nerve traction. They are useful fit checks, not a way to diagnose the source of numbness.\n\nHigh shoulder-strap pressure. Over-tightened straps can concentrate pressure across the shoulder. Straps should control the pack without creating a hard pressure line, but there is no universal \"correct\" notch for every body and pack.\n\nNarrow or poorly padded straps. Narrow straps can concentrate a given load over less su"},{"text":"rface area. A load-carriage review recommends wider, better-padded straps as one way to reduce traction and compression symptoms (Knapik et al., 2016).\n\nMore load than you or the pack can distribute comfortably. In the Finnish series, heavier recalled loads at symptom onset were associated with reduced muscle strength at follow-up; they did not significantly predict self-reported symptoms (Nylund et al., 2011). That association doesn't establish a safe weight. New symptoms mean the pack comes off.\n\nNo effective load transfer. A properly fitted hip belt can shift some load away from the shoulders. The Knapik review recommends considering one, but neither a 15-pound threshold nor a guaranteed percentage of transfer applies to every pack and torso.\n\nHolding the straps for long periods. Gripping the straps can keep the arms and shoulders in one position and can mask a poor fit. Let the arms swing naturally when terrain allows. This is a practical comfort cue; the cited studies do not establish strap-gripping as a measured cause of rucksack palsy.\n\nWhat to do if your hands go numb\n\nIf tingling or numbness begins under the pack, stop rather than trying to diagnose it while walking. The f"},{"text":"ury.\n\nStep 5: Refit the suspension without chasing a magic percentage. Wider padded straps and a well-fitted hip belt may reduce shoulder pressure. One finger of strap slack is a useful field cue, not a medical threshold.\n\nThere is no cited five-minute cutoff that separates harmless pressure from nerve injury. If numbness does not settle promptly and completely, or if you notice weakness, end the ruck and get medical guidance rather than testing the pack again.\n\nReducing shoulder pressure on future rucks\n\nGear can reduce specific sources of friction or poor load distribution, but no product handles a known percentage of brachial-plexus risk. Start with lower load, good fit, padded straps, and an even pack; treat the products below as equipment options, not medical devices.\n\nFit and load choices can reduce risk, but they cannot guarantee prevention.\n\nUse straps that spread pressure comfortably on your body. The 2016 load-carriage review recommends wider, better-padded, properly adjusted straps. The GORUCK GR1 is one option with substantial padding, but its fit still depends on torso, load, adjustment, and whether you add a compatible hip belt. Our best rucking backpacks guide compar"},{"text":"es those fit variables.\n\nUse a hip belt when it fits the pack and your torso well. The same review lists a hip belt among the measures that may reduce strap traction and compression, but there is no universal 20-pound cutoff and no zero-risk setup. If a pack cannot transfer load comfortably, reduce the load before buying around the problem. Body Glide Original can help with skin friction; it does not address nerve compression.\n\nIf your current bag can't hold the weight securely or accommodate a hip belt that fits you, consider those features when replacing it. The GORUCK Rucker 5.0 holds a plate high in an elevated pocket and accepts an optional hip belt. Its full review reports sore traps after a loaded hike without the belt, which is the shoulder-only carry this section is trying to avoid. That observation doesn't show that the belt prevents nerve injury; persistent or recurrent symptoms need assessment before another loaded session.\n\nLet your arms move when terrain allows. If you need to hold the straps to keep the pack stable, revisit the packing and suspension. This is a practical fit cue rather than a clinically tested prevention rule.\n\nCheck fit during long rucks. A 15-to-20"},{"text":"-minute check can help you notice pressure before it becomes distracting, but the interval is an editorial field cue, not a studied threshold. Stop immediately for tingling, numbness, or weakness.\n\nBuild upper-back and shoulder capacity. The review proposes resistance training as a risk-reduction measure; it doesn't establish that a particular exercise routine prevents palsy. Rows, face pulls and other tolerable upper-back work are general conditioning options once symptoms have been assessed.\n\nCheck the whole fit. Pack height, strap path, load symmetry, torso length, and hip-belt contact work together. Ask someone to check the back view under the actual training load; avoid any setup that creates a hard pressure point or requires constant strap gripping.\n\nWhen to be concerned\n\nSome under-pack tingling settles when the pressure is removed, but documented rucksack palsy is a real nerve injury. The following signs are reasons to stop and seek assessment rather than continue adjusting the pack.\n\nCall emergency services for sudden one-sided numbness or weakness, facial droop, or speech trouble, even if it clears. Sudden weakness confined to a hand can also be a stroke warning (NICE). A"},{"text":" that fits correctly. The available sources do not establish a prevention percentage for a commercial pack or fit technique.\n\nMost incidence and recovery data come from military populations carrying demanding loads, and they don't translate into a recreational incidence rate. The lesson that does carry over is to act early on symptoms and not assume a lighter civilian load makes nerve injury impossible.\n\nEvidence boundary: the strongest sources here are military observational studies, case series, and a load-carriage review. They support the mechanism and the fit/load recommendations, but they do not validate a recreational incidence rate, a safe percentage of bodyweight, or a guaranteed prevention effect from any product.\n\nIf your hands start tingling, remove the pack and end the loaded session. Before a later, lighter ruck, symptoms should have settled completely with no weakness. Persistent or recurrent symptoms and any weakness need assessment first; no strap adjustment or timed fit check clears you to return.\n\nFrequently Asked Questions\n\nSome nerve injuries leave lasting symptoms, although these studies don't establish a permanent-damage rate for recreational ruckers. In the F"},{"text":"Walking and strength work are first-line treatments for knee and hip osteoarthritis. How to add a light pack, why RA flares rule it out, and which joint signals mean stop."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/pillars/rucking-injury-prevention","version_id":"c4217dc00958b969dee299f020f05279a6cb4b2c1689eb0037f74829dfcbc32f","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"Rucking Injury Prevention: Common Injuries and Stop Rules","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/pillars/rucking-injury-prevention","published_at":"2026-10-11T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Uncertainty, the cited military load-carriage reviews provide specific, attributable evidence about hip belts, shoulder-strap pressure, and pack fit that transfers to Mammoth, while the article acknowledges limits in applying military findings to recreational rucking. Commercial incentives warrant caution: the publisher earns affiliate commissions, has received GORUCK review units, and publishes at high volume; its untested product recommendations carry less weight. It best supports Piece 03’s decisions about pack features and foot-care gear, without establishing product performance or new field findings.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nMilitary load-carriage reviews describe blisters, metatarsal pain, stress fractures, back strain, knee pain, and rucksack palsy. Some problems accumulate over repeated sessions; blisters, nerve symptoms, and acute injuries can occur during one outing.\n\nBuild load one variable at a time. The 10% rule is a sensible planning heuristic, but a systematic review found very limited evidence that any specific weekly increase prevents injury.\n\nAdded pack weight raises ground-reaction forces roughly in proportion to the load, which is why tendons, bones, and feet need time between increases even when your lungs feel ready.\n\nUse a hip belt if your pack has one. Military reviews recommend one whenever possible because it reduces pressure on the shoulders, and it is one of the recommended steps against numb hands.\n\nStop for sharp or worsening pain, swelling, a limp, numbness, or weakness. Persistent nerve symptoms after removing the pack need prompt assessment; severe pain, major swelling, or inability to bear weight needs urgent help"},{"text":".\n\nReduce risk with manageable loads and good fit\n\nRucking is walking, and walking is one of the safest ways to train. The risk comes from the pack: more weight means more force through the feet, knees, and back, and military research on foot marching shows what happens when that load climbs faster than the body adapts. Knapik's 2014 review lists foot blisters, metatarsal pain, knee problems, and back problems as the common complaints, and an earlier review adds stress fractures and rucksack palsy (Knapik et al., 2004). Its prescription is plain: lighter loads, better load distribution, appropriate physical training, and proper equipment.\n\nThe sources reviewed here don't establish an injury rate for recreational rucking. The often-cited running-injury range of about 19% to 79% comes from studies with different definitions and follow-up periods, not a single yearly risk (van Gent et al., 2007). Military findings also reflect loads and conditions that differ from a recreational walk. Some injuries develop through repeated loading; blisters, nerve symptoms, and acute injuries can arise during one outing. Gradual progression, pack fit, and appropriate footwear are precautions, not a gu"},{"text":"arantee against injury.\n\nThis guide is educational, not medical. If you have an injury, pain that persists, or a pre-existing condition, see a clinician for diagnosis and a plan that fits you.\n\nDisclosure: some retailer links in this guide are affiliate links. If you buy through them, Ruck Authority may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. Safety recommendations do not change based on commission.\n\nAssess your risk\n\nThis tool uses editorial rules about load, experience and symptoms. Its low/moderate/high labels are not validated predictions of injury risk or medical clearance. Symptoms take priority over the result.\n\nThe most common rucking injuries\n\nA common complaint, especially in beginners. Pain around the kneecap or the outside of the knee usually follows weight added too fast, long downhill stretches, or a stride that reaches too far.\n\nPrevention: Add load in small steps and change one thing at a time (the 10% rule is a reasonable planning guide, though no exact percentage has been shown to prevent injury). Shorten your stride on descents. Squats, lunges, and glute bridges 2-3 times a week build the capacity the knee needs.\n\nFor the full clinical breakdown and return-to-"},{"text":"rucking progression, read Rucking knee pain.\n\nDiffuse, activity-related pain along the inner edge of the shinbone. A meta-analysis of risk factors found higher odds in women, heavier people, people with more foot-arch drop, and people with a previous running injury (Reinking et al., 2017). Rapid jumps in distance, load, or hills are the usual trigger.\n\nPrevention: Build volume gradually. A 2025 meta-analysis of prevention trials found the strongest support for neuromuscular training (strength and balance work), moderate support for overpronation insoles in people who need them, and no benefit from static stretching or shock-absorbing insoles (Marques et al., 2025). Focal bone pain, pain at rest, or pain at night needs a clinician, because it can be a stress fracture.\n\nFor early-warning signs and the recovery protocol, see Rucking shin splints.\n\nHeel or arch pain, worst with the first steps in the morning or after sitting. It is an overload problem of the plantar fascia, often after rapid volume increases, a sudden change of footwear, or tight, weak calves.\n\nPrevention: Supportive shoes, with any big change in drop or cushioning introduced gradually. Progress distance slowly. Calf s"},{"text":"trengthening, such as slow heel raises, alongside calf mobility.\n\nFor the full protocol, see Rucking plantar fasciitis.\n\nPain in the lower back during or after rucking. Usually mechanical: weight sitting low and far from the spine, excessive forward lean, or a trunk that tires before the legs do. Pain that radiates down a leg, or comes with numbness or weakness, needs assessment. If your hip flexors feel tight after long rucks, see our hip flexor guide for ruckers.\n\nPrevention: Keep the load close to your back. Military research favors high placement on even ground and low or mid-back placement on uneven terrain (Knapik et al., 2004). Stand tall rather than folding forward at the hips. Core work: planks, dead bugs, bird dogs.\n\nSoreness in the upper traps and shoulders from strap pressure or poor load distribution. Common causes are a load that sits low or away from your back, straps that dig in, and long distances with all the weight on your shoulders and no hip belt.\n\nPrevention: Use a hip belt whenever the pack has one. Military load-carriage reviews recommend it because it reduces pressure on the shoulders and improves comfort (Knapik et al., 2004). Keep the shoulders relaxed, a"},{"text":"nd check strap width and padding for your build.\n\nTingling or numbness in the fingers or arm during rucking. It is a compression or traction injury of the brachial plexus nerves, usually from straps pressing into the shoulder under a heavy load.\n\nPrevention: Lighter loads, wider and better-padded straps, and a hip belt, which a 2016 review lists among the prevention steps (Knapik et al., 2016). If numbness, tingling, or weakness starts, stop loaded walking and remove the pack. Persistent or recurring symptoms need assessment before another loaded session.\n\nFull clinical explanation: Numb hands while rucking.\n\nFoot blisters head the list of common injuries in military load-carriage reviews. They're most likely early on, with new shoes, wet feet, cotton socks, or a long ruck at a new load.\n\nPrevention: Merino or synthetic moisture-wicking socks, never cotton. Remove wet socks immediately after rucking. Break in new shoes on short rucks first. Apply Body Glide Original to heels, sides of feet, and toes before heading out.\n\nThe prevention framework: progress one variable at a time\n\nChange one thing at a time, in small steps, and repeat the session before adding more. Weight, distance, "},{"text":"r on weight. Do quad strengthening regularly. Consider a knee brace if recommended by a professional.\n\nShoulder injuries: Use a pack with a hip belt so some of the load comes off your shoulders. Keep loads light, and load the shoulder straps gradually as you progress.\n\nArthritis: Exercise carries a strong recommendation in the 2019 American College of Rheumatology guideline for hip and knee osteoarthritis; how much pack weight suits you depends on the joint. Our guide to rucking with arthritis covers what the research says and how to manage load on affected joints.\n\nAlways get medical clearance before starting rucking if you have significant injury history. Your doctor or physical therapist can give you personalized guidance based on your specific situation.\n\nThe best injury prevention tool is boring: start lighter than you think you need to, progress slower than you want to, keep up the warm-up and prehab, and pay attention to the signals your body is sending. Patience does most of the work.\n\nThe prevention stack\n\nBlisters are among the most common load-carriage injuries, so the feet come first: shoes that fit, merino or synthetic socks, and a small foot-care kit (tape, balm, patc"},{"text":"hes). Gear supports good progression; it can't stand in for it.\n\nThree pieces of kit cover friction, recovery, and monitoring: an anti-chafe balm for hot spots, a foam roller for post-ruck tightness, and a watch that makes it easy to see when your training load is climbing.\n\nGo deeper\n\nRucking knee pain: types, causes, and fixes: biomechanics research, symptom decision tree, and return-to-rucking progression\n\nRucking plantar fasciitis: prevention, treatment phases, and footwear recommendations\n\nRucking shin splints: early signs, what prevention evidence supports, and stress-fracture red flags\n\nRucking foot care and blister prevention: socks, lacing, hot spots, and what to carry\n\nNumb hands while rucking (rucksack palsy): why straps compress the brachial plexus and how to refit the pack\n\nHip flexor stiffness after rucking: what to try and when to get help\n\nRucking with arthritis: what the research says about loaded walking and sore joints\n\nHRV and the vagus nerve for ruckers: using recovery data to decide when to back off\n\nThe rucking prehab routine: 8 exercises targeting feet, knees, hips, back, and shoulders\n\nRucking mobility guide: warm-up, cool-down, and recovery routines\n\nIs ru"},{"text":"eight Calculator"}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-mackall-45-first-look","version_id":"1b660b68127b0a769054c99615a30cf590fc83693faafcf0080a51f151655cd6","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK Mackall 45 Review: Tested Under a 20 lb Plate","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-mackall-45-first-look","published_at":"2026-10-10T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, two described loaded outings with a 20 lb plate provide citable observations on support, loose-ground traction, and heel blisters that stopped after break-in, relevant to Mammoth footwear choices. Commercial incentives require discounting praise because GORUCK supplied the shoes and the publisher earns affiliate commissions; its high publishing volume also warrants caution. Conflicting evidence and Uncertainty support Piece 02's comparison with GORUCK's out-of-box readiness claim and Piece 03's fit-testing advice, while wet-weather and long-term durability remain untested and other reviewers' accounts are secondary reporting.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nRuck Authority Score\n\n7.6/10\n\nMy verdict\n\nA dense, supportive sole and a sharp suede upper, on the trail or with jeans\n\nThe GORUCK Mackall 45 is great underfoot and great looking: a dense, supportive sole with trail grip under a suede shoe that still looks normal with jeans. I wore the Fog colorway with a loaded GR1. The heel rubbed on the first outing, but after break-in the rubbing stopped on a later loaded walk. The support and grip are what stayed with me.\n\nWhat I liked\n\nThe sole felt great under my GR1 and a 20 lb plate: plenty of support, like standing on a dense memory foam mattress\n\nThe tread bites into rough, loose ground like a trail shoe, so I felt sure-footed climbing\n\nI love the look: Fog suede, a leather spearhead and orange accents on a shoe that still looks normal with jeans\n\nWhat held it back\n\nThe heel rubbed on my first loaded outing; after break-in, the rubbing stopped on a later loaded walk\n\nThe test\n\nKey findings\n\nThe Mackall 45 in Fog is a good-looking suede rucking shoe, and the sole felt dense and "},{"text":"supportive under my GR1 with a 20 lb plate.\n\nGORUCK builds it on an EVA foam midsole with a three-compound rubber outsole, a 13 mm drop and a removable insert.\n\nThe tread bit into rough, loose ground, and the Fog suede with orange accents looks sharp with jeans.\n\nThe heel rubbed on my first loaded outing, then stopped rubbing on a later loaded walk after break-in.\n\nFrom the field\n\nOriginal photos from my testing. Free to use with credit and a link to this review.\n\nHow I wore them\n\nWhat I liked underfoot\n\nThe Fog suede\n\nHeel fit: the part to get right\n\nHow other reviewers found the fit\n\nWarranty and care\n\nShould you buy the Mackall 45?\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nIs the GORUCK Mackall 45 comfortable?\n\nDoes the Mackall 45 need a break-in period?\n\nIs the Mackall 45 a backpack?\n\nHow high is the Mackall 45's heel drop?\n\nIs the Mackall 45 waterproof?\n\nDoes the GORUCK warranty cover the Mackall 45?\n\nThe GORUCK Mackall 45 is a shoe built for rucking that still looks normal with jeans. The 45 is for 1945 and Camp Mackall's wartime history, and you can see the old trainer shape in it. After a loaded outing in them, the part I'd lead with is underneath: the bottom of the shoe is great. Plent"},{"text":"y of support, and it feels like standing on a dense memory foam mattress.\n\nGORUCK sent me this pair in the Fog colorway, men's size 12, which is my usual shoe size. GORUCK didn't approve what I say here or ask for specific coverage. Links to GORUCK on this page may earn us a commission.\n\nHow I wore them\n\nThis review covers that first outing and a later loaded walk after break-in, so it's about fit, feel and grip; long-term durability and wet weather are still ahead of them.\n\nWhat I liked underfoot\n\nThe sole is the highlight. It has plenty of support for a low-cut shoe, and with the GR1 and a plate on my back it still felt like that dense memory foam mattress.\n\nGORUCK describes the midsole as a low-durometer EVA foam and pairs it with a dual-density insert, so the support comes from the combination rather than a hard sole. On my feet, under the added weight, it gave a little and then held. That's the \"dense\" half of my mattress comparison.\n\nOn rougher terrain, the texture on the bottom was enough to dig in and give me stability. I felt comfortable climbing rough ground because I knew I wasn't going to lose my footing. The lugs bite like a trail shoe's, and there's a good amount of g"},{"text":"rip.\n\nThat grip comes from an outsole built for more than one job. The lugs are deep enough to dig into dirt and loose ground, on a shoe GORUCK still designed to go from the trail to errands. Three rubber compounds generally let a maker put grippier rubber where traction matters and tougher rubber where the sole wears fastest.\n\nHere's what GORUCK lists for the Fog version:\n\nSource: GORUCK's Mackall 45 Fog product page, checked September 25, 2026.\n\nThe 13 mm drop puts your heel higher than your toes. GORUCK says it chose that offset to reduce strain on the calves and Achilles. A higher drop does ask the lower leg to stretch a little less on each step; whether that suits you depends on your stride and the shoes you're used to.\n\nThe Fog suede\n\nIt's a really good-looking shoe. The pigskin suede upper, the leather spearhead on the side and the reinforced double stitching at the toe and heel give it that older trainer character. I'm a big fan of the gray with the orange accents. That may be a millennial thing; it's roughly how I've accented my house.\n\nSuede is sanded leather, which is where the soft nap comes from, and it generally softens to your foot with wear. Fog is the lighter color"},{"text":"way, so a suede brush is worth keeping by the door after a dusty trail. If you'd rather not think about it, Black + Gum hides dirt.\n\nHeel fit: the part to get right\n\nOn my first loaded outing, the back of the heel rubbed, and you can see where on my pair: the collar lining there is roughed up, with some pilling. I finished with blisters and wore my old shoes on the next outing. After breaking the Mackalls in, I took them on another loaded walk and the rubbing stopped.\n\nThat was a break-in issue for my feet, not an ongoing problem with this pair. A different heel may feel comfortable from the start, or need a different fit altogether.\n\nGORUCK designs the Mackall to be ready out of the box, and SEALgrinderPT reported a secure heel from the start. Mine needed time; now the support and traction are the story.\n\nIf a new pair rubs your heel, try it on shorter walks before committing to a long loaded route. Check heel hold with your usual socks; our foot care guide covers lacing and hot spots. Break-in solved my rubbing, but it won't fix every fit problem.\n\nHow other reviewers found the fit\n\nOther reviewers have had a smoother start, which fits the different-heels point. These are their a"},{"text":"ccounts, on different colorways, not mine.\n\nSEALgrinderPT wore the Black + Gum version and reported a comfortable initial fit, a secure heel and a planted feel in the first hours.\n\nMindBodyDad wore the Americana for rucking and yard work. He liked the support under load and the traction, and wanted a little more room in the forefoot.\n\nPut together with my follow-up, the sole and traction remain the clear strengths. Heel fit varies from foot to foot; mine settled after break-in.\n\nFor how the Mackall compares with other shoes on pavement, trail and mixed routes, see our rucking shoes by terrain guide.\n\nWarranty and care\n\nGORUCK's SCARS guarantee works differently for footwear than for its bags. The footwear terms cover manufacturing defects and exclude normal wear, which is narrower than the lifetime guarantee on its packs.\n\nTo clean them, GORUCK says to take the insert out, rinse the shoes and let them air dry, with no washer or dryer. GORUCK doesn't list the Mackall 45 as waterproof.\n\nShould you buy the Mackall 45?\n\nBuy it if:\n\nYou want one low-cut shoe for loaded walks and everyday wear. It's built for rucking and still looks normal with jeans.\n\nYou like a dense, supportive sole. "},{"text":"That was the best part of the shoe for me under a 20 lb plate.\n\nYou ruck on rough or loose ground. The tread dug in like a trail shoe's and gave me solid footing on climbs.\n\nYou like a raised heel under a pack. GORUCK lists a 13 mm drop, which it says is meant to ease strain on the calves and Achilles.\n\nSkip it if:\n\nYou want a flatter shoe or a different style. Our rucking shoes by terrain guide covers other picks for pavement, trail and mixed routes.\n\nYour routes are often wet. A waterproof trail shoe is the better tool there, and the same terrain guide covers those.\n\nYou mostly walk without a pack. A cushioned daily trainer is the better tool, and Kit Authority ranks the best walking shoes for that kind of mileage.\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nIs the GORUCK Mackall 45 comfortable?\n\nYes, underfoot especially. The sole felt dense and supportive with a loaded GR1 on my back, like standing on a dense memory foam mattress. My heel rubbed at first but stopped on a later loaded walk after break-in.\n\nDoes the Mackall 45 need a break-in period?\n\nGORUCK designs it to be ready out of the box. Mine rubbed on the first loaded outing; after break-in, the rubbing stopped on a later loaded walk."},{"text":"-compound outsole\n\nRemovable dual-density insert\n\nThe heel rubbed on my first loaded outing, though it stopped after break-in on a later loaded walk\n\nYou're welcome to quote the findings or use the original photos with credit to Ruck Authority and a link to this page.\n\nRelated Articles\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: 25L Tested With a 20 lb Plate\n\nHands-on GORUCK Rucker 5.0 review: the 25L with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails, the lumbar pad, plate pockets, and how it beats the GR1 and GR2.\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 vs 4.0: Should You Upgrade?\n\nWhat changed from the GORUCK Rucker 4.0 to the 5.0, what the new lumbar pad and magnetic pockets felt like on my back, and who should upgrade.\n\nGORUCK Achilles 30 lb Weighted Vest: Hands-On Review\n\nMy hands-on review of the 30 lb GORUCK Achilles: quick dog walks, flexible sewn-in weight, fit adjustment, pellet noise and shoulder fatigue.\n\nGORUCK Spy Ruck 12 lb: Hands-On Review\n\nI like the black 12 lb Spy Ruck for comfortable weighted walks. Its sliding straps let me set the fit, but the load is permanent and essentials need a separate place."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-rucker-5-0-review","version_id":"66af62696466704fa6aa3667b98d7310f8b80deddad80459b13077cda96833c3","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: 25L Tested With a 20 lb Plate","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-rucker-5-0-review","published_at":"2026-10-09T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the September trail outing provides new, citable observations about pack stability, lumbar comfort, sweat, and subsequent shoulder soreness that transfer to Mammoth's load-carrying demands. Commercial incentives require noting the GORUCK-supplied pack, affiliate commissions, and high publishing volume; Uncertainty limits findings to one reviewer's first week in dry weather with an unweighed total load, leaving multi-day durability, rain performance, and hip-belt benefits unverified.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nRuck Authority Score\n\n8.2/10\n\nMy verdict\n\nFor pure rucking, this is the GORUCK bag I'd pick\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0 is built for one job, carrying a plate, and it does that job better than any other GORUCK bag I've carried. On a filmed September 2026 hike on hilly Wisconsin trail with a 20 lb plate, the 25L stayed locked to my back and the lumbar pad felt supportive. I carried it without the optional hip belt and my traps were sore afterward, so for heavier loads I'd set up the sternum strap and try the belt.\n\nWhat I liked\n\nStayed solid against my back over uneven, hilly ground with a 20 lb plate and extra gear\n\nThe large lumbar pad felt supportive without demanding attention during the walk\n\nThe 25L left room for camera gear alongside the plate; internal pockets were useful for keys\n\nWhat held it back\n\nMy traps felt sore after I got home; I carried it without the optional hip belt\n\nThe test\n\nKey findings\n\nI carried the 25L GORUCK Rucker 5.0 on uneven, hilly Wisconsin trail with a 20 lb plate plus camera gear and other sup"},{"text":"plies I didn't weigh.\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0 stayed stable against my back on rough ground, and I didn't notice the plate shifting inside.\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0's large lumbar pad felt comfortable and supportive, and I soon stopped noticing it while walking.\n\nI carried the GORUCK Rucker 5.0 without its optional hip belt, and my traps were sore once I got home, so for heavier loads I'd add the sternum strap and try the belt.\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0 has two dedicated plate pockets that hold the plate up high against your back instead of letting it sink to the bottom of the bag.\n\nOf the three GORUCK bags I've carried with a 20 lb plate, the Rucker 5.0 is the better pure ruck, because the GR1 and GR2 hold the plate in an open sleeve and have no lumbar pad.\n\nFrom the field\n\nOriginal photos from my testing. Free to use with credit and a link to this review.\n\nWhat I carried and where I used it\n\nFit, stability and shoulder comfort\n\nThe 25L felt right on my back\n\nThe lumbar pad was less intrusive than it looks\n\nMy traps were sore afterward\n\nExpect contact with your back, and some sweat\n\nPlate storage, pockets and everyday use\n\nFeatures shown in the video\n\nHow it compares with the GR1 and GR"},{"text":"2\n\nWho I would recommend it to\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nThe GORUCK Rucker 5.0 stayed solid against my back on a hilly, uneven trail with a 20 lb plate and extra gear. I was wobbling around on the ground more than the bag was moving on me. That secure feel is the strongest reason I would choose it for dedicated rucking.\n\nThe large lumbar pad felt supportive, and I soon stopped noticing it while walking. My traps were sore once I got home, which I put down partly to carrying without the optional hip belt; that's the next thing I'll try.\n\nGORUCK supplied this 25L pack for review. The GR1 and GR2 comparison further down comes from my own time with both bags. If you already own a Rucker 4.0, my Rucker 5.0 vs 4.0 upgrade guide covers what changed and whether it's worth replacing.\n\nThe full review film plays at the top of this page. The watch page has chapter jumps and the complete transcript.\n\nWhat I carried and where I used it\n\nThe 20 lb figure is the plate weight. I also carried camera equipment, water and other supplies, but did not weigh the complete load.\n\nThe side pouches visible in some trail footage are third-party accessories. They are not included with the Rucker. The table"},{"text":"ioning is probably part of the story too.\n\nIf you get the same thing, the setup is the first place to look. Snug the straps enough that the pack rides high, but not so tight that you're shrugging; your shoulders should sit down and back. The Rucker's removable sternum strap pulls the shoulder straps in toward your chest, which can take pressure off the outer edge of the shoulders and stop the straps sliding outward. Both the sternum strap and a hip belt matter more as the plate gets heavier. On a pack this size, a belt is likely more about stopping sway and taking some pressure off your shoulders than carrying the load the way a framed hiking pack's belt does. Our rucking form guide covers strap setup, and how heavy your ruck should be covers building up the weight gradually.\n\nExpect contact with your back, and some sweat\n\nThe close fit that keeps the Rucker stable also means it sits flat against your back, so like any bag worn this close, it's a warm place to be; my shirt was damp across the back when I took it off. A synthetic or wool shirt and a dry one for the drive home take care of it. In hot weather, plan water and pace too; our summer heat safety guide covers that.\n\nI didn'"},{"text":"on details demonstrated in the close-ups. Material grades and capacity limits are manufacturer specifications, not measurements from my hike.\n\nShell and contact fabric: 1000D CORDURA on the body; smoother 210D HT CORDURA against the back and beneath the straps.\n\nPlate pockets: the short pocket accepts GORUCK Standard 10, 20 and 30 lb plates. The long pocket accepts Long 30 and 45 lb plates and can also hold Standard plates. GORUCK rates the pair to 75 lb combined.\n\nStructure: a built-in frame sheet and a 30mm lumbar pad.\n\nHardware: YKK zippers with 550 cord pulls, a removable sternum strap, four padded carry handles and front/side MOLLE webbing.\n\nWater and hydration: bottom drain holes, an internal bladder attachment point and a hose exit beneath the top handle.\n\nSource: GORUCK's current Rucker specifications, checked September 23, 2026. My actual plate load was 20 lb; the maximum capacity is not a recommended starting weight.\n\nThis review covers my first week with the bag, in dry weather, so rain and long-term wear are still ahead of it. The stitching looked substantial in the close-ups, and the SCARS lifetime guarantee backs it.\n\nHow it compares with the GR1 and GR2\n\nI've also ca"},{"text":"rried the GR1 and the GR2 with a 20 lb plate. They're GORUCK's everyday and travel bags, and both hold the plate in an open sleeve with no lumbar pad, which is exactly where the Rucker pulls ahead. For pure plate rucking, it's the one built for the job; the other two win when the bag has more to do than carry weight.\n\nEach column is my own use of that bag with a 20 lb plate, not a side-by-side test. My GR1 review and GR2 review cover each bag in full.\n\nWho I would recommend it to\n\nThe Rucker 5.0 makes sense if you want a dedicated bag for carrying plates and doing ruck workouts. Its secure feel on rough ground, comfortable lumbar support and room for my extra equipment made a positive first impression.\n\nIf you want to compare it with packs from other brands first, our best rucking backpacks guide covers the field.\n\nBuy it if:\n\nMost of what you carry is a plate, water and keys, and you want the plate held high and still.\n\nYou ruck on rough or hilly ground, where a load that stays put matters most.\n\nYou want lumbar support built into the bag rather than a flat back panel.\n\nYou want room for a few extras, like camera gear, alongside the plate without moving up to a bigger bag.\n\nSkip i"},{"text":"pace for camera gear alongside my plate. I have not compared it with the 20L. Choose around torso fit, where the lumbar pad sits and the cargo you need to carry.\n\nNot for stability: mine stayed stable without one. My traps were sore after I got home, though, so for heavier loads I'd try the optional belt along with the sternum strap, which likely takes some pressure off the shoulders.\n\nGORUCK describes it as highly water resistant, and it has drain holes to let water out. It isn't a waterproof case, so on wet days I'd keep electronics in a dry bag.\n\nA 20 lb plate, plus camera gear and other supplies. I didn't weigh the complete load. GORUCK's 75 lb plate-pocket rating is its specification, not a load I carried.\n\nSpecs, photos and where to buy\n\nHands-on tested · Gifted by GORUCK\n\nRucker 5.0\n\nDedicated rucking pack with dual elevated plate pockets, magnetic internal storage and a 30mm lumbar pad. In our review the 25L stayed stable with a 20 lb plate plus gear on hilly trails, and the lumbar pad felt supportive. For heavier loads, add the optional hip belt.\n\nTwo dedicated plate pockets hold the plate high and still\n\nLarge contoured lumbar pad that felt supportive on the trail\n\n1000D "},{"text":"Cordura shell with a lifetime warranty\n\nMagnetic internal pockets and room for camera gear beside the plate\n\nMy traps were sore after a loaded hike without the optional hip belt\n\nYou're welcome to quote the findings or use the original photos with credit to Ruck Authority and a link to this page.\n\nRelated Articles\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 vs 4.0: Should You Upgrade?\n\nWhat changed from the GORUCK Rucker 4.0 to the 5.0, what the new lumbar pad and magnetic pockets felt like on my back, and who should upgrade.\n\nGORUCK Mackall 45 Review: Tested Under a 20 lb Plate\n\nI wore the GORUCK Mackall 45 in Fog with a loaded GR1: a dense, supportive sole, tread that bites on rough ground and a suede upper that works with jeans.\n\nGORUCK Achilles 30 lb Weighted Vest: Hands-On Review\n\nMy hands-on review of the 30 lb GORUCK Achilles: quick dog walks, flexible sewn-in weight, fit adjustment, pellet noise and shoulder fatigue.\n\nGORUCK Spy Ruck 12 lb: Hands-On Review\n\nI like the black 12 lb Spy Ruck for comfortable weighted walks. Its sliding straps let me set the fit, but the load is permanent and essentials need a separate place."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-rucker-5-0-vs-4-0-review","version_id":"54479d2596b49a0ecdf91bff88af124cc281bbeee27e613103538767d586a5d2","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 vs 4.0: Should You Upgrade?","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/gear/goruck-rucker-5-0-vs-4-0-review","published_at":"2026-10-09T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the described 25L pack trial with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails provides specific observations about stability and lumbar comfort relevant to carried loads, supporting pieces 01, 02, and 03. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, flag the supplied review unit, affiliate commissions, and high publishing volume; attribute comfort findings to this reviewer and treat the untested 4.0 comparison and broader durability claims as unverified.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nWhat changed from the 4.0 to the 5.0\n\nWhat the changes felt like on my back\n\nThe lumbar pad is the upgrade you'd feel\n\nThe magnetic pockets are a quality-of-life change\n\nWhat didn't change\n\nShould you upgrade from a Rucker 4.0?\n\nBuying your first Rucker\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nThe Rucker 5.0's two documented changes are a larger, contoured lumbar pad of 30mm high-density foam and magnetic closures on the internal pockets, which replace the 4.0's hook-and-loop.\n\nThe shell, frame sheet, carry handles and SCARS lifetime guarantee carry over; both come in 20L and 25L.\n\nCarrying the 25L Rucker 5.0 with a 20 lb plate, the lumbar pad felt supportive and the pack stayed stable on rough, hilly ground.\n\nKeep a 4.0 that fits you well. Upgrade if its lumbar pad bothers you or its hook-and-loop pockets have worn out. First-time buyers should get the 5.0, or a discounted 4.0 if saving money matters more.\n\nThe Rucker 5.0 refines the 4.0 where you feel it most. GORUCK kept the dedicated plate pockets, the 1000D CORDURA shell, the fr"},{"text":"ame sheet and the lifetime guarantee, and changed the two things you touch most: the lumbar pad against your lower back and the closures on the internal pockets.\n\nI've carried the 25L Rucker 5.0 with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails, and my full Rucker 5.0 review covers that in detail. I haven't carried a 4.0, so the older bag's side of this comparison comes from GORUCK's published information. GORUCK supplied my 5.0 for review.\n\nWhat changed from the 4.0 to the 5.0\n\nGORUCK released the 5.0 on May 8, 2026. For the full launch breakdown, including why the magnetic closures matter at pre-dawn training and events, see our Rucker 5.0 launch analysis.\n\nWhat the changes felt like on my back\n\nThe lumbar pad is the upgrade you'd feel\n\nOn the table, the 5.0's lumbar pad looks enormous. On my back, it felt supportive and soon faded into the background, and I had no lower-back discomfort on the filmed hike.\n\nThe reason a bigger, contoured pad matters is the curve of your lower back. A pad that fills more of that hollow gives the pack a firmer second point of contact below your shoulder blades, so the bottom of the pack has less room to rock with each step. That second contact probably "},{"text":"did as much for the 5.0's stability on rough ground as the plate pocket did.\n\nSo the upgrade question mostly comes down to your 4.0's lumbar pad. If you finish a ruck with a sore or rubbed lower back, or you feel the bottom of the pack moving, that's the problem the 5.0 was built to fix. If you never think about your lower back in a 4.0, you already have most of what the 5.0 offers.\n\nThe magnetic pockets are a quality-of-life change\n\nI kept my keys in a magnetic internal pocket, and it was simply easy: one hand, no rip of hook-and-loop. Magnets are quiet and don't clog with lint, sand or dog hair the way hook-and-loop does over years of use. They're a genuine quality-of-life upgrade, and if your 4.0's pockets have worn out and stopped holding, they count for even more.\n\nWhat didn't change\n\nThe plate still rides high and tight against your back, which is why both generations carry better than an everyday bag with a plate sleeve. With a 20 lb plate, the 5.0 stayed stable where I had to work to keep my own balance. Strap setup, the sternum strap and the optional hip belt work the same way in both generations, and my full review goes into what I'd change for heavier loads.\n\nShould you "},{"text":"upgrade from a Rucker 4.0?\n\nUpgrade to the 5.0 if:\n\nYour lower back gets sore, rubbed or hot in the 4.0, or you feel the bottom of the pack rocking.\n\nThe hook-and-loop on your 4.0's internal pockets has worn out and no longer holds.\n\nYou're moving up to heavier plates, where lumbar contact and stability matter more.\n\nYou've been meaning to change sizes anyway.\n\nKeep your 4.0 if:\n\nIt fits well and you finish rucks without lower-back complaints.\n\nMost of your frustration is shoulder soreness. The lumbar pad won't fix that; a snug sternum strap, a hip belt or building up the weight more gradually will do more. Our rucking form guide covers strap setup.\n\nYou'd rather put the money toward a heavier plate or better shoes.\n\nGORUCK hasn't announced a way to retrofit the new lumbar pad or magnetic closures to a 4.0, so upgrading means replacing the bag.\n\nBuying your first Rucker\n\nIf you don't own either, I'd buy the 5.0. It's the version I've carried, and the lumbar pad is the change you'd notice on every ruck. A discounted 4.0 is still a proper dedicated ruck with the same plate-first design, so if you find one on sale and you'd rather keep the difference, it's a sensible buy. If you're on"},{"text":"ly starting out with light loads, GORUCK's cheaper Basic Rucker is worth a look first, and our best rucking backpacks guide compares the field beyond GORUCK.\n\nDeciding between a Rucker and GORUCK's everyday bags instead? I've carried all three with the same 20 lb plate, and my GR1 review and GR2 review compare them directly.\n\nFrequently asked questions\n\nThe Rucker 5.0 adds a larger, contoured lumbar pad of 30mm high-density foam in place of the 4.0's 20mm pad, and magnetic closures on the internal pockets in place of hook-and-loop. The shell, frame sheet, elevated plate storage, carry handles and lifetime guarantee carry over.\n\nYes, mainly because of the lumbar pad. In my own use of the 5.0, the pad felt supportive and the pack stayed stable on rough ground. If a 4.0 already fits you comfortably, the difference is smaller.\n\nYes, especially at a discount. It's still a dedicated rucking pack that holds the plate high, with the same construction and guarantee. You give up the newer lumbar pad and magnetic pockets.\n\nGORUCK released the Rucker 5.0 on May 8, 2026.\n\nGORUCK hasn't announced a retrofit. The lumbar pad and magnetic closures are built into the 5.0, so getting them means buyin"},{"text":"g the new bag.\n\nQuick Comparison\n\nRelated Articles\n\nGORUCK GR1 vs GR2: Which Ruck Should Ruckers Buy in 2026?\n\nGORUCK GR1 vs GR2 for ruckers. Which compartment design, capacity, and use case wins, plus a 5-question buyer's framework and FAQ.\n\nGORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: 25L Tested With a 20 lb Plate\n\nHands-on GORUCK Rucker 5.0 review: the 25L with a 20 lb plate on hilly Wisconsin trails, the lumbar pad, plate pockets, and how it beats the GR1 and GR2.\n\nGORUCK Achilles 30 lb Weighted Vest: Hands-On Review\n\nMy hands-on review of the 30 lb GORUCK Achilles: quick dog walks, flexible sewn-in weight, fit adjustment, pellet noise and shoulder fatigue.\n\nGORUCK Spy Ruck 12 lb: Hands-On Review\n\nI like the black 12 lb Spy Ruck for comfortable weighted walks. Its sliding straps let me set the fit, but the load is permanent and essentials need a separate place."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://ruckauthority.com/guides/goruck-m26-preorder-analysis-new-pack-rucking-market","version_id":"becf8ba49acee9240bc51eae0bfce3d6c78ccebbcd9ca4d9d0b2f08308a4f4f1","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK M26: Preorder Status, Shipping Target & Alternatives","url":"https://ruckauthority.com/guides/goruck-m26-preorder-analysis-new-pack-rucking-market","published_at":"2026-10-09T00:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Rucking gear reviews (packs, vests, plates, socks, base layers). Affiliate commissions; GORUCK supplied review units. Very high recent volume suggests bulk/SEO publishing; check enclosure MIME types (image enclosures)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, this newly announced pack offers citable manufacturer-attributed design details and availability information, while explicitly leaving carried weight, load retention, and loaded comfort unverified. Freshness and novelty supports Piece 01, and its delivery uncertainty and unresolved load-carrying suitability support Piece 03; Commercial incentives requires noting affiliate commissions, GORUCK-supplied review units, and high publishing volume, without treating this source-based guide as independent testing.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"text":"Free newsletter\n\nRucking notes by email\n\nNew gear reviews and practical training notes. Join now to get the newsletter from its first issue. Unsubscribe anytime.\n\nChecked October 9, 2026: GORUCK's product page lists the M26 preorder as closed. All six variants are unavailable. Shipping targets Q4 2026, with Q1 2027 possible; neither is a guaranteed delivery date. Limited safety stock is planned, but a restock is not assured.\n\nThis is a source-based guide to the announced pack, not a hands-on review. For readers choosing a rucking pack now, availability and secure weight carry matter more than preorder excitement.\n\nWhat GORUCK has announced\n\nGORUCK says it built the M26 on the M23, using feedback from the GORUCK subreddit.\n\nThe announced design includes a rain-flapped lid pocket, two side pockets whose volume sits inside the pack, extended main-compartment zipper access, and YKK zippers without AquaGuard. Both sizes have laptop compartments described as fitting a 16-inch MacBook.\n\nSizes and materials\n\nThese are the announced choices in the closed preorder. ROBIC nylon appears in the interior; it is not the advertised exterior fabric.\n\nWhat remains unverified\n\nThe listing does not es"},{"text":"tablish a reliable finished-pack weight or dedicated plate compatibility. Do not infer secure plate retention from laptop fit. Before choosing any pack for weighted training, check the manufacturer's instructions for the specific plate and retention system you intend to use.\n\nPublished capacity alone also cannot settle fit. Think about your torso, everyday load, access needs, and whether you will carry a laptop alongside training weight. Those are separate decisions from choosing a color or exterior fabric.\n\nTreat the images and shipping window carefully\n\nGORUCK labels concept imagery as non-final. The illustrations on this article are editorial images, not evidence of finished-product construction or our use of the pack.\n\nFor an existing order, follow the seller's order updates and the terms supplied at purchase. A target quarter is useful for planning, but it is not a promise that the pack will arrive before a trip or event. Ask GORUCK directly about cancellation options for your order.\n\nAlternatives to compare while the preorder is closed\n\nChoose an alternative by the job you need it to do. A training pack and an everyday organizer need not solve the same problem. Our existing c"},{"text":"omparison of Mystery Ranch and other packs can help frame that decision; 5.11 is another brand to compare.\n\nCompare each pack against the features you need, and check current stock, size and delivery estimates at the retailer. Our Rucker 5.0 comparison, RUSH 24 2.0 listing, and Mystery Ranch's 2-Day Assault comparison provide starting points.\n\nDecide around your next use\n\nIf you need a pack for a scheduled trip or a rucking community outing, choose around confirmed availability and your actual load. If your current pack works, a closed preorder is not a reason to replace it.\n\nUse your normal packing list and fit requirements to make that comparison. We cannot establish which size fits you from capacity labels alone, and this article does not claim hands-on fit testing.\n\nThat requires evidence of plate fit, retention and loaded comfort. A laptop compartment by itself does not answer those questions. Check those details before treating any everyday pack as a training replacement.\n\nOnly if you need one. Compare available packs against the job your current setup cannot handle; avoid buying an interim pack solely because a preorder has a long wait.\n\nRelated Articles\n\nHow to Wash a Rucks"},{"text":"ack: The Right Method for Ruck Packs\n\nHow to hand wash, dry and store a ruck pack, based on care guidance from GORUCK, Osprey, Deuter and Eagle Creek, plus when a pack is worth repairing.\n\nMemorial Day Rucks: How to Carry With Purpose\n\nHow to plan a respectful Memorial Day ruck, choose a manageable route and load, and keep remembrance more important than workout performance.\n\nThe 4 Best Rucking Books to Read in 2026\n\nFour books for ruckers: Michael Easter's Walk with Weight, Kayla Girgen's Ruck Fit, The Comfort Crisis and the mindset memoir Can't Hurt Me.\n\nGORUCK Labor Day Sale 2026: Current Deals After the Holiday\n\nLabor Day has passed. Check current GORUCK deals, compare Rucker and plate options, and avoid expired offers or discounts on gear that does not fit."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-running-pants","version_id":"28be243117027254a6e4779fa7e5a25dbdf399fec0a6fbae1b105b46113f8b20","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Running Pants of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-running-pants","published_at":"2026-10-15T14:35:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this newly reported comparison supplies transferable clothing evidence from over a year of mountain running in rain, snow, and cold, including a specified spray test, wet-weather observations, and fit limitations. Commercial incentives requires discounting praise from this affiliate-funded reviewer; under Uncertainty, these remain one publisher’s findings, with Mammoth loaded-ruck experience absent. It supports Piece 01’s newly reviewed options, Piece 02’s assessment of weather-resistance claims, and Piece 03’s clothing tradeoffs without establishing any particular pants as required.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Running Pants of 2026\n\nBest joggers and wind pants for runners\n\nOctober 15th, 2026Home → Gear Reviews → Apparel\n\nRunning pants are useful when shorts aren’t enough but tights aren’t your first choice. They add protection from wind, rain, cold, and trailside brush while still giving you room to move, layer, and adjust as conditions change.\n\nOur main tester is an FKT-setting runner and ultramarathoner, and he tested running pants for more than a year across the Northeast on roads, rail trails, tracks, and technical mountains. He wore them in pouring rain, snow, slush, cold wind, and warm, humid weather.\n\nWe tested both single- and double-layer running pants and evaluated each pair for comfort, breathability, durability, weather resistance, fit, and how well it performed across different kinds of runs and conditions. We also paid attention to practical details like pockets, zippers, elastic, fabric feel, mobility, and whether the pants stayed comfortable as my effort and the temperature changed.\n\nTop Pick: The men's and women's REI Swiftland Hybrid Running Pants are our Best Overall running pants. Their wind- and water-resistant front panels, softer stretch fabric, useful storage"},{"text":", and comfortable fit make them versatile enough for a wide range of weather and running conditions. They held up well throughout testing and were the pair I reached for most often when conditions were variable. And they're relatively affordable and stylish, backed with a top-notch guarantee.\n\nRelated: Best Men’s Running Tights and Best Women’s Running Tights\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\nMen’s\n\nWomen’s\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall running pants: REI Swiftland Hybrid Running Pants\n\nPrice: $90Comfort subjective rating: 4/5Breathability subjective rating: 4/5Weather resistance subjective rating: 4/5Available sizes: XS-3XLMaterials: 90% polyester/10% spandex; waistband, sides, inner thighs and ankles: 71% nylon/29% spandexWhat we liked: Wide size range, soft and flexible nylon fabric, excellent weatherproofing, non-PFC DWR finish, uses bluesig"},{"text":"ey are large enough that most phones will bounce in the pockets. There is also one back zippered pocket; all pockets are nylon and inside the pants. The Mier joggers are made using 95% recycled and 5% new fabric. They’re offered up to a 3XL, with 46” waist and 51.5” hips.\n\nBreathability\n\nThe Mier joggers have some breathability, though they breathe less than most other pants we tested. They showed light sweat staining oat the extremes of heat and running intensity, but for shorter runs, these pants performed well. They do absorb rain and sweat, and would do best with fair weather conditions.\n\nOur Verdict\n\nWhile they lack some features of more expensive pants, they’re a great option for runners seeking utilitarian running pants for road running around town or trail running in a nearby park, and runners can feel good knowing the pants are made almost exclusively from recycled fabric.\n\nBest lightweight running pants: Zpacks Trail Cool Joggers\n\nPrice: $90Comfort subjective rating: 4/5Breathability subjective rating: 4/5Weather resistance subjective rating: 3/5Available sizes: S-XLMaterials: Toray PrimeFlexWhat we liked: Smooth and flexible fabric, quick-drying, durable What we didn’t l"},{"text":" also offering a generous cut throughout with no limitations on butt, thigh, or calf size. Their range of sizing includes a 43.5” hip for their small size up to a 48” hip for their XL size. However, the measured inseam of our tester’s medium pants was 28” and some reviewers online noted the short inseam.\n\nOur Verdict\n\nThese pants are ideal for road and trail runners looking for a lightweight, quick-drying pair of pants with generous pockets and 4-way stretch fabric. They’re a great option for 3-season running, and will keep you covered on chilly, misty, or windy days. Zpacks Ultralight Trail Cool Joggers are simply designed, with three pockets, ankle elastic, and a stretchy waistband, so they’re also a great match for runners who desire simplicity.\n\nBest running pants for cold weather: REI Co-op Active Pursuits Midweight Joggers\n\nPrice: $89Comfort subjective rating: 5/5Breathability subjective rating: 3/5Weather resistance subjective rating: 2/5Available sizes: XXS - 3XLMaterials: 93% polyester, 7% spandexWhat we liked: Non-gendered, very soft fabric, wide size range, bluesign fabric What we didn’t like: The relaxed fit on the lower legs made it possible to catch one leg on the oth"},{"text":"rice: $130Comfort subjective rating: 4/5Breathability subjective rating: 5/5Weather resistance subjective rating: 3/5Available sizes: XS - XXLMaterials: Fortius Air 50, 84% nylon / 16% elastane, with FC0 DWRWhat we liked: Adjustable ankle closures, wide size range, quick-drying, non-PFC DWR finish, bluesign-approved fabrics What we didn’t like: Cut is tighter on the thighs, expensive\n\nOur Best Breathable running pants winner is the Arc’teryx Incendo Pants. They’re a lightweight running pant that has a soft brushed nylon texture. There are two open front pockets that very loosely fit a phone, and one smaller fold-over mesh outer pocket on the side that can fit keys, snacks, or trash. These pants are sustainably made, with a non-PFC DWR finish and using only bluesign-approved fabrics.\n\nThe Arc’teryx Incendo pants are ready for any kind of warm weather adventure you might want–be it running up a mountain or jogging on a rail trail. Photo by Justin Martino-Harms.\n\nFabric and Fit\n\nThe Incendos are tapered but flexible, and offer up to a XXL size (44.5” waist, 50” hips). They have good wind resistance, though their lightweight nature means they get wet from rain fairly quickly. However, "},{"text":"d try on pants in person when possible or make sure the online ordering service you use offers an easy return policy.\n\nWhen it’s cold and windy, running pants can offer more warmth than leggings. Photo by Aubri Drake.\n\nFit and flexibility\n\nRunning pants tend to be offered in a slim fit jogger style, tapering to the ankle. Some pants are tighter in the thighs or calves due to this particular style; we’ve noted this issue when present in our reviews above.\n\nAdditionally, some waistbands we tested lacked elasticity, making the fit more challenging for some testers. Current designs prioritize an unrestricted range of motion, so while the fit may be more snug, the fabric they’re using has good stretch and does not limit flexibility.\n\nWeather protection\n\nSome running pants offer a durable water repellent (DWR) finish for increased resistance to precipitation. While this can be very helpful for runs in the rain or snow, it can affect the texture of the fabric, or contain PFAS or PFCs, many of which are considered “forever chemicals.” None of the pants we tested with a DWR finish contain PFAS or PFCs. DWR can break down over time, but can also be refreshed with a treatment like Nikwax’s Te"},{"text":"d interiors or fleece-lined fabrics that trap warmth while staying breathable. Pairing them with a moisture-wicking base layer and weather-resistant shell helps keep you warm and dry during cold-weather runs.\n\nWhat’s the best material for running pants?\n\nSynthetic fabrics like polyester and nylon blends dominate because they dry quickly, wick sweat, and resist abrasion. Elastane (spandex) adds stretch and mobility. Some brands now use recycled polyester or sustainable performance fabrics that reduce environmental impact while maintaining durability.\n\nHow do I choose the right running pants for trail running?\n\nFor trail running, prioritize durability, freedom of movement, and protection. Thicker fabrics with some water or wind resistance are ideal, and ankle cuffs or tapered fits help prevent debris from getting inside. Reflective details and a secure waistband with zippered pockets are also helpful when running uneven terrain or remote trails.\n\nShould I wear running pants or shorts in the rain?\n\nIt depends on temperature and personal preference. Running pants made from water-resistant or quick-dry fabric can keep you warmer in cold, wet weather. In milder rain, some runners prefer "},{"text":"others prioritize a relaxed fit for comfort. Choose based on your preference—compression may help on long runs, while looser fits are better for easy mileage or recovery days.\n\nHow we researched and tested\n\nA spray bottle filled with water and strips of paper towels (top left), Paper towel strips after testing (top right), The testing space, measuring out the 30” of distance for the spray bottle (bottom left), All pants with a DWR finish, after testing (bottom right). Photo by Aubri Drake.\n\nWe tested running pants over more than a year, encompassing two falls, two winters and two springs in the Northeast.\n\nWe tested running pants while running on roads, track, and trail, in temperatures ranging from 20–70°F and down to 10°F with windchill.\n\nTesting was completed in the Northeast and conditions included precipitation like rain, snow, slush, and fog. Running intensity during testing varied from steady, easy, and recovery paces all the way up to VO2 max, threshold, and tempo paces.\n\nFor pants with DWR, we methodically tested their weather resistance in the sink. We placed dry paper towel strips inside the pants leg, and then sprayed the pants leg ten times with water from 30 inches aw"},{"text":"sts for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability.\n\nWe tested men’s down vests on hikes, backpacking trips, alpine runs, bike rides, and cold camps for warmth, comfort, fit, packability, and durability."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-down-vests","version_id":"252d9dff495ff3e6f41ac4fea92d341a24a0495dada9891b4b97f582509a4dec","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Down Vests for Women of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-down-vests","published_at":"2026-10-13T16:20:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, the described backpacking tests offer citable observations transferable to Mammoth: restricted pocket access under a hip belt, Stretchdown wetting out faster than competing vests, and abrasion performance while scrambling. Commercial incentives requires discounting this affiliate-funded review's praise; Uncertainty requires attributing observations to this single publisher and distinguishing listed specifications and untested models from field evidence. Freshness and novelty supports the newly reported multi-month comparisons, best used for Piece 02's performance assessment and Piece 03's evaluation of an optional insulating layer under Necessary versus optional; Mammoth practitioner confirmation is missing.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Down Vests for Women of 2026\n\nThe top Down and Synthetic Insulated Vests for warmth, weather resistance, comfort, fit, and style\n\nOctober 13th, 2026, updated with a new vestHome > Gear Reviews > Apparel\n\nA good insulated vest adds warmth around your core without the bulk of a full puffy jacket. It’s an easy layer for cool hikes, chilly mornings at camp, travel, and everyday wear, and it can fit over a base layer or under a shell when temperatures drop.\n\nWe tested women’s down and synthetic vests over several months of hiking, camping, backpacking, travel, and everyday use. Testing included time in Colorado’s high country, along with trips in Idaho and Wyoming, in conditions ranging from cool, dry weather to drizzly evenings and colder nights at camp.\n\nWe evaluated warmth, fit, comfort, durability, weather resistance, packability, pockets, and how well each vest worked within different layering systems. We also compared the vests side by side for warmth and water resistance.\n\nTop Pick: The Patagonia Down Sweater Vest is our Best Overall women’s down vest. It stood out for its warmth, comfort, packability, and versatility across both outdoor and everyday use. It was also one of "},{"text":"others. The outer shell is constructed with a bluesign-approved recycled nylon ripstop that’s treated with a DWR coating. With dual zippered hand pockets and two spacious internal drop-in pockets, there’s ample space to carry essentials.\n\nWarmth\n\nWhile this vest is not the warmest in our test, it’s still quite cozy and is on the lighter side and small enough to pack into the backcountry as long as going ultralight is not a concern.\n\nFlaws but not dealbreakers\n\nWhile the REI Co-op 650 vest offers excellent value, there are a few things it lacks compared to others in our test. The fill power is lower than all other down vests in our test, and notably lower than the 800-fill of the Patagonia Down Sweater. It’s also not quite as warm or weather resistant as others, but overall the performance for the price is very good.\n\nOur verdict\n\nWith any budget item there are features you’ll have to sacrifice and we still think this is a great piece of gear. However, if you’re looking for a warmer vest with a higher loft and better down insulation, consider others in our review.\n\nWarmest Down Vest: Rab Microlight Vest\n\nWeight: 8 oz.Available sizes: XXS-XXLFit: standardShell fabric: 30D Pertex Quan"},{"text":"side. It’s still light enough for most outdoor adventures, but if weight savings is a priority you’ll want to consider other options, though you will sacrifice some of the weather-resistant qualities that this vest offers.\n\nThe Rab Microlight Vest is the overall winner in our Best Men's Down Vests guide where we especially liked the balance of function and sustainability.\n\nBest Packable Vest: Mountain Hardwear Stretchdown Light Vest\n\nWeight: 8.3 oz.Available sizes: XS-XLFit: standardShell fabric: 20D Durable Stretch Doubleweave, 86% Nylon, 14% ElastaneInsulation type: 700-fill RDS-certified downPockets: What we liked: Stretchy, comfortable, great for freedom of movementWhat could be better: price, shell fabric could be more water-resistant and breathable\n\nEven with the lack of sleeves, not all vests offer a wide range of motion due to the stiff shell materials used for down insulation. Mountain Hardwear changes that with their Stretchdown shell material. For active pursuits, whether hiking, backpacking, or rock climbing, the Stretchdown vest offers the most comfort and range of motion of any vest in our test. And it packs into its right-hand pocket, making it easy to stash in a bac"},{"text":"kpack. We used this on long and tough late summer backpacking trips in the high alpine of northern Colorado and only took it off in the heat of the day. If you want a vest that can stand up and stay comfortable for all sorts of outdoor adventures, this is it.\n\nMaterials\n\nThe Stretchdown Light vest is made with a stretchy, 20-denier shell material that’s both durable and comfortable. On boulder field scrambles, we had no issues with abrasion when wearing this vest. The Stretchdown has 700-fill RDS-certified down in the staggered baffles.\n\nIt’s not as lofty as other vests in our test, but that sleeker design is part of what makes it great for aerobic activities like hiking and climbing. We were less likely to overheat in this vest than any others in our test.\n\nWeatherproofness\n\nOn our testing hikes and backpacking trips, we found that the vest blocks wind well in 25-mph gusts above treeline, but the water resistance was not as good as others in our test; it wetted out faster than the others.\n\nPockets\n\nFor pockets, it’s got two zippered hand pockets.\n\nFunctional and stretchy\n\nIf you want the functionality of the stretchy shell material you’ll have to fork over the extra cash, but that"},{"text":"pe that in future seasons Mountain Hardwear will bring the women’s model back. The last thing we need is fewer technical options for women’s-specific gear!\n\nWho needs an insulated vest?\n\nPuffy vests are useful pieces of gear for taking the chill off without needing a full jacket. They can also be layered over other insulating pieces, like fleece jackets, and under more protective pieces like rain jackets. A puffer vest is more compressible and packable than a fleece vest, making it better suited for travel and outdoor adventure.\n\nIf you’re heading out into bitterly cold conditions, a vest probably isn’t the best option, and you’ll want to reach for your parka. But for times when it’s chilly or cold, but not quite frigid, a vest can be the perfect companion to keep your core warm. Ultimately, whether a vest is right for you will come down to personal preference.\n\nWhen to wear an insulated vest\n\nInsulated vests shine in that tricky in-between weather when it’s too cold for just a shirt or fleece but too warm for a full jacket. They’re perfect for keeping your core warm while allowing your arms to move freely—ideal for hiking, layering, or just getting things done around town on cool "},{"text":"pellents that avoid the use of PFCs that are harmful to the environment. Many vests use shell material made of recycled material.\n\nWe also look for companies that support fair working conditions for the people making their clothing and gear, including vests that are Fair Trade Certified sewn.\n\nAdditionally, we value companies with good warranty and repair options. Outdoor gear and clothing is expensive to make, especially when done right. So typically, you’ll need to spend a little more to get a vest (or other gear) that’s made with sustainability in mind, but we think that’s worth it, given that you usually get a higher quality garment that’s better for you, the earth, and the people who make it.\n\nDurability\n\nDurability is a crucial factor to consider with any outdoor gear or apparel. We will rarely recommend a piece of gear or apparel that lacks durability (except in the instance of unique pieces of ultralight gear). Durability not only makes gear more sustainable, it also helps you spend less money and acquire less stuff in the long run.\n\nWe believe all of the puffer vests in this review offer great durability, and some stand out more than others. Durability and weight can somet"},{"text":"affordable and vegan-friendly, making it a great choice for wet or unpredictable weather.\n\nYou can read more in Which is better: a down or synthetic jacket?\n\nAre insulated vests warm enough for extreme cold?\n\nNot on their own. Insulated vests keep your core warm but leave your arms exposed, so they work best as a layering piece in very cold weather. For extreme cold, pair a vest under a parka or insulated jacket for maximum warmth.\n\nAre insulated vests easy to pack?\n\nYes—especially down vests. Most compress into a small stuff sack or their own pocket, making them easy to stash in a backpack or travel bag. Synthetic vests pack slightly larger but still offer excellent warmth for their weight.\n\nWhat temperatures are best for wearing a puffer vest?\n\nPuffer vests are versatile across seasons. They’re ideal for cool to cold conditions, such as early fall hikes, chilly camp mornings, or layered under a shell on subfreezing days. They work well anywhere you need warmth without the bulk of full sleeves.\n\nWhy do bankers wear vests?\n\nThe primary reason why bankers wear puffer vests, according to Marketplace, is because they offer a more casual and comfortable look that still maintains a cert"},{"text":"e. Along with her editorial expertise, she spends almost every day getting outside, whether hiking, backpacking, mountain biking, or backcountry skiing. When working as a backcountry guide in Alaska, she was particularly hard on gear and is now always highly critical of outdoor gear and apparel, including what makes a good vest. Both because of her outdoor experience and outdoor industry journalism experience, she’s able to dive deep into materials, quality, and performance when testing.\n\nStasia is a lifelong backpacker, skier, hiker, mountain biker, and trail runner passionate about lowering barriers to entry for outdoor sports. When she’s not writing or cooking, you’ll find her in the mountains, probably above treeline, and probably testing gear.\n\nMore of her work and adventures can be found on her website.\n\nTo see all Stasia’s Treeline articles, see her author profile.\n\nFind the best running pants for every season and terrain. Tested for comfort, breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs in all kinds of weather.\n\nFind the best women's down vests for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, an"},{"text":"d packability.\n\nWe tested women’s rain boots for waterproofing, traction, comfort, fit, and durability through downpours, muddy trails, farm work, and everyday walking."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-womens-rain-boots","version_id":"8f101ae52afcbdf84cb5b62b856bf5eb774f8104d77dc3e4c7d87fb8f059ff72","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Rain Boots for Women of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-womens-rain-boots","published_at":"2026-10-13T14:55:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the newly added Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX assessment offers four months of testing, about 35 miles of use, 15-minute creek-submersion comparisons, and a week in wet, cold Iceland, with narrow-fit and break-in drawbacks relevant to Mammoth footwear decisions. Commercial incentives requires discounting the affiliate-funded publisher's praise as independent confirmation; under Uncertainty, these remain one publisher's findings, without Mammoth-specific loaded-ruck testing, and support Pieces 01, 02, and 03 rather than establishing a definitive boot recommendation.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Rain Boots for Women of 2026\n\nWaterproof boots and rubber rain boots for mud, puddles, warmth, and staying dry\n\nOctober 13th, 2026, updated with a new winner for best waterproof hiking bootHome → Gear Reviews → Footwear\n\nRain boots need to do more than keep your feet dry. Depending on how you use them, they also need enough traction for slick surfaces, a comfortable fit for long days on your feet, and the durability to handle mud, rain, and repeated wear.\n\nWe tested women’s rain boots in Alaska, New England, Iowa, the Pacific Northwest, New York City, and California. Testing included rainy commutes, farm work, long walks, rafting trips, muddy trails, and flood cleanup, giving us a chance to use these boots in everything from everyday rain to demanding wet-weather conditions.\n\nWe evaluated waterproofness, traction, fit and comfort, durability, and how easy each boot was to get on and off. We also considered how well different styles worked for specific uses, from ankle boots for everyday wear to taller boots for deeper water and muddy conditions.\n\nTop Pick: The XTRATUF Legacy Salmon Sisters Boot is our Best Overall women’s rain boot. Our testers have worn XTRATUF Legacy boots f"},{"text":"or long days on Alaska’s rivers and in wet, cold conditions, and they continue to stand out for their waterproofing, traction, comfort, and durability.\n\nLooking for men’s rain boots? See our Best Men’s Rain Boots guide.\n\nPairing them with a shell? See our Best Lightweight Rain Jackets guide.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall women’s rain boots: XTRATUF Legacy\n\nPrice: $175Sizes available: 6 - 11Shaft height: 15”Waterproof: YesMaterial: Rubber/neopreneFootwear closure: Pull onBest for: Wading in rivers or puddles, chores, spring hiking, running errands, working on boats, slogging through marshes What we liked: waterproofness, durability, ease of walking, comfort, versatility, temperature and height adjustment with roll-top, arch support, excellent traction on slippery surfacesWhat we didn’t like: not as warm as other b"},{"text":" with sister-owned, Alaska-based artists and fisherwomen called the Salmon Sisters and with women-owned fishing apparel company FisheWear for beautiful designs both in and outside the boot. These fisherwomen gave an otherwise drab (yet classic) rubber boot a colorful twist, designed to be shown off when rolled down.\n\nBest affordable women’s rain boots: Asgard Ankle Boots Waterproof Chelsea\n\nPrice: $39Sizes available: 5 - 12Shaft height: 5”Waterproof: YesMaterial: RubberFootwear closure: Pull on with tabsBest for: Running errands, working in the garden, heading to work in the rain, collecting eggs from the chicken coop, walks around town What we liked: budget-friendly price, waterproofness, pliable and comfortable feelWhat we didn’t like: flaps can catch and bend when pulling shoes on, narrow footbed\n\nFor those looking at a stylish and functional Chelsea rain boot on a budget, this rain boot might be your perfect match. The Asgard Women’s Ankle/Chelsea rain boots are the price of a large pizza, and will last you significantly longer than the pizza will. Although there are a few design snags that didn’t make this our overall favorite boot, we couldn’t ignore how well the Asgard boot "},{"text":"functions for less than $40.\n\nThe Asgard ankle boots were waterproof when walking in puddles of water.\n\nThe Asgard ankle boots were waterproof when walking in puddles of water.\n\nFit and Comfort\n\nThe over-the-ankle design of these boots helps to keep muck and rain out of your footbed, but make sure you wear a mid-calf sock along with it! Our tester did have a bit of chafing with ankle socks and jeans. The openings are narrow but extremely elastic, which helps for ease of pulling on and taking off these boots. One tester with regular-to-wide feet did note that the narrow footbed did become uncomfortable on one afternoon of walking. Their toes had room to wiggle, but the middle of their feet started to rub and hurt after multiple hours out walking.\n\nDesign and Function\n\nAsgard boots utilize removable EVA foam insoles for comfortable strides, and anti-slip rubber soles to keep you stable and upright in slick weather. These boots are made from waterproof rubber that held up well in our testing — the rubber held up to bending, stretching, and scraping along sidewalks and short hikes without compromising waterproofing.\n\nElastic-goring by the ankles added ease of pulling on and taking off "},{"text":"en’s Original Chelsea Boots\n\nFit and Comfort\n\nIt took a while for the author to get used to the tighter ankles, but as one tester called out, “The more I wear my Blundstones, the easier they get to pull on.” We also love that Blundstones, unlike other Chelsea boot designs, have two pull tabs for easier maneuvering, rather than one simple one at the back. Both of these loops come in handy rather than stomping your foot down and into the boot, like other Chelsea boot designs can lead to.\n\nOnce they’re on, the classic Blundstones are a delight to wear around, as long as you’re okay with tighter ankles. The ankles do help protect you from water, dirt, rocks, and mud getting into your shoe and under your toes, but we do suggest wearing them with either a calf-height sock or tucked in pants — or both, as the author prefers. A steel shank in the heel adds support while you walk, while the elastic around the ankles helps conform to your feet while giving you the flexibility we look for in lace-up boots.\n\nDesign and Function\n\nWhat we really appreciate about the Blundstones are their ability to go from town to trail. If the day brings a jaunt across a field or trekking out into the drizzly w"},{"text":" fit to be uncomfortable and the shoes felt like, \"they never really broke in no matter how much I walked in them.\" So if you've got wide feet, go with the Classics!\n\nBest waterproof hiking boots: La Sportiva Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX\n\nPrice: $219Sizes available: 5.5 - 11Shaft height: 6.25\"Waterproof: YesMaterial: GORE-TEXFootwear closure: Lace-upBest for: Hiking muddy trails, backpacking, creek and river crossingsWhat we liked: Water resistance, grip, agilityWhat we didn’t like: Narrow fit, moderate ankle support, need a break-in period\n\nThe La Sportiva Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX Hiking Boots are the best waterproof women's hiking boots we tested and one of the most agile, lightweight, grippy boots, too.\n\nWhile we've tested them across Colorado and Washington in creek crossings and mud, what really convinced us these are the best waterproof hiking boots was wearing them for a week straight in Iceland.\n\nIn the notoriously wet and cold weather of Iceland, we realized that the Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX boots aptly handle mud, ice, snow, and river crossings for multiple days.\n\nAfter four months of testing, we realized that they not only support trekking in ideal conditions, but they’re also the"},{"text":" kind of boots you want to be wearing when hikes don’t go according to plan.\n\nWe named it Best lightweight waterproof hiking boots in our Best Waterproof Hiking Boots guide and it also won Best Waterproof Hiking Boot in our Best Women's Hiking Boots guide.\n\nRead more about them in our in-depth review of the La Sportiva Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX boots here.\n\nHow we tested\n\nWe wore these boots for about 35 miles while evaluating qualities such as grip, durability, breathability, and weather resistance, and we directly compared them to more than 10 other pairs of hiking boots.\n\nWaterproofness\n\nMany hiking boots are built with GORE-TEX, but that doesn't mean they are equal. We tested so-called waterproof hiking boots side by side in a local Colorado creek by fully submerging them for 15 minutes. Not only did they protect us from short-term submersion, they also kept water out and our feet remained dry and comfortable at the end of our trek.\n\nWhile some boots passed the first test, only a handful of them managed to pass the second. And the Ultra Raptor 3 Mid GTX boots were the most reliable boots among them when it came to weatherproofing.\n\nExcellent grip and traction\n\nA waterproof hiking "},{"text":"ycled plastics, or plant-based materials.\n\nProper care and maintenance: Extending the life of your boots through regular cleaning and proper storage minimizes unnecessary replacements. See our care and maintenance section on how you can best take care of your rain boots.\n\nEnd-of-life disposal: If your boots wear out, check for recycling programs or upcycling options to keep them out of landfills.\n\nOpting for eco-conscious rain boots helps minimize the environmental impact of your gear.\n\nFAQ\n\nAre rain boots truly waterproof?\n\nNot always. Manufacturers can claim that their boots are waterproof for having a waterproofing treatment and waterproof bottoms. However, if boots cannot withstand submersion in a river bank, deep puddle, or boggy mud for an hour at a time like we tested, we won’t call them waterproof.\n\nCan I wear rain boots in the snow?\n\nAbsolutely! Not every rain boot is insulated, but waterproof boots are the first step towards keeping you warm and dry. Our testers wore Muck Boots and Xtratuf Legacies year round, including winter with the addition of warm ski socks. If you need more insulation, see our guides to the Best Women's Winter Boots and Best Men's Winter Boots.\n\nCan"},{"text":"comfortable across conditions, and is repairable too."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-down-vests","version_id":"df2f34bf808b4c01bfd73c84bd0a7e281f694fba77d09d81d49eed44c97e51df","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Down Vests for Men of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-down-vests","published_at":"2026-10-12T15:34:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, backpacking and mountain testing, home-scale weighing, layering observations, and the Swiftland's questionable warmth benefit provide citable evidence for Mammoth carry-weight tradeoffs and optional insulation choices, supporting Pieces 02 and 03. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require identifying affiliate commissions and attributing findings to this single publisher; broad durability and wet-performance claims lack controlled verification, and Mammoth practitioner evidence is missing. Under Freshness and novelty, the update does not identify which vest is new, so it does not clearly establish what changed for Piece 01.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Down Vests for Men of 2026\n\nThe best men's insulated vests for warmth, aerobic activities, and more\n\nOctober 12th, 2026, updated with a new vestHome → Gear Reviews → Apparel\n\nWhen shoulder season or cold mornings settle in, a down vest is one of the easiest layers to reach for. A good vest adds warmth around your core without the bulk of a full puffy jacket, and it can work just as well on the trail as it does around town.\n\nWe tested insulated vests in Colorado and Wyoming during late summer, fall and winter, wearing them on hikes, mountain runs, backpacking trips, bike rides, camping trips, and around town. We also carried them in packs and wore them with backpacks to see how they held up during regular outdoor use.\n\nWe evaluated warmth-to-weight, shell durability and weather resistance, insulation and baffle construction, fit, comfort, layering, and overall versatility. We paid particular attention to armholes and torso fit, how easily each vest layered over or under other clothing, and how well it held up over time.\n\nTop Pick: The Rab Microlight Down Vest earns our Best Overall award. It combines warm recycled down insulation with a durable Pertex Quantum shell, a comfortab"},{"text":"le fit, and the versatility to work for hiking, everyday wear, and layering in colder conditions. Of the vests we tested, it offered the best overall balance of warmth, comfort, durability, and fit.\n\nLooking for women's down vests? See our Best Women's Down Vests guide\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission, without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall men’s down vest: Rab Microlight Down Vest\n\nWeight: 10.5 ozAvailable sizes: S-XLFit: RegularShell fabric: 30D Pertex Quantum with NetPlus and fluorocarbon-free DWRInsulation type: Recycled 700FP Down with Grangers hydrophobic finishWhat we liked: Very comfortable, recycled down fill, recycled shell, warm, great fitWhat could be better: Heavier than some vests\n\nThe Rab Microlight Vest is the best down vest overall because it’s comfortable, warm, durable, and looks great. What truly makes this vest stand out is its sustain"},{"text":"verything. It’s fantastic overall, and we almost picked it as the best overall down vest, but the Rab Microlight fits better in the arms and has recycled down fill.\n\nThe Down Sweater vest is the vest version of our overall winner for Best Down Jacket in our Best Down Jackets guide. We also dedicate a single product in-depth review to the Patagonia Down Sweater Jacket.\n\nPatagonia Down Sweater Vest\n\nMaterials\n\nThe Down Sweater Vest is made with a 100% recycled 20-denier nylon ripstop outer shell fabric that’s treated with a PFC-free durable water-repellent (DWR) coating for water repellency.\n\nYou can read more about perfluorinated chemicals (PFCs/PFAS) in waterproofing and why the outdoor industry is moving away from them here.\n\nIncredible warmth\n\nThis is one of the warmest vests we’ve worn. On chilly mornings in the Wind River Range, it was warm enough under a windbreaker to keep your core temperature up. And it’s a great addition to a fleece jacket or midlayer when it gets even colder. The Down Sweater Vest is warm enough to wear often without being too hot, so you don’t often need to take it off.\n\nWeight and packability\n\nAt 8.9 ounces, it’s light and packable enough to throw in yo"},{"text":"ur bag for later. This isn’t quite as light as the featherweight Ghost Whisperer, which weighs a mere 5.1 ounces, but under 9 ounces is light enough for most people.\n\nSoft and comfortable\n\nWarmth and weight aside, the thing we love most about the Down Sweater Vest is that it’s downright comfortable. The lining material is incredibly soft and not slippery like some lightweight insulated vests. The face fabric feels so good on your skin that we’re always running our hands along the face material to confirm that it is truly as comfortable as we remember. And the updated fit on the Down Sweater Vest makes it even more comfortable. Previous versions of this vest didn’t fit quite as well; it was boxy and not great for layering under a rain jacket or with a fleece underneath. But the newest version of this vest fits great, making it that much more comfortable.\n\nSustainability\n\nThe Down Sweater Vest is also one of the more sustainable vests we tested. Its shell materials are made of recycled fishing nets, and its 800-fill down insulation is 100% Responsible Down Standard certified by Control Union. Patagonia’s lifetime warranty is one of the best out there as well. They will repair this ve"},{"text":"le Pertex Quantum with a PFAS-free DWR coating. This shell fabric is windproof and softer to the touch than most other down jackets. This shell fabric is super cozy, rivaled in comfort only by the Patagonia Down Sweater and the Rab Microlight vests.\n\nThe only downside to the Eos Vest is that it’s more expensive than many comparable down jackets. For reference, it costs $89 more than our overall top choice for a down vest, the Rab Microlight, and more than the Patagonia Down Sweater Vest. But neither of those options is made in the USA or uses 900-fill down insulation. So even though it does cost more, we think it’s worth it for a high warmth-to-weight ratio down vest that’s made in Seattle, Washington.\n\nIf you’re looking for the best 900-fill down vest out there, we think the Eos Vest is worth considering, even considering the slightly higher price.\n\nBest ultralight men’s down vest: Mountain Hardwear Ghost Whisperer Vest\n\nWeight: 5.6 oz Available sizes: S-XXLFit: RegularShell fabric: 10-denier x 10-denier ripstop recycled nylonInsulation type: 800-fill-power goose downWhat we liked: Ultralight, extremely packable, comfortableWhat could be better: Not as durable, expensive\n\nThe Moun"},{"text":" how different down vests pack down.\n\nMuch like weight, compressibility is important if you plan on carrying your insulated vest in a backpack. The more you can compress your puffer vest, the more space you’ll have in your pack for everything else you need to carry.\n\nGenerally speaking, down insulation is more compressible than synthetic insulation. So, a down-insulated vest is going to be more compressible than a synthetic insulated vest.\n\nIf you store your puffer vest compressed for a long time, that insulation will lose its long-term ability to loft, making it less warm. For this reason it’s best to store your down or synthetic vest on a hanger in your closet rather than stuffed into a stuff sack or pocket.\n\nPockets\n\nWe compared pocket placement and size on each vest, especially whether it could fit a phone.\n\nMost insulated vests have hand warmer pockets. Some also have a chest pocket, which is a great place to keep a smartphone in cold weather both for your easy access to it and to protect its battery life. Some vests don’t have any pockets at all. These are probably the lightest vests you’ll find, but lack the functionality that pockets provide.\n\nHood\n\nHooded vests are less co"},{"text":" typical layering system.\n\nShould a down vest fit tight or loose?\n\nA down vest should fit snug but not tight. It should trap warmth close to your core without compressing the down, which reduces loft and insulation. Leave enough room to fit a sweater or light jacket underneath, and make sure the armholes aren’t restrictive, so you can move freely and layer as needed.\n\nWhat's the difference between down and synthetic insulation?\n\nDown insulation comes from the fine plumage of ducks or geese. It’s extremely light, warm, and compressible, making it ideal for dry, cold conditions. However, it loses warmth when wet and requires more care.\n\nSynthetic insulation, made from polyester fibers, mimics down’s loft but retains heat even when damp and dries faster. It’s easier to wash and usually more affordable, but slightly heavier and less packable than down.\n\nAre insulated vests warm enough for extreme cold?\n\nNot by themselves. Insulated vests keep your core warm, but since they don’t cover your arms, they’re best used as a layering piece in extreme cold. Pair one under a down jacket or shell for added insulation during frigid conditions.\n\nAre insulated vests easy to pack?\n\nYes—one of their "},{"text":"nd commuting\n\nCold nights while camping\n\nSide-by-side weight comparisons\n\nWarmth-to-weight performance\n\nFit and armhole comfort\n\nLayering and freedom of movement\n\nShell durability and weather resistance\n\nPackability and everyday versatility\n\nAbout the author / why you should trust us\n\nSam Schild is a thru-hiker, trail runner, cyclist, and mountain athlete based in Colorado. After moving to Colorado a decade ago, he discovered the benefits of the puffy jacket, then later the slimmed-down sleeveless version, the insulated vest.\n\nHe has thru-hiked the Pacific Crest Trail, Continental Divide Trail, Appalachian Trail, Grand Enchantment Trail, Pacific Northwest Trail, and the Colorado Trail twice.\n\nHe has bikepacked the Colorado Trail, Kokopelli Trail, Sky Islands Odyssey Route twice, and completed a 7,000-mile cross-country bikepacking trip.\n\nOn all these trips, he’s carried a puffy jacket, but spent too much time considering whether the perfect insulated vest might be a better choice. Now he’s convinced that sometimes a vest is all you need.\n\nYou can read more about Sam on his author page or on his website.\n\nFind the best running pants for every season and terrain. Tested for comfort, "},{"text":"breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs in all kinds of weather.\n\nFind the best women's down vests for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability.\n\nWe tested women’s rain boots for waterproofing, traction, comfort, fit, and durability through downpours, muddy trails, farm work, and everyday walking."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-waterproof-socks","version_id":"ecf119cf5f407aea8f56f0ce4bf2bd6105c24a28d2e2850c5b2f1de32339379d","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Waterproof Socks of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-waterproof-socks","published_at":"2026-10-09T23:42:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this updated review offers citable wet-weather foot-care testing, including bucket submersion, washing, and a 70-mile rainy ride during which Bridgedale socks wetted through after about three hours. Commercial incentives requires flagging affiliate commissions and reducing the weight of praise; Uncertainty and Conflicting evidence require attributing these single-reviewer observations and noting inconsistent RANDY SUN material descriptions and limited support for extreme-cold claims. It supports Piece 01’s updated recommendations, Piece 02’s waterproofing assessment, and Piece 03’s evaluation of waterproof socks as an optional layer with fit, warmth, and drying tradeoffs.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Waterproof Socks of 2026\n\nVersatile, durable and waterproof socks for hiking, running, and biking\n\nOctober 9th, 2026, updated with a new best for hiking and new winner for best for cold weatherHome > Gear Reviews > Apparel\n\nWet feet can turn an otherwise great hike, ride, or run into a miserable day. Waterproof socks add another barrier between your feet and rain, puddles, wet trails, and water that makes its way past your shoes, while still allowing more breathability than an improvised plastic barrier.\n\nI tested waterproof socks while hiking, biking, running, and walking in wet Midwest conditions, including muddy trails, steady rain, puddles, and long rides in bad weather. I also submerged the socks in water to compare how well they kept moisture out and how they performed after getting thoroughly wet.\n\nI evaluated each pair for waterproofing, fit, comfort, breathability, durability, and drying performance. I also paid attention to sock height and cuff design, since keeping water from entering through the top can matter just as much as the waterproof membrane itself.\n\nTop Pick: The Sealskinz Dunton Waterproof All Weather Ankle Length Sock with Hydrostop is our Best Overall w"},{"text":"an find on the market.\n\nRANDY SUN Ultra Thin Mid Length waterproof socks were, by far, the thinnest and lightest weight socks of our testing pool.\n\nThe RANDY SUN Ultra Thin socks are especially ideal for summer and warm-season hiking, biking, and/or running. However, their durability is slightly challenged because of their thinner build. We also found these socks did not dry as quickly as competitors like the Bridgedale StormSock, even though they’re a thinner sock. This has to do with the material they’re made of, which is 96% polypropylene. You might also be unwilling to buy these socks as polypropylene often contains PFAS chemicals or “forever chemicals,” which have a negative impact on the environment.\n\nWhile most people recommend wearing a liner sock underneath waterproof socks for comfort and extra warmth, I found that these socks don’t need it and feel comfortable without a liner. Still, we recommend normal-thickness waterproof socks, like the Sealskinz Dunton or Bircham, for long-distance hiking and trekking, as they will be more durable over time.\n\nBest waterproof socks for running and trail running: Drymile ACTIVE\n\nMaterial: 96% Nylon / 3% Spandex 1% Reflective YarnPrice:"},{"text":"ale StormSock is an ideal pick if you’re a hiker, biker, or camper looking for a reliable pair of waterproof socks. We put these socks to the test on a 70-mile bike ride in a continuous downpour, and on casual hikes in the Midwest rainy season. Their wide fit around the ankle, superior room in the toe and arch of the foot, and added cushion throughout won us over.\n\nThe most comfortable, cushioned high-performance waterproof sock of our testing pool, the Bridgedale StormSock rose to the top of the list for our go-to pick for all outdoor activities.\n\nThese midweight socks fall within a happy medium of weight (not too thick, not too thin) meaning they transition well across seasons. The StormSock uses the brand’s proprietary Merino FusionTech liner and a durable nylon outer sock with waterproof treatment to make them extremely breathable and water resistant. We dunked the socks in a bucket of water to test their water resistance and to see how fast they’d dry. They outperformed all other socks in our testing pool for both water repellency and drying time.\n\nWhile Bridgedale did not design the StormSock with a completely seamless toe, the seams at the toe and ankle are minimal. I found "},{"text":"them to have a very comfortable fit and did not notice any rubbing, abrasion, or discomfort along the seam of these socks. Even with a pair of liner socks underneath and a boot on my foot, there were no hot spots on my toe (or anywhere else) while wearing the StormSock thanks to their smooth, knit design and cushioned fit.\n\nMost folks wear liner socks underneath their waterproof socks for warmth and comfort. You’re easily able to fit a pair of lightweight liner socks with plenty of room to spare under the Bridgedales. Even in chilly 50-degree weather with continual rain for hours while biking from Chicago to the Indiana Dunes National Park, these socks kept my feet warm. While water soaked through about 3 hours into the bike ride, they held up for several hours in gnarly conditions en route.\n\nIn my opinion, these socks are an ideal height for bike shoes and hiking boots, as I tend to wear mid or high top shoes and boots. However, if you’re interested in even higher socks, they do come in a knee-height version too. Bridgedale also makes a lightweight version and a heavyweight version depending on your intended use. The lightweight socks come in handy for warm-season hiking and bikin"},{"text":" ACTIVE on a particularly rainy Midwest day, finding they kept their feet not only dry but warm in chilly temps.\n\nWaterproof socks are a niche piece of gear, but in the right conditions they can make the difference between finishing your trip in comfort or slogging home with frozen, wrinkled feet. They work best in situations where your footwear alone can’t keep moisture out:\n\nAll-day rain: Even the best trail runners and hiking boots will eventually wet through in hours of steady rain. A waterproof sock adds a final barrier between your foot and the elements.\n\nMud and slush: On shoulder-season trails or during spring melt, waterproof socks keep your feet dry when mud and melting snow push in through mesh shoes.\n\nSnow travel: If you don’t have insulated boots, a waterproof sock can help keep snowmelt from chilling your feet. Pair with a warm liner sock for extra insulation.\n\nWater crossings: Shallow creeks or boggy meadows are less of a worry when you know your socks won’t instantly soak through.\n\nThat said, waterproof socks are not an everyday replacement for wool hiking socks. They run warmer than standard socks and can feel clammy in hot weather. They also can’t stop water from "},{"text":"pouring in over the cuff if you wade too deep. For most hikers, runners, and bikers, waterproof socks are a problem-solving piece of gear: stash them in your pack and pull them on when you know you’ll be in hours of rain, snow, or mud.\n\nIf you want to extend their usefulness, combine waterproof socks with gaiters or rain pants to seal off the top and prevent water from sneaking in at your ankles. And always keep a dry backup pair of regular socks in your kit — just in case conditions outlast even the best waterproofing.\n\nWhat to look for in waterproof socks\n\nConsider whether you want a lightweight sock that will wick moisture in the heat or a thicker sock that fends off winter temperatures.\n\nHere are the criteria that every good pair of waterproof socks must have to rest assured your feet will stay safe, protected, and dry on your adventures:\n\nMaterial\n\nMaterials play a big role in the overall performance, durability, fit, and comfort of a waterproof sock. According to the Portland brand Showers Pass, waterproof socks are typically constructed with three key elements: knit exterior, waterproof membrane, and knit interior.\n\nIn general terms, the combination of material used in the d"},{"text":" and if they came out smell-free. From our research, we learned it’s key to wash waterproof socks inside out and we washed each pair of socks we tested in the washing machine before tumble drying and air drying.\n\nDuring the drying process, we ran two tests: let them tumble dry on a low heat cycle and, alternatively, let them air dry on a drying rack. Durability also plays a role in the washing process. We monitored for any pilling of fabric or fraying of stitches caused by washing each pair.\n\nPros and Cons of Waterproof Socks\n\nWaterproof socks have their time and place in the outdoors. While not every bike ride, long-distance trek, or casual hike requires them, it’s helpful to have a pair of waterproof socks in your gear closet for rainy, snowy, or humid weather. And while they are an investment, they’re also definitely better than wet, rubbing, or blistering feet…and are a much better option than plastic bag liners. Literally, people put plastic sandwich bags on their feet to serve as a barrier between the rain and their bare skin. Not only is this not sustainable but plastic has no added breathability, meaning whatever sweat builds up inside the bag will surround your feet. Breat"},{"text":"of water to compare waterproof performance and see how each pair handled more direct water exposure. Throughout testing, I evaluated waterproofing, comfort, fit, breathability, durability, and drying performance.\n\nI also paid attention to differences that became especially important during use, including sock thickness, cushioning, stretch, cuff height and security, and how easily each pair fit inside different footwear.\n\nHiking, biking, running, and walking\n\nRainy trails, mud, and puddles\n\nBucket-submersion waterproofing tests\n\nDifferent socks worn underneath\n\nMachine washing and air-drying tests\n\nWaterproofing and wet-through\n\nComfort and fit\n\nBreathability\n\nDurability and cuff security\n\nDrying performance\n\nWhy you should trust us / About the author\n\nI’m Erica Zazo—a freelance writer and gear reviewer who has covered outdoor products and adventure travel for more than a decade. I’ve reviewed products for numerous outdoor and travel publications, including Backpacker Magazine, CNN Underscored, Popular Mechanics, GearJunkie, and more, educating readers about what they need to know to get outdoors safely and enjoyably. I’m an expert in all things outdoor adventure and have spent cou"},{"text":"ens of socks over thousands of miles of hiking, here are the best wool and synthetic hiking and backpacking socks — hiking socks to prevent blisters, the best socks for hiking in hot weather, compression, and more."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-sleeping-pad-inflators","version_id":"678284250968c73e7b8f391aedae91c9d1fddc3fdad6b309f0fd685c1e536b86","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Sleeping Pad Inflators of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-sleeping-pad-inflators","published_at":"2026-10-08T21:40:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this review supplies new evidence from 1.5 years of backpacking and comparative tests of sleep-system accessories, including a measured 7-hour-35-minute Lux runtime, roughly two-minute inflation, and adapter and dirt-exposure limitations relevant to carried weight and exhausted camp setup. Commercial incentives requires discounting affiliate-funded praise; Uncertainty and Conflicting evidence require attributing this single publisher's findings and flagging inconsistent weight and noise figures rather than treating them as settled. It supports Piece 01's newly reviewed gear, Piece 02's performance comparisons, and Piece 03's optional-gear decisions under Necessary versus optional because breath or pump sacks provide workarounds.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Sleeping Pad Inflators of 2026\n\nPad pumps for inflatable sleeping pads for backpacking and camping mattresses\n\nOctober 8th, 2026Home → Gear Reviews → Backpacking & Thru-Hiking\n\nFor ultralight backpackers, thru-hikers, and backpackers of all types, inflating a sleeping pad is the worst camp chore. It either requires huffing and puffing or capturing air in a stuff sack and pumping it, which is significantly less fun to do if it is windy, buggy, cold, rainy, or you're at altitude. With 3.5\" and 4\" inflatable backpacking pads becoming the trend, there is more inflating needed than ever. Pad pumps, aka sleeping pad inflators, use batteries and fans to inflate your sleeping pad, eliminating one of the worst camp chores.\n\nElectric pad inflators are USB-powered or battery-powered, and are faster and require less human power and attention than manual pumps, pump sacks that come with your pad, or breathing into your pad. Pad pumps are often lighter than manual pump sacks, too. This guide doesn't cover manual sleeping pad inflators, which I find annoying and way less convenient than electric pad inflators.\n\nWe spent 1.5 years testing as many sleeping pad inflators as we could find, from "},{"text":"those that work for every backpacking pad or camping mattress out there to ones that only work for one manufacturer. We tested while backpacking on the Arizona Trail and off-trail on the Grand Enchantment Trail, as well as while backpacking in Northern California, the Washington Coast, the Eastern Sierra, Colorado, and while thru-hiking the West Coast Trail. We also ran side-by-side lab tests, noting inflation time on the most popular standard test pads, battery drain, valve compatibility, and noise measured by decibels.\n\nTo judge pad pumps, we considered weight, inflation speed, battery life, power sources, valve compatibility, noise level, durability, and value. We covered everything from sub-30g USB fans to 100g+ multi-function electric vacuum pumps. Ultimately, the right pick depends heavily on what pad you have and how much you value weight vs. speed.\n\nTop pick: The Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Lux is the best overall backpacking sleeping pad inflator. With an inflator, deflator, easy USB-C operation off your phone, and features like a noise machine and camping lantern, it has quickly become a must-have for ultralight backpackers looking to reduce their camp chores and is so cleve"},{"text":"r, deflator, lantern, and noise machine. It is incredibly battery efficient. Since it is a system that runs off your battery pack or phone instead of requiring its own charging, you don't have to worry about charging a separate battery (and the time and USB ports it requires in a trail town to do so). It runs off USB-C and barely uses power, is relatively quiet, and weighs less than 0.5 oz, making switching to a pad pump a minimal weight penalty for backpackers.\n\nWe were able to run the Alpenblow Lux for 7 hours and 35 minutes off one fully-charged 10,000 mAh battery pack, the longest of any pad pump we tested. Using those numbers as estimates, at 2 minutes per inflation, by our measurements, you could potentially get 227 inflations before needing to recharge.\n\nSee more in our in-depth review of the Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Lux.\n\nThe Alpenblow Lux connects to a sleeping pad to handle inflation without having to sit and blow up the pad by mouth.\n\nThe Alpenglow Gear Alpenblow Lux weighs less than half an ounce, making it easy to justify carrying even for ultralight backpacking.\n\nA tester uses the Alpenblow Lux to inflate a NEMO sleeping pad; the pump works with adapters for NEMO and "},{"text":"enjoy doing.\n\nWe also appreciate the adaptability of the Alpenblow Lux, which works with nearly every brand of backpacking pad. The Alpenglow Lux comes with four sleeping pad valve adapters, which are clearly marked with diagrams on its packing tube. You can buy adapters for three other brands of sleeping pads. It comes with four valve adapters that work for Therm-a-Rest, NEMO, Sea to Summit, Big Agnes, and EXPED pads.\n\nWhile not the fastest or lightest pump out there, the Alpenblow Lux hit all the marks we looked for in a pad inflator and then some without costing more than competitors. The Lux is the best overall pad inflator for backpackers, ultralighters, and anyone who hates inflating their backpacking pad.\n\nThe Alpenblow Lux took us about 2 minutes to fully inflate a NEMO Tensor All-Season sleeping pad. It registered only 40 dB on our noise-measuring app, which is better than we can say for other pad pumps. After it finished filling, it requires about two additional mouth inflations to get this pad as firm as I like— it’s unfortunate that the Alpenblow can't fully achieve that level of firmness, like the FLEXTAIL ZERO PUMP can. Still, I'm willing to do two cursory breaths bef"},{"text":"e FLEXTAIL Max Pump 3 weighs more than the other backpacking pad inflators we tested but isn't as robust as the camping mattress inflators we considered.\n\nUltimately, we found the Max Pump 3 cool-looking, but more difficult to use than the FLEXTAIL ZERO PUMP. Its outdoor-indoor versatility ended up being the downfall, as it got confusing figuring out what adapter goes where and to what pad or mattress. It only has six adapters, but somehow, the whole system was more complicated than it needed to be. I thought it was just me, but we had multiple testers—all with hundreds of nights of experience in camping and backpacking inflators—try to figure out this pump. Several of them worked in gear stores with jobs explaining these kinds of devices to customers. Our takeaway was that while versatility is useful for most gear, when it comes to inflators, it is best to get the pad inflator that does one thing really well.\n\nRex Creations Pad Pal\n\nPrice: Not currently purchase-able for any priceWeight: 0.32 oz / 9 gPower source: USB-C equipped Phone or Power bank, also compatible with USB-A portsNoise level: Loud. 82 dB (low), 91 dB (medium), 99 dB (high)\n\nRIP Rex Creations Pad Pal, which was av"},{"text":"deflate functions, but when they do, it makes breaking down camp a lot easier.\n\nDurability\n\nBackpacking pad inflators have small fans that must be protected from sand, grit, and dirt. USB ports need to be kept free of debris. Camping pad pumps need to survive getting tossed around in gear boxes. None of the pumps broke, but the plastic battery cover on the NeoAir inflator detached and we were hypervigilant about getting sand in Alpenglow pad fans. We kept durability in mind when considering pad inflators.\n\nDo you actually need a sleeping pad inflator?\n\nNope. For backpacking pads, most include a pump sack or can be inflated by breath (though that can introduce moisture and potentially mold to the inside of your pad).\n\nHowever, a pad pump is life-changing. I generally hate pump sacks. I find them heavy, difficult to justify as a multi-use item, with finicky valves that never seem to stick. They also don't deflate your pad.\n\nI typically inflate by breath to save weight compared to carrying a pump sack, but it's annoying to do, especially at altitude after an exhausting day of hiking. I've lied to myself, saying the process of doing all that breathing gets fire in my belly to warm up b"},{"text":"efore going to sleep. But I hate it. Plus, backpacking pads are getting thicker these days, with many top pads at nearly 4\" tall! The pad pump changes everything and makes one of the worst camp chores into an easy part of an automated pre-sleep routine.\n\nFor car camping mattresses, I think a pad inflator is essential. If you're spending camping mattress dollars for a good night of sleep, I think the extra ~$60 to get a pad inflator is more than worth it. Camping has enough chores as it is. Spending 30 minutes taking turns manually inflating a camping mattress is the sort of camp chore that teaches kids that camping is not fun and makes adults never want to camp again. Get an easy-to-use, easy-to-connect mattress pump, and it is a total camping game changer.\n\nValve compatibility guide\n\nUnfortunately, there is no universal pad valve. Here are the main sleeping pad valve types:\n\nNote: EXPED and Big Agnes use the same system\n\nYou can see the table above to see what pad pump is compatible with what valves.\n\nWhile some valve adapters are included, adapter packs are available for most electric inflators and are worth buying for older or less common valve types.\n\nCare and maintenance\n\nKeep"},{"text":" your pad inflator away from sand and dirt: That can get caught in motors and break the whole system. I keep mine in a small DCF ditty bag along with my electronics. I usually throw down a polycro or Tyvek groundsheet if I'm inflating my pad while cowboy camping. Inside a tent with a floor, I haven't taken extra precautions.\n\nStore inflators dry: Unless the pump you choose is water-resistant, moisture inside the motor shortens lifespan.\n\nKeep nozzle adapters in a small zip bag: They are easy to lose.\n\nUSB-powered inflators: As with all other electronics, keep the USB-C port clear of debris. Blow it out before connecting.\n\nElectric inflators with onboard batteries: Charge before each trip (trust me on this one). Don't store fully depleted.\n\nFAQ\n\nDo I need a sleeping pad inflator for backpacking?\n\nNo, but it turns what’s arguably the worst camp chore into an easy task. As pads get thicker, pump sacks take longer to use. Pad pumps are often lighter than pump sacks, too. Plus, they help prevent breathlessness at altitude.\n\nWhat's the difference between a USB-powered inflator and one with an onboard battery?\n\nUSB-powered inflators hook up to your phone or battery pack to run. Onboard ba"},{"text":"g the best headlamps for brightness, battery life, comfort, and ease of use for backpacking, hiking, running, climbing, and camping."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-winter-boots","version_id":"85df28e1e6b983751fc364c96f8060a9d025a6fe52989cc6bed14e98cb8e0d5c","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Winter Boots for Men of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-mens-winter-boots","published_at":"2026-10-08T19:55:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this review of older boots adds reported long-term wear and specific cold-weather field experience, including VECTIV use on Katahdin in January and Oboz use at -20°F, plus weight, rubbing, and breathability drawbacks relevant to Mammoth footwear choices. Under Commercial incentives, Treeline earns affiliate commissions while disclaiming brand sponsorship and paid reviews; under Uncertainty, these remain one publisher’s observations, with no Mammoth load-carrying tests or independent verification of temperature ratings. It supports Piece 02’s assessment of performance claims and Piece 03’s insulation, fit, and weight tradeoffs.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Winter Boots for Men of 2026\n\nWinter Boots for Warmth, Water Resistance, Fit, Comfort, And Ice Traction\n\nOctober 8, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Footwear\n\nCold is no stranger to me. I grew up in Colorado and have spent winters in Alaska, Maine, and New Hampshire, where winter boots have had to handle everything from deep snow and icy streets to slush, outdoor work, and long days in the cold.\n\nWe’ve tested men’s winter boots from Alaska and Maine to Colorado, Iowa, Quebec, and the Yukon. I’ve also spent years working and recreating outside in winter, including guiding in Alaska and Colorado and responding to Search and Rescue, ambulance, and fire calls during some seriously cold conditions. That experience has given me a pretty good sense of what works and what doesn’t when your feet need to stay warm, dry, and comfortable for hours.\n\nFor this guide, we looked most closely at traction, waterproofness, fit, comfort, shaft height, and warmth. We also considered how each boot worked for its intended use, whether that meant walking around town, hiking, working outside, or dealing with extreme cold.\n\nTop Pick: The SOREL Caribou is our favorite overall. My dad bought his pair in 1992, a"},{"text":"nd I’ve watched him wear them through winters in New York, Colorado, and Maine. The newest model is the same, and the removable 9 mm liner, waterproof leather, roomy fit, and strong traction make them especially well suited to cold, snowy conditions. His pair lasted long enough to need a replacement liner only after about 20 years of use.\n\nLooking for women's winter boots? See our Best Women's Winter Boots guide.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\n* Prices reflect MSRP and are subject to change. We note any discounts we see on our Deals page, which is updated daily.\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall men’s winter boot and best winter boot for extreme cold: SOREL Caribou\n\nInsulation: Removable 9 mm washable felt inner boot with snow cuffShaft Height: 9.25\"Temperature Rating: -40°FFootwear Closure: LaceWaterproof: YesWhat we liked: long-term durability,"},{"text":"Caribous aren't just practical—they’ve become a symbol of reliability through years of snowy adventures. If you need dependable boots for extreme cold, with a legacy to match, the SORELCaribou remains a top contender.\n\nThey’re so beloved that they are also the winner of our Women’s Winter Boot for Extreme Cold.\n\nFor even more details, you can read our in-depth review of the SOREL Caribou.\n\nBest men's winter boots for walking: Blundstone Classic Chelsea Boots\n\nInsulation: None (insulated version available)Shaft Height: 4.5” Temperature Rating: None stated (insulated version also unrated)Footwear Closure: Slip on, over the ankleWaterproof: No, water-resistantWhat we liked: Classic look, well-made, lasts many years and becomes more comfortable with more wear, water-resistantWhat we didn’t like: Not suited for extreme cold or deep snow\n\nIf you’re looking for a winter boot that can go from the office to the barn and on to the bar, the Blundstones may very well be the best choice for you. With a few technological upgrades—like their cushioning Shock Protection System (SPS) midsole—they’ve kept the classic look while keeping the footwear relevant for a new era. SPS is designed to disperse"},{"text":" shock when your foot strikes the ground, which reduces the amount of stress on your skeletal system. (Read: comfier, happier feet and joints!)\n\nBlundstone leather boots also have removable, anatomically correct EVA footbeds for extra cushion and support. The soles hold a 4mm tread for good grip on ice, slush, mud, and pavement alike. The soles also hold a steel shank that runs down the middle, providing enough rigidity to support and stabilize these ankle boots.\n\nThe Blundstones are a breeze to slip on and off. While the majority of the boot is leather, the stretchy sides are made of elastic to aid with easy on-and-off. Front and back loops add to the ease.\n\nAlthough an Australian-based company, Blundstone offers plenty of warm options. Love the Blundstone look but need more insulation? We also tested the Blundstone Thermal All-Terrain boot, a thermal waterproof boot with Thinsulate and shearling insulation.\n\nYou can read our in-depth review of those Blundstone Thermal boots for more information.\n\nBest men's casual winter boots: L.L.Bean Flannel-Lined Insulated Bean Boots\n\nInsulation: 200 g PrimaLoftUpper Lining: 100% cotton brushed chamois cloth; 7.5 oz per sq. yd.Bottom Unit Lin"},{"text":"ve a D-ring to work with winter hiking gaiters, so they play well with other winter hiking gear.\n\nFor most hikers, we think the VECTIV Fastpack will be all you’ll need.\n\nBest men's winter work boots: Muck Boot Arctic Sport Boots\n\nInsulation: 5 mm neoprene, fleece linedShaft Height: 16”Temperature Rating: -40°FFootwear Closure: Pull-onWaterproof: YesWhat we liked: Very convenient when working outside and coming inside throughout the day, waterproof, warm, easy slip-on/pull-off, arch supportWhat we didn’t like: Heel rubbing, tight on wider shins after prolonged use\n\nIf you don’t want to deal with lacing up your boots every time you go outside, the Muck Boot Arctic Sport Boots are for you. The Muck Boot Arctic Sports are warm, waterproof, durable, and exactly what you want on your feet in harsh conditions and extreme cold.\n\nCalled the workhorse of the men’s boot world for a reason, the Arctic Sports are way less of a fashion statement than our winner for walking, the Blundstones, or L.L.Bean Boots, but infinitely more functional.\n\nWhile definitely on the heavier side for winter boots, the Arctic Sports are filled with 5 mm of neoprene insulation, lined with fleece, and have 2 mm of th"},{"text":"oing to find.\n\nWith insulated and non-insulated options, these are the ultimate winter boots for those who live with heavy, wet snow — especially along coastlines. The non-insulated options work well in shoulder seasons, warmer winters, or just plain wet snow.\n\nWhile XTRATUF boots come in a range of heights, our favorite is the Legacy 15”. Fifteen inches is tall enough to keep out even deep puddles, but the waterproof triple-dipped neoprene rubber shafts allow you to roll them down for sweaty feet. At that height, the XTRATUF Legacies settle comfortably at the top of the calf in a snug but not restrictive manner.\n\nFor the best fit, XTRATUF recommends sizing up if you wear a half size. Try the boots with the socks you plan to wear.\n\nBe warned: the non-insulated version is not a good option for those whose feet get cold easily! They’re great for those who run warm or for wet winter weather that’s above freezing. It’s just enough warmth but not too much.\n\nThese are my go-to winter and rain boots (I spend 200+ days a year in them).\n\nRead my long-term, in-depth review of the XTRATUF Legacy boots for more details.\n\nBest men's winter boots for wide feet: KEEN Revel IV Polar Boots\n\nInsulat"},{"text":"ur activity level and how much weight you’re comfortable carrying for extended periods.\n\nBreathability\n\nBreathability often competes with warmth and waterproofing. If you’re prone to sweaty feet, look for boots with moisture-wicking linings or materials that balance warmth and ventilation.\n\nInsulation Types\n\nDifferent insulation materials suit different climates. Thick wool or synthetic insulation works best for freezing temps, while lighter materials suffice for milder winters. Choose the right insulation based on your activities and weather conditions.\n\nHow to choose the right winter boots for your needs\n\nFinding the perfect pair of winter boots starts with considering where you’ll wear them and what you’ll be doing. Whether you’re braving city slush or trekking through rural snowdrifts, here’s how to choose boots that meet your needs.\n\nFor urban winters\n\nIf you’re commuting, running errands, or navigating icy sidewalks, focus on:\n\nWaterproofing: Keep your feet dry in rain, snow, or slush with waterproof materials and sealed seams.\n\nGrip: Sidewalks and stairs can be slippery, so look for soles with excellent traction.\n\nStyle: Winter boots can still look great! Leather or faux-fur"},{"text":"acrificing the grip or comfort needed to actually spend time outside in winter.\n\nFrom there, we looked more closely at what mattered for each award. A winter hiking boot needed traction and support for icy terrain and enough versatility for different conditions. A work boot needed to stay warm and comfortable through long stretches outside while still being easy to get on and off. Boots for city use had different priorities, including comfort, traction, and practicality for moving between indoor and outdoor environments.\n\nWe used those field observations alongside product specifications and research to decide which boots made the most sense for each use case.\n\nSnow and icy terrain\n\nDeep-cold winter conditions\n\nWet snow and slush\n\nWinter hiking and outdoor work\n\nLong-term everyday winter use\n\nTraction on snow and ice\n\nWaterproofness\n\nFit and comfort\n\nWarmth and insulation\n\nShaft height and intended use\n\nAuthor’s Expertise: Why you should trust us\n\nCold is no stranger to me. I grew up in Colorado with New York-expat parents and spent winters in Alaska, Maine, and New Hampshire. I’m no stranger to cold feet. I wouldn’t give up the cold for a sunny beach day, ever — but I do prefer to "},{"text":"assador, or an employee of any of the companies mentioned here. This is unbiased, and we do this to help you find the best product for the price. Treeline Review doesn’t accept sponsored content, native advertising, or paid reviews.\n\nYou can read all of my articles on my author page.\n\nWe reviewed 27 men’s ski jackets to find the 7 best hardshells, backcountry, insulated, 3-in-1, sub-$350, and waterproof coats. Here are our top rated winners.\n\nProtect your head on the slopes with the best ski and snowboard helmets, reviewed and tested by our Treeline experts. Get ready for your next snow adventure!\n\nLooking for a way to stay upright in icy conditions? Sure-footed traction in snow and ice allows you to carry your outdoor life into the shoulder seasons and beyond."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/exped-terra-sleeping-bag-review","version_id":"9b22e426245124bef28813636a999eca8b200950b63c6e6ccd0028b7a03f4d41","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Exped Terra Sleeping Bag Review: Big Down Warmth Without the Big Price","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/exped-terra-sleeping-bag-review","published_at":"2026-10-08T14:10:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, nine months of reported sleep-system testing adds transferable backpacking evidence, including 9 L compression and zipper snagging despite Exped’s anti-snag claim. Commercial incentives requires discounting this affiliate-funded reviewer’s praise; Uncertainty and Conflicting evidence require attributing its observations and making clear that above-freezing testing does not validate the bag’s low-temperature limits or wet-weather performance. The findings support Piece 01’s newly reviewed gear, Piece 02’s claim comparisons, and Piece 03’s warmth, bulk, and weight trade-offs without establishing that this particular bag is necessary.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Exped Terra Sleeping Bag Review: Big Down Warmth Without the Big Price\n\nA down mummy bag for backpackers who need reliable warmth at an excellent price\n\nOctober 8th, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Backpacking\n\nOur verdict\n\nThe Exped Terra sleeping bag is a down sleeping bag for backpackers and campers looking to level up their game without spending too much money. If you already own a sleeping bag and have found it insufficient, the Exped Terra is a quality bag that gives you a lot of features for a reasonable price.\n\nIt's soft, lofty, relatively wide, and comfortable, and comes with Exped's legendary repairs and customer service. It comes with its own compression sack (usually an extra $40) and compacts down surprisingly well.\n\nMultiple Treeline Review testers took the Exped Terra 15 out for nine months of testing, including a month straight of camping in the Sierra at 8,000 feet and camping in Pinnacles National Park in the spring. We had testers of multiple sizes, heights, and familiarity with camping test this bag. Some of our testers were self-described \"cold sleepers,\" whereas others run warmer. We had side, belly, and back sleepers in the test pool.\n\nExped Terra Sleeping Bag\n\nThe"},{"text":"elected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nExped Terra Sleeping Bag specifications\n\nAll variants\n\nInsulation: 650+ grey duck down, RDS-certified, OEKO-TEX® 100 certified\n\nShell: 20D recycled ripstop polyester, OEKO-TEX® 100 certified, PFAS-free DWR\n\nLining: 20D recycled ripstop polyester, OEKO-TEX® 100 certified\n\nShape: Mummy\n\nZipper: Left-hand, anti-snag\n\nDraft tube: Yes\n\nDraft collar: Yes\n\nHood: Yes, contoured with drawcord\n\nInterior pocket: Yes\n\nStuff sack included: Yes (compression)\n\nStorage sack included: Yes\n\nSustainability: OEKO-TEX Standard 100 fabrics, RDS-certified down, recycled shell and lining, PFAS-free\n\nTerra 30°F (0°C)\n\nTerra 15°F (-9°C)\n\nTerra 0°F (-18°C)\n\nBest for\n\nCampers and backpackers purchasing their second sleeping bag after being disappointed with lower-quality down bags or synthetic sleeping bags\n\nCampers and backpackers who want one bag for both activities\n\nCampers and backpackers considering the NEMO Disco "},{"text":"who want similar features and to spend less money\n\nComparison table\n\nWhat we liked\n\nGood value for the features\n\n3D foot box means feet don't get cold as easily\n\nAvailable in lots of lengths and widths, so you could get the same bag for everyone in your group\n\nComes with a compression stuff sack, which usually costs extra\n\nRecycled shell and lining fabrics, OEKO-TEX 100 certified, with a PFAS-free DWR\n\nInternal pocket is handy to keep your phone from freezing overnight or keep your lip balm close by\n\nWhat could be better\n\nLimited color options\n\nIt's heavier than down sleeping bags that use higher fill (it only has a 650 fill)\n\nDoesn't use waterproof down\n\nOnly available with the zipper on the left side of the bag\n\nThe 30°F bag is independently tested to have a “Comfort rating” of 39°F and a “Lower Limit” of 30°F, so we think the model name should read 35°F instead of 30°F\n\nHow we tested\n\nMultiple Treeline Review testers used this sleeping bag for over a month straight while backpacking and camping in the Eastern Sierra. We also tested it while camping in Pinnacles National Park.\n\nWe've tested the Exped Terra sleeping bag using camping cots, camping mattresses, and on backpacking sl"},{"text":"eeping pads.\n\nTreeline testers have slept in the Exped Terra sleeping bag in camping tents, backpacking tents, and indoors in rustic cabins. We've even used it for sleepovers on the ground in the living room and on couches at friends' houses.\n\nWe've had back-sleeping, side-sleeping, and belly-sleeping testers. We had testers of different body shapes and sizes, ages, and metabolism rates, from self-described \"hot sleepers\" to \"cold sleepers.\"\n\nMost importantly, we tested the Exped Terra sleeping bag side by side against similarly priced bags: the REI Co-op HunkerDown 20, the Kelty Cosmic, and even a budget sleeping bag we got for free from a car insurance company.\n\nWe tested the Exped Terra for different needs, considering the trade-offs among price, weight, packability, long-term durability, features, and comfort. Warmth wasn't really negotiable.\n\nPerformance in the field\n\nWarmth and temperature accuracy\n\nWe tested the Exped Terra 15 sleeping bag and found it lofty, soft, and warm. We tested in temperatures above freezing. While we didn’t test at the limit, we were surprised by how warm, even hot, this bag felt, even for cold sleepers. Some of the other sleeping bags we tested side"},{"text":" by side that claim to be 15°F bags felt cold under similar conditions, even though we were testing them literally side by side in the same tent and on the same NEMO Tensor Extreme Conditions sleeping pad.\n\nIt's worth noting that Exped did their temperature rating assuming you are using a sleeping pad with an R-value of 7. R-value is a measure of insulative capability. The R-value of your sleeping pad can arguably be more important than your sleeping bag's temperature rating for keeping you warm. Most independent tests for sleeping bag warmth assume you're sleeping on a pad with an R-value of 5, whereas we occasionally see an R-value of 7+ be rated for -40°F temperatures. That means we take the temperature ratings listed in the name with a grain of salt.\n\nExped recommends using a sleeping pad with an R-value of at least 6.5. You can read more about R-value in our sleeping pads guide.\n\nComfort and fit\n\nThe mummy shape on the Exped Terra 15 sleeping bag was quite comfortable. We tested in a long wide size and found it reminiscent of the Spoon shape on the NEMO Disco sleeping bag. There was room to move; the hood was easy to secure. For cold sleepers, you may find the wide unnecessary"},{"text":"hares a lot with the Terra. They are similar in weight, come in similar levels of warmth, and have a similar audience of backpackers and campers.\n\nThe Exped Terra is about $70 less, depending on the bag's length and degree. The NEMO Disco Endless Promise has a more robust sustainability story, as it is made from recycled materials and is fully recyclable at the end of its life. The NEMO Disco Endless Promise also has a Spoon shape, which appeals to backpackers and campers who often feel cramped in traditional mummy bags. The Disco also has Thermo Gill vents that can be opened and closed to better regulate temperature.\n\nChoose Terra if you are fine with mummy bags, are looking to save some money, and don't often find yourself needing to vent from an over-warm sleeping bag. For more, read our full review of the NEMO Disco Endless Promise.\n\nExped Terra vs. REI Magma\n\nTemperature rating: 30°FShape: MummyInsulation: 850 fill downWeight: 1 lb 8.4 ozPacked size: 7 x 15 in\n\nThe REI Co-op Magma is REI's higher-end backpacking sleeping bag made of quality materials. Still, it's competitively priced for what you get, using 850-fill down insulation instead of the 650-fill down insulation found"},{"text":"bers.\n\nJenson\n\nPros: Price matching, free shipping, no membership required, limited-time sale offers.\n\nExped\n\nYou can sometimes find more options when purchasing directly from the manufacturer. Purchasing directly from the manufacturer may make it easier to process a warranty return for a defect.\n\nExped Terra FAQ\n\nHow do the three Exped Terra temperature ratings compare?\n\nThe Exped Terra 30°F is Comfort Rated to 39°F and has a Lower Limit of 30°F, which should keep \"hot sleepers\" comfortable. The Exped Terra 15°F has a tested Comfort rating of 25°F and a Lower Limit rating of 12°F. The Exped Terra 0°F has a Lower Limit of 1°F and a Comfort Rating of 14°F.\n\nIs the Exped Terra good for backpacking or just camping?\n\nThe Exped Terra is a down bag that is very compact (it comes with its own compression stuff sack) and relatively lightweight, making it a good sleeping bag for backpacking and camping.\n\nHow does the Exped Terra compare to the Exped DeepSleep?\n\nThe Exped Terra is a down sleeping bag that is lighter and more compact than the synthetic Exped DeepSleep. The Exped Terra costs more because down insulation often costs more than synthetic insulation, but down bags often last longe"},{"text":"r. Some down bag owners have found their bags still warm after 10+ years, whereas synthetic bags often feel colder after 3 years, depending on how you care for them.\n\nDoes the Exped Terra perform well in wet conditions?\n\nThe Exped Terra shell material has some durable water repellency (DWR), meaning raindrops and other precipitation will bead up on the surface and roll off. However, the Terra doesn't use waterproof down insulation, so once water penetrates the shell material, wet down will lose its insulating properties.\n\nWhat sizes does the Exped Terra come in?\n\nThe Terra is available in five sizes — Small, Medium, Medium Wide, Long and Long Wide — fitting people up to 67, 72 or 77 inches, with shoulder girths from 58 to 68 inches.\n\nIs the Exped Terra's temperature rating accurate?\n\nThe 30°F Terra's ISO Comfort is 39°F and Lower Limit 30°F, so cold sleepers should go with the 15°F for warmth. Testers found the 15°F warm, even for cold sleepers, in above-freezing conditions.\n\nDoes the Exped Terra come with a compression sack?\n\nYes. The Terra includes a compression stuff sack plus a storage sack, and the 15°F bag compresses to about 9 L.\n\nWhy you should trust us / About the author\n\n"},{"text":" as Yale and MIT, as well as Capitol Hill, the Trust for Public Land, the American Hiking Society, and BikeTexas.\n\nYou can read more about Liz on her Wikipedia page and on her Treeline author page!\n\nSkip the huffing and puffing. Find the best electric sleeping pad pumps, tested for weight, inflation speed, battery life, and compatibility with your pad.\n\nCold and warm sleepers tested the Exped Terra down sleeping bag for nine months. See how its temperature ratings, mummy fit, and packability held up.\n\nWe spent 400+ nights testing the best headlamps for brightness, battery life, comfort, and ease of use for backpacking, hiking, running, climbing, and camping."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/brawyn-essential-supreme-bra-review","version_id":"d31e9f337a8af67a512cb7f07814e94acf86a69cdd3058918f2608d24de42d6d","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra Review: A Merino Sports Bra With Breathing Room","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/brawyn-essential-supreme-bra-review","published_at":"2026-10-08T13:09:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, four months of newly reported backpacking and extended-wear testing provide citable observations about comfort beneath pack straps, odor, drying, durability, and inadequate support for running that transfer to Mammoth clothing decisions. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, Treeline earns affiliate commissions, its three testers constitute one publisher rather than independent corroboration, and prolonged cold performance and the revised pads remain untested. This supports Piece 01’s newly reviewed gear, Piece 02’s assessment of performance claims, and Piece 03’s fit and support tradeoffs without establishing that this particular bra is necessary.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra Review: A Merino Sports Bra With Breathing Room\n\nCenter ruching ends the uniboob, merino keeps the funk away, and it's comfy enough to forget you're wearing it.\n\nOctober 8, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Apparel\n\nOur verdict\n\nBRANWYN’s Essential Supreme Bra, an evolution of their merino wool Essential Busty Bra, provides a solution for the dreaded “uniboob”—that is, the amorphous shape created when breast tissue is smooshed down while wearing a compressive, cup-less bra.\n\nAs my own, now-independent boobs can attest, BRANWYN succeeded in this quest by adding center ruching to create separation, along with sleeves for removable pads (which must be purchased separately) that provide additional definition.\n\nAs someone who fluctuates between a 36C and D cup, usually on the lower end, I’ve been wearing the BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra in size medium since late spring 2026. I’ve worn it on day hikes and walks, backpacking trips, multi-day travels, at the gym, and around the house.\n\nThanks to its soft feel, odor resistance, contoured shape, lightweight support, and overall versatility, it’s become the bra I reach for most often. It’s kept me comfortable in a varie"},{"text":"ty of situations, including lifting weights, physical therapy, and backpacking in the mountains, and it fits well under a variety of clothing, including dressier options.\n\nThat said, the Essential Supreme Bra doesn’t provide enough support for higher-impact activities (although I have done a few very short, very light jogs while wearing it), and its sizing isn't as inclusive as it could be for those with larger busts. But it is a great all-around option for people who want a comfortable, everyday sports bra with light support that also gives your boobs a little bit of breathing room.\n\nBRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nBRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra specifications\n\nPrice: $76 (set of optional cup pads is an additional $4.50)\n\nCup/band size range: Cup sizes from A to G, band sizes from 28” to 44”\n\nSupport level: Low\n\nStyle: Convertib"},{"text":"hing provides cup separation, which gives breathability and a more defined silhouette than cupless sports bras\n\nOptional pads provide additional definition and modesty\n\nThe straps are convertible and lie flat beneath backpack straps\n\nThe use of merino wool is great for comfort, odor resistance, and regulation of heat and moisture\n\nWhat could be better\n\nAs with most sports bras, the removable pads can migrate in the pocket and feel a bit thin overall\n\nFabric can get stuck in the hooks at the end of each strap if not hand-washed\n\nSizing could be more inclusive\n\nHow we tested\n\nThe Essential Supreme is made mostly of merino wool and is quick-drying; the author sweated profusely on this day, and the bra dried quickly in the sun.\n\nAs someone who fluctuates between a 36C and D cup, I chose the medium size, which provided just the right amount of support — not too tight and not too loose. The version I tested was the original model, which came with circular cup pads; in the newer model, the pads are an optional, separate purchase and are more triangular, creating a less obtrusive silhouette under the bra material.\n\nI spent four months testing the BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra in a variety "},{"text":"cup and band size (a medium) meant I felt a bit more compression than I do in their Essential Busty Bra in a large.\n\nFellow tester Stasia agrees that it’s more supportive than the Busty Bra, “but would still consider it very much a low support/impact bra,” and I agree. For me, using the cup pads provided a little extra hold, as did crossing the straps in the back. I also felt that the thick (2.5”) band was more supportive under my bust than thinner-banded sports bras that I use, and I rarely have to fiddle with it to get it to lie flat during activity.\n\nI’ve felt perfectly supported across the range of activities I’ve tried while wearing this bra, and I haven't needed to adjust it in motion, even during heavy upper-body workouts (say, shoulder day at the gym). While I felt comfortable enough doing short, light jogs while testing, I wouldn’t use this bra for a real run or, say, a HIIT class, since it doesn’t provide enough support or compression for bounce control.\n\nComfort over extended wear\n\nI owned four BRANWYN bras before testing this one because they are cute, comfortable, and durable — and the same holds true for the Essential Supreme Bra. As someone with some sensory sensitiv"},{"text":"ing it. I wasn’t surprised, then, that the Essential Supreme was just as funk-free on multiple wears. In addition to daily wear, I wore it on a week-long trip to visit my family, on several long weekend trips into the mountains, and during several multi-day backpacking trips.\n\nIn fact, as I write this, I’m several days — and several hard workouts — into wearing it without so much as a quick rinse (just in case you’re concerned, I have taken it off at some point every day). I think it’s been soaped up maybe three times in four months. Tester Liz agrees with me on the bra’s stank resistance, saying, “I almost never wash it because the odor doesn’t stick around.”\n\nEase of use\n\nThe Essential Supreme Bra is pretty simple to slip on over your head, especially since it isn’t a tight compression bra. The optional bra pads slide in easily, although they may need to be repositioned once the bra is on. The convertible straps use a simple slider for easy length adjustment and connect to the bra body via flat metal hooks at the ends of each strap and fabric loops on the back of the bra. I’ve found that the straps stay exactly where I’ve adjusted them; I haven’t had to readjust since I first sta"},{"text":"tified, meaning its growers must meet high standards for animal welfare and sustainability.\n\nConvertible straps\n\nThe straps connect using simple metal hooks that clip into fabric loops on the back of the bra and can be worn straight over each shoulder or crossed in the back.\n\nRemovable pads\n\nLike many removable sports bra pads, these do migrate in the large pocket and may need to be readjusted with each wear.\n\nThe bra has a built-in pocket in each cup that accommodates an optional pad (purchased separately and available in three sizes: XS/S, M/L, XL/XXL).\n\nCenter ruching\n\nThe bra is constructed with center ruching knit directly into the front, creating separation for comfort and appearance.\n\nWhat's new\n\nWhen the BRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra was first released in March 2026, it cost $78 and came with removable pads. The price has since dropped to $76, and the pads, now triangular rather than circular, must be purchased separately for $4.50.\n\nFAQ\n\nDoes the BRANWYN Essential Supreme run true to size?\n\nWith only six sizes (XS to XXL), there is some variability in sizing, although the brand’s sizing chart does an excellent job of listing the size recommendations for each cup (A to G) a"},{"text":" nylon and elastane, the band is actually the same material blend as the rest of the garment, which creates a uniform feel against the body.\n\nWhile both bras are nearly the same price, the Essential Supreme offers encapsulation, convertible straps, and optional pads that allow for more user customization.\n\nBRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra vs. Girlfriend Collective Paloma Racerback Bra\n\nMSRP: $68Material: Recycled plastic, spandexAvailable sizes: XXS-6XL\n\nTreeline’s pick for Best Sports Bra for Everyday Wear goes to Girlfriend Collective’s Paloma Racerback Bra, ga compressive, racerback-style bra made from recycled synthetic materials.\n\nWhile the Paloma offers more compression than the BRANWYN Essential Supreme, allowing users to engage in moderate-impact activities without bounce or strain, its material is not quite as breathable as the Essential Supreme’s majority merino blend. The latter’s quick-drying and odor-resistant qualities, along with its lack of compression, enhance the bra’s all-day wearability.\n\nBRANWYN Essential Supreme Bra vs. Ridge Merino Send-It Seamless Sports Bra\n\nMSRP: $60Material: Recycled nylon, merino wool, spandexAvailable sizes: XS-XL\n\nRidge Merino’s Send-It S"},{"text":"customers.\n\nWhy you should trust us / About the author\n\nShawnté Salabert is an avid hiker, backpacker, trail runner, gym rat, and yoga fiend who wears a sports bra for at least an hour or two every day — if not all day. As someone who fluctuates between a 36C and 36D, she spent four months testing a medium BRANWYN Essential Supreme at home, in the gym, on the trail (on day hikes and overnights), and while traveling. She has years of experience testing sports bras for this and other outlets, and, as someone with some sensory sensitivity, she is very particular about what she likes and doesn’t like.\n\nShawnté is the author of Hiking the Pacific Crest Trail: Southern California (Amazon | Bookshop), a guidebook to the first 942.5 miles of the iconic National Scenic Trail, with another book coming from Mountaineers Books in 2027. She is also the host of the Gear Abby podcast, where she draws on her experience as an outdoor journalist and educator to answer listeners' questions about outdoor people, places, products, and pastimes.\n\nYou can read more on her website at shawntesalabert.com and discover her other Treeline Review stories on her author page.\n\nFind the best running pants for eve"},{"text":"ry season and terrain. Tested for comfort, breathability, and performance to keep you moving on road and trail runs in all kinds of weather.\n\nFind the best women's down vests for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability.\n\nWe tested men’s down vests on hikes, backpacking trips, alpine runs, bike rides, and cold camps for warmth, comfort, fit, packability, and durability."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-walking-shoes-men","version_id":"269805f4b18e128a306ef239975a5de1cdff96e6551ee3fa8a07d48fc2b86e9a","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Walking Shoes for Men of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-walking-shoes-men","published_at":"2026-10-06T15:53:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, the Transport 2's described hiking tests across several regions, traction observations, and specific wide-foot fit failure provide citable evidence for endurance footwear, supporting Pieces 02 and 03. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, Treeline earns affiliate commissions, so its praise deserves less weight as independent confirmation; the predominantly pavement and workplace testing does not establish performance under multi-day loaded rucking, and the Clifton GTX waterproofing endorsement exceeds its limited testing.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Walking Shoes for Men of 2026\n\nComfortable, supportive men's shoes for travel, fitness, work, and all-day wear\n\nOctober 6th, 2026Home → Gear Reviews → Footwear\n\nAnyone who has ever walked a mile in ill-fitting, unsupportive, or generally uncomfortable shoes knows how important a quality pair of walking shoes is, especially if you’ll be on your feet all day. A pair that feels fine taking out the trash can turn into heel rubbing a couple of hours into a work shift or arch fatigue while hurrying to catch your layover flight.\n\nWe tested the top walking shoes and tracked performance for over a year, from icy sidewalks in January to scorching pavement in July, to help you prevent those aforementioned discomforts. We wore them on daily commutes, errands, travel days, and up to 12-hour hospital shifts on our feet. We tested comparable models side by side to see how cushioning and support actually held up against each other, rather than just against a spec sheet.\n\nWe paid close attention to whether the cushioning packed out, whether the fit stayed secure, whether new hot spots formed, and how well the sole gripped on wet, slick tile and trails. We also factored in style and how easily "},{"text":"the shoes transitioned between activities, since many people don't want to swap shoes between work and happy hour, workouts, or errands.\n\nTop Pick: The HOKA Bondi SR earns our Best Overall award. It remained cushioned and comfortable through 7-hour brewery shifts and 12-hour nursing shifts. It gripped equally well on wet tile and dry pavement during testing, carries the American Podiatric Medical Association Seal of Acceptance, and is stylish and functional enough to transition from work to walks.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\nThe winners\n\nBest overall men’s walking shoes: HOKA Bondi SR\n\nWeight (per pair): 27.8 ozDrop: 6 mmCushioning: MaximumAvailable sizes: Men’s 7-15, regular and wide sizesWhat we liked: Cushioned, stable, supportive, comfortable, slip resistant, and can be used for exercise walking or casual wearWhat we didn't like: Bu"},{"text":"s is the best waterproofing you’re going to find in athletic footwear, so despite our limited testing in them, we have a lot of faith in this pair if you’ll be walking or commuting in wet conditions.\n\nBest men’s walking shoes with high cushion: HOKA Bondi 9\n\nWeight (per pair): 21.0 ozDrop: 5 mmCushioning: MaximumAvailable sizes: 7 - 16What we liked: Excellent cushion and comfort; great for running and other exerciseWhat we didn't like: Not for casual wear; not as responsive as other running-focused walking shoes\n\nThe HOKA Bondi 9s are the non-slip-resistant, road running shoe-version of our best overall pick, the HOKA Bondi SR. These shoes are heavily cushioned and have HOKA’s MetaRocker technology in the midsole to encourage forward motion and a smooth ride.\n\nThe HOKA Bondi 9s differ from the HOKA Bondi SRs primarily in that they don’t have the slip-resistant grip on the outsole and they weigh less/are less clunky for running and faster walking. The Bondi 9s cost $5 less than the Bondi SRs.\n\nThe HOKA Bondi 9s are featured in the Best Recovery Shoes guide.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the HOKA Bondi 9 road running shoes.\n\nLike the HOKA Clifton 11s, we also love these shoes"},{"text":" for active recovery and daily road running. The main difference between the Clifton 11s and Bondi 9s is that the Bondis have more plush cushioning and are less responsive than the Cliftons. The Cliftons are better suited for a wider range of running paces, while the Bondis are more comfort-driven and better reduce impact on the joints.\n\nWe tested the men’s and women’s HOKA Bondi 9s as both a running shoe and a walking shoe. They perform well in both roles, and are a great option for those looking for lots of impact absorption to reduce aches in joints.\n\nBest men’s walking shoes for trails: HOKA Transport 2\n\nWeight (per pair): 22.6 ozDrop: 5 mmCushioning: BalancedAvailable sizes: 7 - 15What we liked: Grippy Vibram outsole, balance of cushion and support, comfortableWhat we didn't like: Not available in extra-wide sizing, not as versatile as other shoes in terms of style and occasion\n\nIf you’re looking for a walking shoe that crosses over to hiking, the HOKA Transport 2 is our top choice. Like all HOKA shoes we’ve tested, these shoes balance cushion and support for all-day comfort regardless of what surfaces you’re standing or walking on, while the Vibram outsole handles more techni"},{"text":"is in both her feet. After doing lots of research, she started wearing the Birkenstock Arizonas for better support while her plantar fascia healed.\n\nTo be clear, the Birkenstock Arizonas do not cure plantar fasciitis, and you should see a medical professional if you have foot pain. This is simply the best shoe we’ve tested to help support our feet while our plantar fascia heals.\n\nNot everyone enjoys walking in sandals, though, and these may not be your best option for particularly wet or cold environments (though we often pair our Arizonas with wool socks when it’s chilly). But if your priorities include bespoke arch support and footwear that will last you many years, the Arizonas are a tried-and-true favorite.\n\nBest men’s zero-drop walking shoes: Lems Mesa Verde\n\nWeight (per pair): 20.1 ozDrop: 0 mmCushioning: ModerateAvailable sizes: 4.5 - 15What we liked: Offers good style for a barefoot shoe, has a wide toe box, and has some cushion for those transitioning to barefoot shoesWhat we didn't like: Zero-drop isn’t for everyone, and some barefoot shoe veterans may want something even more minimalist than this\n\nThe Lems Mesa Verde is a zero-drop, minimalist walking shoe that still off"},{"text":"n for those who want a minimalist shoe for walking. These shoes are our best everyday barefoot shoes pick for our Best Barefoot Shoes guide.\n\nLearn more in our in-depth review of the Xero Shoes Prio Neo.\n\nThey do not provide a lot of cushion like most of the walking shoes we tested, but they are lightweight, breathable, and flexible for more natural movement. They have a wide toe box for your feet and toes to splay out naturally. The upper is thin and flexible, and snug when on. While testing, we occasionally wore these as gym shoes if we were walk-commuting to the gym.\n\nThese shoes are minimalist, and designed for a “barely there” feel, while also still feeling comfortable enough for all-day walking or travel. What you may lose in cushioning or foam you gain in groundfeel, gradual foot strength, and balance. The flex on these shoes is amazing, and our testers (who are accustomed to zero-drop, minimalist footwear) felt comfortable in them for over 55 miles of walking during testing.\n\nThe Prio Neo is Xero’s most stable, all-around “athleisure” shoe. Xero also offers the Prio Suede, as a separate model. They also have a more budget-friendly price than the other shoes we tested, so if"},{"text":" you can often feel the lack of cushion while wearing them (often in the form of new aches in your joints). When these elements of the shoe wear down, it’s time to replace them.\n\nFAQ\n\nWhat's the difference between walking shoes and running shoes?\n\nWhile running shoes can be used as walking shoes, walking-specific shoes typically prioritize support, comfort, and style, whereas running shoes also prioritize breathability, reducing impact, traction, and propelling forward motion.\n\nRoad running shoes can make great walking shoes, as they tend to be comfortable, adequately supportive, and designed for the same surfaces people commonly walk on.\n\nIf most of your walking will be at a slower pace, or you prefer a more casual or trendy style, you may not want running shoes. In fact, road running shoes can encourage more forward motion than you’ll want for more casual movements, and can strain parts of your foot. Walking-specific shoes support slower movement, and provide cushion that’s engineered more for standing all day rather than impact absorption from pavement-pounding. With slip-on versions available, walking shoes also tend to come in more accessible options for those with mobility re"},{"text":"s over several years, walking hundreds of miles on paved paths, dirt and gravel trails, and hard floors at work. We tested while running errands, wandering around town, hiking, going for walks, working 7–12-hour shifts at breweries and hospitals, walk commuting, and traveling. Our testers wore these shoes across all seasons and weather conditions, ranging from hot, dry summer days to cold, drizzly rain storms, icy winter mornings, and everything in between.\n\nOur testers each have different foot sizes and shapes, so we were able to test shoes with narrow, average, and wide feet.\n\nMost testing surfaces were on paved paths or in indoor spaces, but we also tested every pair on gravel surfaces to assess grip and whether small rocks would wedge themselves in the outsole tread. Additionally, we tested them side by side with other walking shoes, running shoes, and recovery shoes.\n\nHundreds of miles of walking\n\n7–12-hour work shifts\n\nPavement, gravel, and hard floors\n\nSide-by-side shoe comparisons\n\nNarrow, average, and wide feet\n\nCushioning and support\n\nFit and long-term comfort\n\nGrip on slick and loose surfaces\n\nCushion breakdown and hot spots\n\nVersatility across daily activities\n\nWhy you "},{"text":"sion for running into coaching, crewing, writing, creating training plans for her friends, hiking, snowshoeing, and running as much as her hip allows. She earned her MFA in Creative Writing from the Mile-High MFA Program at Regis University. Becca is based in Denver, Colorado.\n\nYou can find her at her website, Instagram, and her Treeline Review author page.\n\nWe tested women’s rain boots for waterproofing, traction, comfort, fit, and durability through downpours, muddy trails, farm work, and everyday walking.\n\nFind the perfect women’s walking shoes—comfortable, supportive, and durable options for daily walks, travel, fitness, and all-day wear.\n\nFind the perfect walking shoes—comfortable, supportive, and durable options for daily walks, fitness, and all-day wear."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-womens-winter-boots","version_id":"fe3351b24a28559fae53c196e3fc07fd88906b30031612f8aedfbe2d4c208fdb","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Winter Boots for Women of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-womens-winter-boots","published_at":"2026-10-05T17:41:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the updated hiking selections offer citable field experience transferable to Mammoth, including VECTIV testing on a four-day Maine winter trip spanning 15°F cold and 45°F rain, plus reported protection limitations when saturated. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require identifying Treeline’s affiliate commissions, weighing its praise accordingly despite its denial of brand sponsorship, and attributing findings to this single publisher without treating them as Mammoth load-carriage validation. This supports Piece 01’s newly reviewed options, Piece 02’s assessment of warmth and waterproofing claims, and Piece 03’s footwear and insulation tradeoffs under Necessary versus optional.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Winter Boots for Women of 2026\n\nThe Top Women’s Winter Boots for Warmth, Waterproofness, Fit, Comfort, and Ice Traction\n\nOctober 5th, 2026 with a new winner for best winter hiking boot, best insulated winter boot, best pull-on, best for walking, and most comfortable winter bootHome > Gear Reviews > Footwear\n\nWinter boots have a big job. They need to keep your feet warm and dry through snow, slush, ice, and cold without being so bulky or uncomfortable that you don’t want to wear them. I’ve spent winters in Alaska, Maine, New Hampshire, New York, and Colorado, and I’ve learned that the right winter boot depends as much on what you’re doing as how cold it is outside.\n\nWe’ve tested women’s winter boots in icy conditions, wet snow, deep snow, and extreme cold, using them for everything from hiking and snowshoeing to shoveling, walking the dog, running errands, and working outside. Some of these boots have stayed in our rotation for years, which has also given us a good sense of how they hold up over time.\n\nWe looked most closely at warmth, tread, waterproofness, fit, and shaft height. We also considered ankle and arch support, how well each boot worked with different socks, durabil"},{"text":"ity, and whether the boot made sense for its intended use.\n\nTop Pick: The SOREL Joan of Arctic Boots are our favorite overall. We’ve worn them through winters from Maine to Alaska and keep coming back to their combination of warmth, waterproofness, traction, durability, and style. They’re sturdy enough for snow and outdoor chores but still easy to wear around town, and the replaceable liner helps extend the life of the boot.\n\nLooking for men’s boots? See our Best Men’s Winter Boots guide.\n\nLooking for kids’ boots? See our Best Kids’ Snow Boots guide.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nWomen’s Winter Boots Comparison Table\n\n* Prices reflect MSRP and are subject to change. We note any discounts we see on our Deals page, which is updated daily.\n\nThe Best Women’s Winter Boots\n\nThe Best overall Women’s Winter Boots: SOREL Joan of Arctic Boots\n\nInsulation: 9 mm washa"},{"text":"'s winter boots for walking: L.L.Bean Women’s 8”Bean Boots\n\nInsulation: Available in: PrimaLoft, shearling liningShaft height: 8”Temperature rating: -30FWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Around town, styleWhat we liked: style, warmth, durability, made in US, variety of heights, colors, insulation, and sizes availableWhat we didn’t like: can be hard to get due to high demand, price\n\nIt’s a given; we couldn’t ignore the classic L.L.Bean Duck Boot. With a history dating back to 1912, these made in the USA boots can be backordered by the 100,000.\n\nThe Bean Boot has a classic look that works in urban settings or on a trail. It's comfortable enough for walking commutes and wearing all-day in the office.\n\nIt’s a look that has been mimicked by boot makers—from J.Crew on the fancy end to XTRATUF on the outdoorsy side. It’s not only comfortable and functional, but it has an aesthetic that works for a lot of settings—and we think that is a factor that makes it a winning boot.\n\nThere are a lot of contenders for the best winter boots for walking. But ultimately, we think the L.L. Bean Boot is a better value than most and is still made in the USA.\n\nFor more information, see our in-d"},{"text":" deeper snow in Maine and New Hampshire and appreciated the high lacing to really get the fit dialed in, especially with thicker socks.\n\nIn testing, we found the Oboz Bridger is also incredibly warm — with 400 grams of Freeze Guard insulation. With 3 mm thickness, that Freeze Guard insulation is more than enough insulation to keep even the coldest toes cozy. It’s also ringed by wool on the top. You may even find these boots too warm!\n\nOne potential ding against the Oboz Bridger is that it’s a stiffer boot than most hiking boots. (With the insulation, waterproof rubber, and waterproof nubuck leather, it’s not a crazy claim.) However, if ankle support is an important aspect of winter boots for you, the stiffness of the Oboz Bridger is actually a plus. They also provide great ankle support by lacing below and above the ankle — another perk of their height.\n\nWhile this wasn't our testers' experience, customer reviewers also warned that the Oboz Bridgers tend to run small, so sizing up a half size isn’t a bad idea, especially if you are wearing thick winter socks.\n\nBest Winter Hiking Boots: The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid Waterproof Hiking Shoe\n\nInsulation: 200 g Heatseeker"},{"text":" Synthetic InsulationShaft height: Mid (4-6”, depending on size)Temperature rating: Not stated, 10-20F in testingWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Hiking, winter backpacking, snowshoeingWhat we liked: Comfortable, lightweight, good groundfeel, thoughtful hiking-specific featuresWhat we didn’t like: When fully saturated, the midfoot and toe box protection was somewhat compromised, can be hard to find the women's version\n\nIf you want a lightweight hiking boot with insulation and waterproofing, the best winter hiking boots are The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated Mid. These boots are hands-down the favorite of hikers who prefer wearing mesh lightweight trail running shoes in the summer but find they need insulation and waterproofing in the winter.\n\nIn our testing, they keep you warm down to 10-20F, just as long as you are moving. They are designed for movement in snow and ice. These boots have some unique features, like a rocker midsole to smooth out the hiking gait, a TPU plate to enhance propulsion and protect the forefoot from rocks, a knob on the heel to keep snowshoe straps from sliding down, and pull tabs on both the tongue and heel to make it easy to pull the b"},{"text":"5-degree rainy day.\n\nMany hikers may prefer the stability and protection of the Oboz Bridger 9\" for hiking. However, if that is too much boot for you, the best winter boot for hiking is The North Face VECTIV Fastpack Insulated.\n\nBest Affordable Women’s Winter Boot: Columbia Ice Maiden II\n\nInsulation: 200 g insulation, microfleece liner, faux fur collarShaft height: 9” Temperature rating: -25FWaterproof: YesFootwear closure: LaceBest for: Around town, styleWhat we liked: style, price, enough warmth without being overkillWhat we didn’t like: not the boots for the coldest climates, could have better traction\n\nWant a warm winter boot that will keep out the snow without breaking the bank? Look no further than the Columbia Ice Maiden II. The Ice Maiden IIs can be found from $45 to 110, depending on which color you prefer. It’s one of the least expensive options anywhere—yet still managed to get good marks on all of our criteria for what to look for in a snow boot (see our standards below). There’s a reason why it’s a bestseller at Amazon for women’s mid-calf winter boots.\n\nThe price tag doesn’t compromise a lot. The Ice Maiden IIs have 200 grams of insulation, a microfleece liner, and a "},{"text":"able enough for all-day wear. We also found their waterproofing to be impressive. With a short break-in time and great flexibility right out of the box, you can wear your boots straight away.\n\nWe found their ankle support and stability insufficient for using as a winter hiking boot on more challenging terrain. They also aren’t as warm as most of the other boots we tested, excelling in fall or spring weather instead of winter.\n\nDanner’s commitment to using high-quality recycled components in their boots means you’ll enjoy them for a long while—and Danner also allows buyers to repair and replace worn-out soles, stitching, and leather.\n\nRead more in our Danner Boots Mountain 600 Leaf GTX review.\n\nWhat to look for in winter boots\n\nWarmth\n\nLook for boots that match your climate needs. Insulated boots with materials like wool or Thinsulate are ideal for very cold areas, while non-insulated options work for milder winters. If unsure, check our \"Should I Get Insulated or Non-Insulated Boots?\" section to find the right fit.\n\nAs the biggest differentiation between hiking boots and winter boots, we looked at reviews for the best insulated and non-insulated boots to make sure that your toes wo"},{"text":"ng more time in deep snow, sustained cold, or icy conditions, a dedicated winter boot usually gives you more insulation, a taller shaft, and winter-specific traction.\n\nFor actual winter hiking, we prefer boots with good ankle support, secure lacing, and enough tread for snow and ice.\n\nWhat socks should I wear with winter boots?\n\nWe like wool socks for winter because they add warmth without holding onto moisture the way cotton does. The right thickness depends on the boot and how much room you have inside it.\n\nWhen trying on winter boots, test them with more than one sock thickness. Your toes should still have some wiggle room, and your heel shouldn’t slide around. On warmer days, a thinner sock may be enough. When it gets colder, having room for a thicker wool sock gives you more flexibility.\n\nWhat are the warmest types of winter boots?\n\nThe warmest boots in this guide generally combine substantial insulation with a thick sole and enough height to keep snow out. Removable felt liners, neoprene, wool, and synthetic insulation can all work well depending on the design.\n\nIf your feet get cold easily or you spend long periods outside in very cold weather, start with our Best for Extrem"},{"text":" from Maine to Alaska. We review the best boots for warmth, water resistance, fit, comfort and traction\n\nWe researched the 100 most popular winter gloves, liner gloves, heated gloves, and mittens to find the best gloves for running, cycling, extreme cold, and touchscreen usage."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-winter-gloves","version_id":"78c32c47fb0757265f105588490347bcc10df722a863df51f98a13444e64e425","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Winter Gloves of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/best-winter-gloves","published_at":"2026-10-02T16:28:27Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this updated review supplies transferable cold-weather evidence, including three years of Alti II use, Nathan convertible-mitt testing at 15–50°F, and observed Achiou touchscreen limitations below about 20°F. Commercial incentives requires discounting praise because Treeline earns affiliate commissions, although the author disclaims brand sponsorship; Uncertainty requires attributing these observations to this single publisher rather than treating cited customer reviews as independent testing. It supports Piece 02’s assessment of performance claims and Piece 03’s warmth, dexterity, drying, bulk, and cost trade-offs, without establishing Mammoth gear requirements.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Winter Gloves of 2026\n\nWarm, thin, lightweight gloves for running, cycling, and extreme cold\n\nA good pair of winter gloves can make all the difference for all-season running, cycling, hiking, and snowplay. Photo by Robert Curzon.\n\nOctober 2nd, 2026, updated with two new winnersHome > Gear Reviews > Apparel\n\nWhen cold weather gets serious, your hands often feel it first. I’ve spent winters in Alaska, Colorado, and Maine, and I’ve worn winter gloves while skiing, hiking, saddling horses in snowstorms, digging vehicles out of snowbanks, and chasing Alaskan huskies across frozen lakes. I love being outside in winter, but only if I can still feel my hands.\n\nFor this guide, we tested winter gloves for everything from everyday chores and dog walks to running, cycling, skiing, hiking, and long days outside in the cold. We used them in snowstorms, icy conditions, wet snow, and deep cold to see which ones stayed warm and comfortable without making it impossible to actually use our hands.\n\nWe paid closest attention to warmth, weather protection, comfort, durability, dexterity, and fit. Depending on the glove, we also tested touchscreen use, cycling grip, and heated settings, because what"},{"text":" matters in a lightweight running glove is very different from what matters in a mitten made for extreme cold.\n\nTop Pick: The North Face Denali Etip Gloves are our favorite overall because they hit a useful middle ground between warmth, dexterity, durability, and price. They’re warm enough for many everyday winter activities without feeling bulky, and we found them easy to use for the kinds of tasks where you still need your fingers to work.\n\nIf you’re looking for ski and snowboard-specific gloves, check out our Best Ski Gloves and Best Snowboard Gloves and Mittens guides\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nWinter Gloves Comparison Table\n\n*We note any discounts we see on our Deals page, which is updated daily.\n\nFor more of our most popular Winter Gear Guide stories:\n\nBest Base Layers\n\nBest Women’s Winter Boots & Men’s Winter Boots\n\nBest Snowshoes\n\nBest Winter Tr"},{"text":"action Devices (Microspikes + Crampons)\n\nThe Best Winter Gloves for Men and Women\n\nBest Overall Winter Gloves: The North Face Denali Etip Gloves\n\nTouchscreen compatible? Yes, thumb and index fingerMaterial: Polyester fleeceInsulated: FleeceClosure: Zipper on back of hand What we liked: Warmth, durability, and dexterity make these an all-around glove for most usersWhat we didn’t like: Won’t be the warmest gloves available\n\nThe North Face Denali Etip Gloves earned our award two years in a row for Best Gloves for Winter Hiking and Running because of their warmth, dexterity, and touchscreen compatibility. They hit the sweet spot of warmth, price, durability, and dexterity, making them a good choice for most winter outdoor activities, and our best overall choice.\n\nThe author testing the North Face Etip Denali on winter chores. Photo courtesy Josette Deschambeault.\n\nThe North Face Denali Etip Gloves are mid-weight gloves made from fully recycled materials: Polyester (shell), nylon (back-of-hand overlay), polyester brushed tricot (palm lining), and TKA 100 polyester fleece (back-of-hand lining).\n\nThey’re warm and breathable, but not meant for extremely cold weather. Reviewers from Ireland"},{"text":"warmth, comfort, and dexterity—and affordable price.\n\nThe MCTi Waterproof Winter Gloves are made from windproof polyester, 3M Thinsulate insulation, a waterproof thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU) membrane, and 100% brushed polyester lining. Note that some reviewers mentioned the lengthy drying process–one reason why they didn’t win for best budget ski gloves. Still, if you don't plan to get your gloves wet, you'd be hard-pressed to find a glove this warm and functional at this price.\n\nThey also have rubber fingers for reinforced durability and a polyurethane coating on the palm. A zippered pocket on the back of the hand (big enough to fit keys, an ID, cash, or a disposable hand warmer) and adjustable wrist buckles are bonus features on an already-stellar glove.\n\nWhile we ultimately picked the MCTi Waterproof Winter Gloves for our Best Waterproof and Windproof Gloves, the Give’r 4 Season Gloves were a close second and admittedly are much more stylish. The Give'r received great reviews from REI users and external media alike–and we really enjoyed wearing them all winter in Maine.\n\nBut at the end of the day, the MCTi Gloves were ⅓ of the price of the Give’r 4 Seasons.\n\nThere were a few"},{"text":"from sweating, still warm gloves without the heating elementWhat we didn’t like: The price barrier is high, but for those who need the warmth, it can be worth it. Some users encounter fit issues.\n\nFor many people, heated gloves may be overkill. But for extremely cold temperatures (or people with Raynaud's), the Outdoor Research Prevail gloves are the key to staying warm and happy out in the snow.\n\nWe chose the OR Prevail Heated GORE-TEX Gloves as our best pick for Heated Gloves and Raynaud’s (we also chose it in our Best Ski Gloves Guide!) because of the customizable heat settings, waterproofness, windproofness, breathability, rechargeable batteries, battery life, and warmth without the heaters on. (Not to mention, they’re touchscreen compatible!)\n\nOutdoor Research advises consulting a doctor before using these gloves if you have difficulty sensing hot or cold temperatures, including because of Raynaud’s. Outdoor Research also warns against using these heated gloves with avalanche beacons because they can interfere with beacon performance.\n\nOne of our testers, notorious for piling on layers when everyone else is stripping them off on the uphill, was skeptical about testing heated g"},{"text":"ures. But we think for most people building up some heat from working in the yard, snowshoeing or any number of other outdoor activities, they provide the right amount of warmth without causing your hands to sweat.\n\nIf you’re more of a mittens fan than you are of gloves, Hestra also makes a 3-finger mitten, as well as a traditional mitten.\n\nWe were happy about the Fall Lines’ warmth and durability. They excel at dexterity, like when zipping up a winter jacket. Some testers raised the point that the gloves were very warm during activity, but less so with inactivity (think hiking versus walking around town).\n\nBest Gloves For Running: Nathan Hypernight Reflective Convertible Mitt\n\nTouchscreen Compatible? Yes, index fingersMaterial: 88% Polyester 12% Spandex Pro stretchInsulated: NoClosure: Elastic cuffsWhat we liked: Versatile–warm as mitt and cooling as gloves, reflective, touchscreen compatibleWhat we didn’t like: Not best for extreme cold\n\nThe Nathan HyperNight Reflective Convertible Mitt is the ideal running glove for cold-weather runners who prefer the warmth and protection of a mitten and the dexterity of a glove. With a pull-over mitten option, you can choose your own adventure"},{"text":" and adapt the gloves to fit your needs. Our tester for these gloves was Treeline writer and editor Becca Downs, a long-time competitive runner and distance running coach. She is prone to particularly cold hands, and tested these gloves in fall and winter in Indiana and Colorado. She claims these are the best running gloves she’s ever worn.\n\nThe Nathan HyperNight Reflective Convertible Mitt can be worn as gloves or mittens, and has a reflective design for nighttime visibility.\n\nThe gloves are 88% Polyester 12% Spandex Pro stretch and are wind-resistant. They have grips in the palms, as well as touchscreen-compatible fingertips. Unlike many winter gloves that advertise they are touchscreen-compatible, these actually work really well and are helpful for using your phone without taking your gloves off. There's also a “nose wipe chamois” on the thumbs, which we find delightfully in tune with many runners’ needs.\n\nThe reflective elements are useful for folks who run/walk in the dark and wish to increase their visibility. The HyperNight gloves come in black with the reflective print and in hi-vis yellow with the same reflective print. On top of that, Nathan added a small pocket to the ba"},{"text":"ne gloves in mild winter weather, but they offer less protection from wind and water than weatherproof shells. They also work well as liners under warmer gloves.\n\nChoosing the right shell material depends on your primary activities, weather conditions, and how much upkeep you’re willing to do. Leather offers rugged durability with some maintenance, while nylon provides lightweight and often more convenient weather protection for those on the go.\n\nRemovable Liners\n\nRemovable liners, and creating a glove system comprised of several pairs of gloves allow for easier drying and the ability to bring only what you need for a particular trip. Using a pair of gloves that are made up of a liner, insulation layer, and shell definitely makes keeping up with your gloves easier when on an outing since it’s a single pair, but you will lose some versatility and won’t be able to vent and add warmth as you would with a removable liner or gloves quiver/systems approach. One consideration when looking to purchase several gloves, however, is that it requires some experimentation and special sizing considerations since the outer shell must be a little bigger than the removable liner.\n\nTreeline Review wr"},{"text":"elf) is sponsored, an ambassador, or an employee of any of the companies mentioned here. This is unbiased, and we do this to help you find the best product for the price. Treeline Review doesn’t accept sponsored content, native advertising, or paid reviews.\n\nSee all of Josette's articles on her author page.\n\nTested on ice and snow from Maine to Alaska for 5 years, here’s our review of the best men’s winter boots for warmth, water resistance, fit, comfort, and traction.\n\nWe spent 8 years testing the top women’s winter boots for ice and snow from Maine to Alaska. We review the best boots for warmth, water resistance, fit, comfort and traction\n\nWe researched the 100 most popular winter gloves, liner gloves, heated gloves, and mittens to find the best gloves for running, cycling, extreme cold, and touchscreen usage."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/black-diamond-distance-15-backpack-review","version_id":"96ace18837c1052eabb221014b6a89ac63f1a3ca56fe5348c2ac0eb46386b686","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"I tested the Black Diamond Distance 15  Backpack while running mountains across Colorado","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/black-diamond-distance-15-backpack-review","published_at":"2026-10-01T23:21:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this review adds months of alpine field use, roughly 200 miles per variant, observed abrasion resistance, and specific drawbacks with 4+ liters of water and bladder access that inform Mammoth pack choices. Commercial incentives requires discounting praise because Treeline earns affiliate commissions; under Uncertainty and Necessary versus optional, this single review supports attributed durability observations and capacity tradeoffs, but does not establish suitability for a full Mammoth load or event requirements. It supports Piece 01's newly reviewed gear, Piece 02's field performance assessment, and Piece 03's carrying tradeoffs; purchase links must be omitted under Exclusions.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"I tested the Black Diamond Distance 15 Backpack while running mountains across Colorado\n\nA 15-liter capacity running pack with vest-style straps for trail running, hiking, scrambling, and other long days on the trail\n\nOctober 1st, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Running\n\nOur verdict\n\nThe Black Diamond Distance 15 running backpack is a high-volume running pack for running, hiking, and ultralight fastpacking missions. If you need to carry more than a traditional 6-10 liter running vest but want the same vest strap design, the Distance 15 is the way to go.\n\nI tested the Distance 15 in the standard Black Diamond version—made with recycled nylon ripstop—as well as the norda collab version, made with Ultra 100 Ecopak material. I used each version while mountain running, peak bagging, and hiking on high-alpine trails in Colorado. These two versions are the same except for the exterior material. The norda collab version costs $100 more than the standard version.\n\nThe Distance 15 handles heavier loads and bulkier gear—think a puffy jacket, emergency bivy, lunch, running poles, extra water, or all of the above plus more. The vest straps allow you to access everything you need without taking the pa"},{"text":"ck off. Each strap has three pockets, plus a fourth zippered pocket on the left strap for a smartphone. The back of the pack also has drop-in pockets for folding running poles. It doesn’t come with any hydration bottles or a bladder, though it is compatible with such items.\n\nBlack Diamond Distance 15 Backpack\n\nThe biggest drawback of the Distance 15 pack is that, other than the two drop-in pockets for running poles, it doesn’t have many exterior pockets on the backside. I can fit both of my Black Diamond FKT poles into one of the drop-in pockets, though. So I use the other drop-in pocket for a jacket. If you have running or trekking poles with wider handles, though, you’ll have to open the cinch top to grab your jacket or other items you have stashed inside.\n\nThis is one of my favorite packs for long days in the mountains. If you’re looking for a higher-volume running pack for long days on the trail, scrambling, peak bagging, and mountain running, the Distance 15 is a great option.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gea"},{"text":"r, we may get a commission, without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nBlack Diamond Distance 15 specifications\n\nPrice: $200\n\nVolume: 15 L\n\nWeight: 11.6 oz (men’s), 11.1 oz (women’s)\n\nStrap type: Running vest straps with two sternum straps\n\nBack panel: 3D mesh\n\nHip belt: None\n\nClosure: Cinch closure with clip\n\nWater carry: Two 500 mL soft flask pockets, hydration sleeve (flasks and bladder not included)\n\nShoulder strap pockets: Four pockets per strap\n\nExterior pockets: Two drop-in pockets for folding running poles\n\nBottom pocket: No\n\nWaterproof: Water-resistant materials without taped seams\n\nMain body material: 87% recycled nylon, 7% polyethylene, 6% recycled polyester\n\nPocket mesh material: Diamond stretch mesh shoulder strap pockets\n\nVariants available: Black Diamond x norda collab 15 L version\n\nMade in: Philippines\n\nComparison table\n\nBest for\n\nRunning and hiking long distances\n\nPeak bagging and mountain running\n\nDesert running and fast hiking with longer water carries\n\nMountain biking and bikepacking\n\nOff-trail alpine running and hiking\n\nWhat we liked\n\nGreat vest pocket design that makes all y"},{"text":"our gear easy to access without stopping\n\nCarries decent water loads plus gear well\n\nFits tight to the body so it doesn’t bounce when moving fast with lighter loads\n\nShoulder straps are comfortable after 10 hours on the trail\n\nIt feels locked onto your back when running and scrambling\n\nWhat could be better\n\nExternal drop-in pockets for running poles are the only pockets on the back\n\nHow we tested\n\nI tested the men’s/unisex Black Diamond Distance 15 running backpack for several months during the summer in both the standard nylon ripstop version and the norda collab version. I wore it while running, fast hiking, scrambling, and on adventures that mixed running, hiking, and scrambling.\n\nI tested the Distance 15 while running and hiking in the mountains of Colorado all summer. I summited several 14,000-foot mountains, ran trails through Colorado’s Sawatch range, and also used it as a daypack for hikes and bike rides. On any outing where I would be on the trail for more than 3 hours, especially above treeline, I carried the Distance 15 because it holds more layers in case of an emergency. I often wore a puffy jacket I packed in this high-volume running backpack while I ate lunch, which "},{"text":"I also carried to the top of the mountain inside the pack.\n\nI am 5’9”, 165 pounds, with a 37-inch chest. I tested the Distance 15 in size medium. A size medium fits chests between 31.5 and 39.5 inches, but I definitely wouldn’t get a large, even though I’m near the top end of the medium’s range. When running downhill with a lot of food, water, or layers, and using all 15 liters of internal volume, I can cinch the vest straps down enough so the pack doesn’t bounce, and I wouldn’t be able to do that with a size large.\n\nSo far, I’ve used this pack for over 200 miles, with a fair amount of bushwhacking, off-trail travel, and scrambling. They both look almost new, though with some dirt and salt. I also tested it alongside other similar models in our Best Fastpacking Backpacks, Best Running Backpacks, and Best Running Vests guides.\n\nPerformance in the field\n\nCarry comfort and load stability\n\nThe Black Diamond Distance 15 pack feels comfortable when you’re running. When loaded to its volume capacity, the shoulder straps distribute weight across your shoulders and chest, so they stay comfortable all day. If you have it loaded with a lot of water, it isn’t as comfortable. However, I wouldn’"},{"text":"n along with the standard ripstop version.\n\nThe vest straps have two flat sternum straps you can tighten or loosen with ladder-lock buckles. The bottom sternum strap is elastic, which helps keep the pack comfortable while securely locking it onto your shoulders and back. The top strap is thin and soft nylon webbing. You can overtighten this strap since it doesn’t stretch, but I never found that to be a problem.\n\nPackability and compression\n\nThe Distance 15 has a zig-zag cord along both sides that lets you compress the pack when you don’t have it stuffed full.\n\nThe top closes with a cinch cord. On top of the cinch closure, you hook the top of the main pack body to a piece of flat elastic with a g-hook. The system lets you compress the top of the pack to keep the load lower, which makes it feel more secure and less likely to bounce around behind your head.\n\nDurability\n\nThe Distance 15 is a very durable pack. I’ve used the nylon ripstop and the 100D Challenge Ultra (UltraWeave) version each for roughly 200 miles so far, running, hiking, and scrambling in alpine conditions, often scraping the pack against rocks as I edge along cliffs, and it still looks almost new.\n\nI tested the previo"},{"text":"r fastpacking backpacks guide. It costs $50 more than the Black Diamond Distance 15 and weighs about the same amount. Overall, we like both the Distance and Pemi a lot because they occupy the “Goldilocks” zone between smaller-volume running vests and larger-volume fastpacks. They’re both extremely durable, comfortable, and capable of carrying larger loads for mountain running, winter trail running, and scrambling.\n\nThe Pemi and Distance packs have a very similar vest pocket design, but the Pemi has a side-access zipper pocket on both shoulder straps, while the Distance has a zippered pocket only on the left shoulder strap. The Pemi’s main compartment uses a roll-top design and has a vertical zippered pocket on the side of the pack that lets you access the main compartment without undoing the roll-top closure.\n\nThe Pemi also has an external UHMWPE stretch mesh pocket on the back of the pack for stuffing items you want to access quickly, such as a rain jacket. The Pemi also has an external water bladder sleeve, so you can remove and reinsert a bladder without unpacking everything in the main compartment.\n\nThe Pemi is made with ALUULA waterproof fabric and thermo-welded seams, so it’s"},{"text":" very water resistant. This ALUULA fabric used on the Pemi’s main body is very similar to the 100D Challenge Ultra used on the Black Diamond-norda collab version of the Distance. The Pemi costs $50 less than the Black Diamond-norda collab version of the Distance.\n\nWe like the Distance and Pemi a lot, and think you’d be happy with either one. You can read our full review of the Hyperlite Mountain Gear Pemi 15 running vest to learn more.\n\nBlack Diamond Distance 15 vs. Ultimate Direction Fastpack/Fastpackher 20\n\nMSRP: $165Capacity: 23.4 L men’s, 23.0 L women’sIncluded hydration: NoneWomen's-specific fit available: Weight: 20.8 oz men’s, 19.8 oz women’s\n\nThe Ultimate Direction Fastpack is another pack that’s similar to the Black Diamond Distance 15. The UD Fastpack is a ~20-liter pack, compared to the Distance, which can hold 15 liters of gear in the size we tested (though the Distance also comes in 22L and 8L versions). The UD Fastpack is available in women’s and men’s sizes, comes in 30-liter and 40-liter versions, and is the overall winner in our running backpacks guide.\n\nThe Fastpack and Distance have a similar pocket layout on the shoulder straps. The Fastpack has a stretch mesh p"},{"text":"s for hiking, camping, and everyday wear. Compare six tested picks for warmth, fit, weather resistance, and packability.\n\nWe tested women’s rain boots for waterproofing, traction, comfort, fit, and durability through downpours, muddy trails, farm work, and everyday walking."}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/tract-toric-lrf-rangefinding-binocular-review/","version_id":"f5f9336208c79200bc12241e1c1bfff993bc13dc61585ea3b1dc620c43de5f46","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"rokslide","title":"Tract Toric LRF Rangefinding Binocular Review","url":"https://www.rokslide.com/tract-toric-lrf-rangefinding-binocular-review/","published_at":"2026-10-10T13:18:20Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Backcountry hunter reviews: rangefinding binoculars, packs, optics. Ad supported."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this newly reviewed rangefinding binocular provides transferable field evidence: measured weight of 2 lb. 5.5 oz. with a stud, rain and fog ranging results, comparative ballistic outputs, and reported diopter movement; it supports Pieces 01, 02, and 03 on features, tested performance, and carry-weight tradeoffs. Under Commercial incentives, the publisher is ad supported and the review includes an availability link, but product provision, payment, and affiliate status are undisclosed; under Uncertainty, its findings remain one practitioner's evidence, with long-term durability explicitly unresolved.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"More and more electro-optics are coming to market for the western hunter. Multiple use is the name of the game, and a single device that replaces two or more makes all the more sense to hunters that are already stuffing their pack with gear.\n\nThis is why range-finding binoculars make so much sense. They eliminate the need for a separate binocular and laser rangefinder, and add the convenience of having ranges available immediately. Add in onboard environmental sensors, inclinometers, compasses, and advanced ballistic solvers, and you are really eliminating some gear.\n\nSounds like a no-brainer, right? The issue has always been that there are required compromises when bringing optics, lasers, and displays together. Early versions were noticeably poor optically. So much so, many hunters returned to separate binoculars and rangefinders.\n\nFlagship RF binos from the big European companies of Zeiss, Leica, and Swarovski largely mitigated the issue with their recent models, and now hunters have the luxury of having top-shelf optics and ballistic rangefinding in one package. The rub on those? Well, that’s the usual one: they cost as much as that high-mileage used car you’ve been looking at "},{"text":"for your teen driver.\n\nOther companies have brought to market more budget-friendly options. Generally, these mid-tier offerings have excellent rangefinding and advanced ballistics–but pair that up with optical performance that, while improved from the 1st generation, still falls behind better mid-priced dedicated binoculars in optical performance.\n\nThe Jons (Jon Allen and Jon LaCorte) of Tract Optics intended to shake up that status quo with the introduction of the Tract Toric LRF 10×42 Rangefinding binocular by improving optical performance and combining it with an advanced laser and computations from Eagle Ballistics.\n\nAt just under 6 inches long, the Toric LRF is more than a half inch shorter than the Zeiss Victory RF’s 7.6 inches. The Toric LRF is compact, but not exactly lightweight. The Toric LRF tips the scale at 2 lbs. 5.5 ounces with an Outdoorsman’s stud attached. The physically larger Victory RF (also with an Outdoorsman’s stud) is actually lighter at 2 lbs. 3 ounces.\n\nThe Toric LRF is fully rubber armored with non-removable 4-position twist-out eyecups. Push-pull “locking” diopter rings are on each barrel. The left barrel adjusts the optic, and the right adjusts the dis"},{"text":"play. The locking feature is rather loose, and I did have one occasion where the diopter had shifted out and moved slightly, so that is something for the hunter to keep an eye on. Additionally, adjusting the screen will affect the optic diopter, so make sure to adjust the screen first, and then move to the left barrel. One note on this. I normally have diopters close to the “0” setting on most optics. In order to get the screen sharp, I had to adjust the screen diopter pretty far to the “-“ side. Accordingly, I had to adjust the left diopter far “-“ as well, and ended up near the far limit of adjustment. Still, after adjustment, I ended up with a sharp, easy observation view and crisp display.\n\nThe Toric LRF is powered by a single CR2 battery that is housed within the focus wheel.\n\nThis conveniently allows the front hinge to accommodate standard 1/4-20 adapters. I immediately mounted a Outdoorsman’s binoculars adapter stud and left it on indefinitely..\n\nThe right side of the Toric LRF has a single rubberized button marked “FIRE.” The button is raised slightly from the surrounding armor and has stippling around the text. The left side has four buttons. Opposite the FIRE button is a "},{"text":"it had not moved. I did a 10-hour cold soak in the freezer with the battery removed. I pulled the frosty Toric LRF out of the freezer, put in the battery, uncapped the lenses, and immediately received instant ranges with ballistic information. Of course, like any frozen optic, it did not take long for the exterior lenses to ice over in the warmer air, naturally interfering with observation and ranging, but I could see the display operating within the right barrel. As the Toric LRF equalized with the air temperature, everything returned to proper working order.\n\nThe Toric LRF has a suitably wide FOV at 330 ft. at 1000 yards. This is similar to traditional “wide field” 10×42 roof prism binoculars, but falls just short of the European competition like the Zeiss at 345 ft. at 1000 yards.\n\nApparent sharpness is excellent, and I could resolve the same element on the chart as I could with the Zeiss Victory RF in close-range chart testing. This wasn’t too surprising as both optics have a relatively low 10x magnification, but I have seen lower apparent resolution on some other mid-tier RF models from other manufacturers, so this was a good sign for the Toric LRF.\n\nColor bias and transmissio"},{"text":"very end of twilight, when the Zeiss did appear a touch brighter. Still, the Toric LRF had very good performance for an RF binocular.\n\nDistortion is very well controlled in the Toric LRF. There is a low amount of pincushion distortion, which is almost identical to that of the Victory RF\n\nAt the edge, fringing is easily visible and more intense than the Zeiss.\n\nThe Tract Toric LRF has the best optics I have tested in a non-Euro RF binocular. I would gladly use the Toric LRF as my primary optic, and it pushes the very best competition in performance, even if it doesn’t quite match them.\n\nThe rangefinding capabilities of the Toric LRF are what really stand out with this device. The on-board sensors include temperature, pressure, inclination, and compass bearing. The device syncs via Bluetooth with the Eagle Ballistics app. The device can store five profiles at a time. The ballistic display can be customized to display environmentals, earthbase effects (sic), bullet flight, windage and elevation, gun profile, and startup logo. The hunter can switch on and off any of those options.\n\nThe Toric LRF reticle is customizable with circle, cross, oval, 1 MOA dot, and 2 MOA dot options.\n\nOne of"},{"text":" the best features is that the user can actually “sight in” the laser to co-witness the reticle to the location the laser is actually hitting. Most rangefinders I have used don’t hit exactly in the reticle, and I have always just tested to find where they are actually hitting relative to the reticle (the Zeiss is on the left side of the circle). With the Tract, the hunter can precisely align the reticle. I did just that, and liked using the scan mode on a road reflector to dial it in. The user can adjust up to 0.5 mil in each direction.\n\nTract has several options for entering environmental data. You can select to use the Toric LRF’s on-board sensors, manually input data, or connect via Bluetooth to the Eagle Ballistic App and have the App do the calculations using the closest weather station data.\n\nThere is a bow mode that has a simple display result and can be set to horizontal or line-of-sight distance.\n\nTarget options include best, near, far, rain/snow, and extended. The best, near, and far return nearly instantaneous results while the other modes have a short delay. The rain/snow setting works well in those conditions but does not help in thick fog/clouds. In those conditions I"},{"text":"-8 motion for a few seconds.\n\nNeed more wind info? Just let the app do the ballistics, and pull range data from the Toric LRF and wind data from a connected wind meter. The phone app is a necessary go-between for this functionality.\n\nRanging performance is simply outstanding. The Toric LRF gives reliable ranges on trees almost instantaneously in Best target mode out to 3000 yards plus in most conditions. Reflective targets like buildings and shining rock faces can go even further. Ballistic data shows up immediately. The ability to sight in the laser and use a tiny MOA dot makes ranging in flat terrain much more reliable than other rangefinders. The overall range-finding performance is superior to the much more expensive Zeiss Victory RF.\n\nRanging in thick clouds, I could get ranges with targets partially obscured by fog, but not when mostly obscured. In rainy conditions, the rain/snow setting did help get ranges out to just under 1000 yards.\n\nThe ballistic engine used in the Toric LRF was developed and patented by James Eagleman 1SG/MSG (RET). Inputs are similar to other ballistic apps, and data is pushed to the Toric LRF by hitting the circular arrows icon at the top of the app p"},{"text":"ices like a kestrel wind meter to get the best solutions.\n\nI used the Tract Toric LRF in the field for scouting, shooting, shed hunting, and bow hunting for elk. The minor optical issues I identified above were not a concern in field use. The optical performance was very close to the European competition in actual field use. Despite the small color variation between barrels, the Toric LRF provides a very comfortable viewing experience with no eye strain after prolonged use.\n\nThe display is excellent, the laser is powerful, and the returns are nearly instantaneous. Simple 1/4-20 mounting of tripod studs makes tripod glassing a breeze and stabilizes long-distance ranging attempts. Here is a video demonstrating ranging speed and performance:\n\nAs far as ergonomics go, the Toric LRF is kind of a “brick” binocular. I got along with it fine, but I needed the inter-pupillary distance a little closer than I would like to get the perfect view of the display. The eyecups are decent, and I suspect they will fit most faces well enough. I preferred the ergonomics of both the Zeiss Victory RF and the Leica Geovid Max, but my friend who has used original Swarovski SLCs forever really liked the fee"},{"text":" level. This is an excellent tool for hunters. The optics are very, very good, and just behind the Euro leaders. The rangefinder is as good as anything available. The Toric LRF’s ballistic rangefinding capabilities are more extensive and precise than even those from Zeiss and Swarovski’s top RF offerings, while being highly competitive with the brand-new Leica Geovid MAX, which costs DOUBLE the Toric LRF.\n\nEagle Ballistics is a full-featured app and on-device ballistics engine that integrates with the Toric LRF effortlessly.\n\nConsidering the price and performance, the Toric LRF is easily one of the most impressive optics I have seen in recent years.\n\nI need more time with the Toric LRF to really evaluate the long-term durability, but it is comforting to know Tract covers the Toric LRF with the “Tract Trust Assurance” warranty, which is their fully transferable lifetime warranty that covers both the optics AND electronics. Not many electro-optics are covered so thoroughly. Available here.\n\nComment or ask Matt questions here.\n\nReview: Swarovski Z6 2.5-15X44 BT\n\nThirty Days to One Thousand Yards\n\nMeopta Optika LR VS Leica Geovid R Binoculars"}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/","version_id":"b0b93fbafa2a8e20d13c937043012a7e5b20a997a84e44c5720dfe4daef470ea","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"rokslide","title":"KUIU Icon 6 3500 Backpack Review","url":"https://www.rokslide.com/kuiu-icon-6-3500-backpack-review/","published_at":"2026-09-23T12:00:06Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Backcountry hunter reviews: rangefinding binoculars, packs, optics. Ad supported."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this new pack review offers transferable evidence: comparative 1.2-mile loops with 28- and 92-pound loads, 510 feet of elevation gain and loss, and a measured equipped weight of 5 pounds 12.5 ounces. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require noting Rokslide's advertising support, that product provision or brand compensation is unspecified, and that one practitioner's short-loop comfort findings do not establish multi-day durability; the findings support Pieces 01, 02, and 03 on new design changes, manufacturer claims, and carrying-weight tradeoffs.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Home\n\nRokstaff\n\nForums\n\nRok Blog\n\nGearArchery\n\nBackpacks\n\nClothing\n\nEssentials\n\nFirearms\n\nFootwear\n\nMuzzleloader\n\nOptics\n\nSleep Systems\n\nStoves\n\nTips & TacticsAntelope\n\nArchery\n\nBackpacking\n\nBear\n\nElk\n\nFirearms\n\nGeneral\n\nHorse Packin’\n\nHunting Tips\n\nMountain Goat\n\nMule Deer\n\nShed Hunting\n\nSheep\n\nTURKEY\n\nVideos\n\nPodcast\n\nLoad Dev\n\nStore\n\nForum\n\nAdvertisers\n\nContact\n\nStore\n\nHome\n\nRokstaff\n\nForums\n\nRok Blog\n\nGearAllArcheryBackpacksClothingEssentialsFirearmsFootwearMuzzleloaderOpticsSleep SystemsStoves OpticsTract Toric LRF Rangefinding Binocular Review\n\nGearGear Reviews: Lathrop Super Wides, Peak Refuel, KUIU Icon 6, and…\n\nBackpacksKUIU Icon 6 3500 Backpack Review\n\nOpticsHow To Choose a Thermal Scope\n\nTips & TacticsAllAntelopeArcheryBackpackingBearElkFirearmsGeneralHorse Packin’Hunting TipsMountain GoatMule DeerShed HuntingSheepTURKEY ElkRifle Elk Hunting 201: Finding Bulls Everyone Else Misses with Cody…\n\nGearGear Reviews: Lathrop Super Wides, Peak Refuel, KUIU Icon 6, and…\n\nElkStories from Western Hunting Legend Jim Zumbo\n\nFirearmsHow AI Is Helping Hunters Score Bucks Faster and Smarter\n\nVideos Sleep SystemsTentipi Safir 7P Canvas vs. Sil-Nylon Review\n\nArchery2023 Head to Head Bow T"},{"text":"est: Part 2\n\nBackpacksStone Glacier Terminus Review\n\nArcheryHead to Head Bow Test: Part I\n\nMule DeerThe Best of Hunting Big Mule Deer Trailer\n\nPodcast\n\nLoad Dev\n\nStore\n\nA backcountry hunter’s backpack is an indispensable tool. It serves as pantry, dresser, optics case, first aid cabinet, seat, and so many other things. The best hunting backpacks do all of those things, but also haul that precious cargo of meat out after the tags are punched.\n\nWay back in 2011, I used the original KUIU Icon frame and bag and appreciated the quality craftsmanship and clever organization. The carbon frame was innovative and did a good job with moderate loads. Under really heavy loads, though, it got overwhelmed, and some hunters had durability issues, including frames cracking and breaking. KUIU went through more iterations and improvements, always preserving their organizational prowess but improving fit and finish. Performance did improve, and many hunters really found the newer Icon Pro and Pro LT to be a good fit for their hunts. I liked the weight and function of the newer packs, but still felt heavy loads got too uncomfortable, and the suspension just didn’t quite fit me right.\n\nSignificantly im"},{"text":"prove heavy-weight carrying capacity and comfort.\n\nSimplify the overall design while dramatically improving performance.\n\nTo reach these goals, KUIU started from scratch, which means the new Icon 6 packs and frames are not backward compatible with the older Icons, including the Pro and Pro LT models.\n\nIt all starts with the new patent-pending frame, which combines a “corrugated carbon architecture” made of thermoplastic carbon fiber. The new frame is taller and wider than previous KUIU frames and is specifically designed to flex where it is needed and stay stiff for load-bearing. The new frame promises increased strength and durability as well as improved “damage tolerance.” KUIU is confident the new frame satisfies design goal #1.\n\nThe suspension fits through hook-and-loop attachment and offers excellent adjustability in both length and angle. The hip belt and shoulder straps have been redesigned in shape and materials, with new foam mapped to be thinner and thicker depending on anatomic need. A thick lumbar pad matches a new wider hip belt.\n\nThe available pack bags include the tested 3500 CI daypack, a versatile 4500 CI option, a multi-day 6000 CI freighter, and an expedition-rea"},{"text":"dy 8200 CI behemoth. Quality 330 denier Cordura and Waterproof Cordura are the primary fabrics used in the construction of the packs. My 3500 test pack is well made with quality stitching and materials throughout. Packs attach to the frame simply by placing the packbag’s hypalon stay pockets over the top stays and tensioning by way of two hook-and-loop straps under the frame by the lumbar pad.\n\nDesign goal #2 was design simplicity. This goal speaks to me because some of my favorite backpacks have high performance and function, but take a lot of time and effort to do things like use the load shelf or switch bags. Here is a huge win for KUIU, as this is one of the simplest designs in a hunting pack.\n\nTo use the load sling the hunter just needs to unbuckle the compression strap bucks, detach the bag attachment straps and lift the hypalon frame stay pockets off the upper frame stays. The top straps will squeeze through the frame stay pockets without removing the buckles from the straps. Switching pack bags is a breeze. The Icon 6 doesn’t have load stabilizer straps, so there is nothing to unthread when removing the pack, attaching the pack, or loading the sling.\n\nIn typical KUIU fashio"},{"text":"h daypack loads is awesome, but obviously it is not meant for hauling meat.\n\nThe previous generation pack-on-frame Icons were certainly capable of hauling just about anything I could strap to them. However, they just didn’t carry those loads as comfortably as some of the top performers. For me, two of those top performers are the Stone Glacier XCurve frame (with Sky 5900 pack) and the Kifaru ARK frame (with Bedlam pack). For this review, I made 1.2-mile loops (510 vertical feet gained and lost) with each pack loaded with 28 pounds of day hunting gear, followed by re-hiking the same loop with an additional 64 pounds of blasting media in a game bag to simulate an elk quarter. Each loop included portions on trail, off trail, and through deadfall.\n\nWith the packs loaded with day gear, it was difficult to really find the difference in comfort. I had zero belt slippage, no hot spots, and no shoulder discomfort with any of these three pack systems. The ARK frame was the stiffest, while the Icon 6 flexed a little more with the body, and the Xcurve was pretty laterally compliant.\n\nOf course, I fully expected bigger differences to emerge during the heavy load loops. I was pleasantly surprise"},{"text":"d by the KUIU Icon 6 in this testing. The Icon 6 belt is wider than previous Icon belts, and at 5.75 inches at its widest, it is about 0.25 inches wider than the Xcurve belt but still narrower than the 7.5-inch ARK belt. The load weight was evenly distributed around the Icon 6 belt, and the load lifters sufficiently pulled the load into a nice balance. During the loop, I cranked the belt tight and tensioned the shoulder straps just enough. The result was a very stable load that doesn’t make the hunter lean too far forward. Keeping with the simple ethos of the Icon 6, there are no “stabilizing straps” connecting the bottom of the pack to the belt, but they just weren’t needed. The new wider frame just snugs the pack to the frame solidly, with or without the load sling loaded.\n\nThese are some of the best frames available to hunters, and as such none of them caused me any discomfort on the hips or lumbar area during the heavy packout load testing. Like the daypack loads, the packout performance was pretty similar. The Icon 6 was dramatically better than prior Icon frames I have used. Earlier designs caused substantial hip/lumbar abrasion and tenderness, as well as shoulder pain and fa"},{"text":"tigue with heavy loads. It didn’t matter how I tried to adjust the suspension. The Icon 6 completely remedied this for me, and brings the Icon 6 on the level with the very best frames available. Compared to the Xcurve, the Icon 6 had a much stiffer feel, but still flexed a bit when crawling over downed logs. The ARK is stiffer laterally, and … while still very comfortable … has more of a packboard-type feel. As a personal preference, I preferred the feel of the Icon 6 and ARK to the Xcurve in the heavy load testing.\n\nThe Icon 6 comes in two frame sizes. The Regular frame is 24.5 inches and the Tall frame is 26.5 inches. There are specific men’s and women’s suspensions available for these frames. My test Regular frame just under 10 inches wide and lumbar pad and top stays.\n\nI weighed the Icon 6 3500 with all the accessories I would actually use including both hip belt pockets and the Pack Load Hauler. The real world empty weight was 5 lbs. 12.5 ounces. This is in line with the Stone Glacier Sky 5900 (with belt pocket, lid, and water bottle holder) at 5 lbs. 13 ounces. The Kifaru ARK/Bedlam tips the scales over a pound heavier at exactly 7 lbs with a medium belt pocket and water bott"},{"text":"le holder.\n\nThe Icon 6 3500 base system including accessories comes in at $599. This pricing puts it squarely in the premium pack market but lower than top competitors. The Stone Glacier Sky 5900 is $725 without any accessories. The Kifaru ARK with the Bedlam runs a base price of $850. The Icon 6 is a downright bargain in the premium pack area especially when accounting for the included accessories.\n\nKUIU has elevated their Icon backpack line with their new Icon 6 frame and bags. The expressed design goals of improved heavy load comfort and simplified use have been satisfied. They have managed to maintain the low weights and organization they are known for while bringing the load-carrying performance and comfort up to compete with the very best in the market. It can not be overstated just how welcome and convenient a simple and intuitive design is for changing bags and converting for load hauling.\n\nEverything isn’t perfect with the Icon 6. The all-new design of the Icon 6 leaves existing Icon/Icon LT users with an incompatible system. I don’t quite get the daisy chain straps, and the Pack Load Hauler is finicky to get on the straps. Even so, there is no denying the Icon 6 system is"},{"text":"Store\n\nSeek Outside Kenai 5400 Review\n\nBuying A Backpack\n\nKifaru’s Extended Mission Ruck II (EMR II)"}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.ssusa.org/content/new-zerotech-trace-x-5-25x-56-mm-ffp-rmg-h-mrad-illuminated-riflescope/","version_id":"991971daa85980e726ccb3fc4bd7d41e0396c188b527973e3c9901e208a8bd70","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"nra","title":"New: ZeroTech Trace X 5-25X 56 mm FFP RMG-H MRAD Illuminated Riflescope","url":"https://www.ssusa.org/content/new-zerotech-trace-x-5-25x-56-mm-ffp-rmg-h-mrad-illuminated-riflescope/","published_at":"2026-10-15T04:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"commercial","notes":"NRA publication. 'New:' product posts are close to press releases (mounts, bags, ballistic apps); treat as manufacturer claims. Mostly firearm content, so filter."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Freshness and novelty, and Credibility, this new riflescope announcement supplies citable manufacturer specifications, including 31.5-ounce weight, 34 MRAD elevation travel, and an IPX7 rating, relevant to carried weight and weather exposure at Mammoth. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, the NRA publication's commercial, press-release-like account supports attributed specifications only; it supplies no independent field testing or disclosed sponsorship relationship with ZeroTech. It best supports Piece 01's assessment of newly released gear.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"text":"Explore The NRA Universe Of Websites\n\nNRA.ORG\n\nManage Your Membership\n\nNRA Near You\n\nFriends of NRA\n\nState and Federal Gun Laws\n\nNRA Online Training\n\nPolitics, Policy and Legislation\n\nClubs and Associations Affiliated Clubs, Ranges and Businesses\n\nCompetitive Shooting NRA Day\n\nCompetitive Shooting Programs\n\nAmerica's Rifle Challenge\n\nCompetitor Classification Lookup\n\nShooting Sports USA\n\nAdaptive Shooting\n\nGreat American Outdoor Show\n\nNRA Whittington Center\n\nEvents and Entertainment Women's Wilderness Escape\n\nNRA Whittington Center\n\nFriends of NRA\n\nGreat American Outdoor Show\n\nNRA Annual Meetings & Exhibits\n\nNRA Day\n\nNRA Country\