pringfield M1A Match\n\nShooting Illustrated\n\nFirearm Training\n\nNRA Marksmanship Qualification Program\n\nNRA Training Course Catalog\n\nWomen On Target® Instructional Shooting Clinics\n\nSafety and Education NRA Gun Safety Rules\n\nEddie Eagle GunSafe® Program\n\nEddie Eagle Treehouse\n\nWhittington University\n\nFirearm Training\n\nNRA Online Training\n\nBecome An NRA Instructor\n\nHunter Education\n\nNRA Gunsmithing Schools\n\nRefuse To Be A Victim®\n\nScholarships, Awards and Contests Scholarships, Awards & Contests\n\nShopping NRA Store\n\nNRA Country Gear\n\nNRA Program Materials Center\n\nNRA Member Benefits\n\nNRA Whittington Center Store\n\nVolunteering Volunteer For NRA\n\nGet Involved Locally\n\nVolunteer At The Great American Outdoor Show\n\nInstitute for Legislative Action\n\nVolunteer at the NRA Whittington Center\n\nWomen's Interests NRA Membership For Women\n\nWomen's Wilderness Escape\n\nNRA Women's Network\n\nWomen On Target® Instructional Shooting Clinics\n\nSybil Ludington Women's Freedom Award\n\nWomen's Wildlife Management / Con"},{"text":"servation Scholarship\n\nBecome An NRA Instructor\n\nYouth Interests Eddie Eagle Treehouse\n\nScholarships, Awards & Contests\n\nNRA Day\n\nNRA Marksmanship Qualification Program\n\nYouth Education Summit\n\nAdventure Camp\n\nYouth Hunter Education Challenge\n\nNational Junior Shooting Camps\n\nYouth Wildlife Art Contest\n\nHome Air Gun Program\n\nNRA Junior Membership\n\nNRA Family\n\nEddie Eagle GunSafe® Program\n\nNRA Gun Safety Rules\n\nCollegiate Shooting Programs\n\nNational Youth Shooting Sports Cooperative Program\n\nRequest for Eagle Scout Certificate\n\nVortex Pro Cantilever Mounts Turn One Base Into a System\n\nVortex Pro Cantilever Mounts Turn One Base Into a System\n\nVortex’s new Pro Cantilever mounts and accessories work as one modular family, so a red dot, clip-on thermal or laser rangefinder can join a riflescope without improvised hardware.\n\nSSUSA Staff\n\nVortex has a fix for the rifle with a top rail that has turned into a pile of adapters. Its new Pro Cantilever Mounts and Accessories, made in the U.S., are designed as a single system. You start with the Pro Cantilever Mount, then bolt on offset or overhead red dots, clip-on thermals or a laser rangefinder as the rifle takes on new work.\n\nYou can get the"},{"text":" mount for 30 mm or 34 mm tubes, with a choice of 1.54- or 1.93-inch ring heights. Like any cantilever, it pushes the rings out ahead of the rail clamps so an AR-style rifle has room for proper eye relief. Vortex says the two heights cover everything from an ordinary riflescope to setups built around clip-ons and night-vision devices. Someone there also had the good sense to print the torque specifications right on the ring caps. Right now, it’s priced at $179.99 on Vortex’s website.\n\nIf you run a low-power variable optic and want a red dot off to the side for close targets, the Pro Cantilever 45-Degree Offset Red Dot Mount attaches to the cantilever base. Roll the rifle a few degrees and the dot is waiting. Anyone who shoots Three-Gun has made that move a thousand times. It takes optics with a DeltaPoint Pro footprint and sells for $89.99.\n\nNot everyone likes tipping the rifle mid-stage. For them, there’s the Pro 12 O’Clock Red Dot Mount at $104.99, which sets a DeltaPoint Pro-footprint dot directly over the main optic. Vortex makes it in both tube sizes, and it fits the new Pro Riflescope Rings as well as the cantilever mounts.\n\nThen there’s the Pro Diving Board Mount, a forward "},{"text":"shelf for gear with nowhere else to go. That might be a red dot, an infrared illuminator or a rangefinder like Vortex’s own Impact 4000 Ballistic Rail-Mounted Laser Rangefinder. Sizing and compatibility match the 12 o’clock mount. It runs $119.99.\n\nYou can order the mounts and accessories from vortexoptics.com, or use the company’s store locator to find a local dealer.\n\nRelated Stories\n\nNew: MDT 35 mm High One-Piece Scope Mount\n\nReview: Leapers UTG Pro P.O.I. Service Rifle Mount\n\nReview: Vortex Venom 1-6X 24 mm Optic And Pro Extended Cantilever Mount\n\nNew: MDT Unimount\n\nNew: Primary Arms GLx Riflescope Mounts\n\nMost Popular\n\nMore like this from around the NRA\n\nLatest\n\nNew: ZeroTech Trace X 5-25X 56 mm FFP RMG-H MRAD Illuminated Riflescope\n\nZeroTech’s Trace X 5-25X 56 mm pairs an illuminated FFP MRAD reticle with 0.1-MRAD turrets and a zero stop for long-range competitors.\n\nBeretta and La Vallée Build a Shotgun You Can See Into\n\nBeretta and watchmaker La Vallée have unveiled the SO10 Kronos, a one-off sidelock with sapphire-windowed sideplates built for Beretta’s 500th anniversary.\n\nChad Heckler Wins Limited Title at 2026 IPRF World Championship\n\nChad Heckler hit 171 of 200 shots to "},{"text":"edge JC Robertson by two impacts and win the IPRF Limited world championship.\n\nNew: Springfield Armory Echelon Pro 9 mm Pistols\n\nSpringfield Armory’s Echelon Pro Comp brings an integral compensator, laser-sculpted grip texture and a steel magazine well to the 9 mm Echelon.\n\n2026 IPSC Shotgun World Shoot: U.S. Women’s Results\n\nLanny Barnes and Lena Miculek each won three world titles while Dianna Muller took silver at the 2026 IPSC Shotgun World Shoot.\n\nKentucky’s Braden Peiser Goes Perfect, and the NCAA Rifle Streak Rolls On\n\nKentucky’s Braden Peiser fired a perfect 600-53X in air rifle against Murray State, the sixth 600 of the young NCAA season.\n\nInterests\n\nSportsmen Expected to Reap $40 Million in Sales Tax Savings in Florida | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nWhy This DOJ Opinion on Handgun Ownership Matters | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nPelican Air Carry-on Case Review | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nSportsmen Expected to Reap $40 Million in Sales Tax Savings in Florida | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nA Summer Of Legends: 1999 National Matches | An NRA Shooting Sports Journal\n\nA Summer Of Legends: 1999 National Matches | An NRA Shooting Sports Journal\n\nA Miracle After T"},{"text":"he Tornado: 1998 National Matches | An NRA Shooting Sports Journal\n\nRecords Fall, Legends Rise: 1997 National Matches | An NRA Shooting Sports Journal\n\n#SundayGunday: Springfield Armory Model 2020 Boundary | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nPelican Air Carry-on Case Review | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\n#SundayGunday: Springfield Armory Model 2020 Boundary | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nReviewed: Sitka’s Blizzard Pro Parka | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\n#SundayGunday: CZ 457 American | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nGun Of The Week: Tisas PX-57 FO Raptor | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nFreedom is On the Ballot in Virginia | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nGun Of The Week: TriStar Arms Raptor II | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nGet the best of Shooting Sports USA delivered to your inbox.\n\nGET IN TOUCH\n\nContact Us\n\nAdvertising\n\nCONNECT WITH US\n\nSECTIONS\n\nNews\n\nComing Events\n\nNational Matches\n\nReviews\n\nVideos\n\nIN THE SPOTLIGHT\n\nNRA Institute For Legislative Action\n\nNRA Explore\n\nNRA MEMBERSHIP\n\nNRA Membership\n\nExclusive Member Benefits\n\nMembership FAQ\n\nManage Your Membership\n\nEXPLORE MORE NRA PUBLICATIONS\n\nTake Your NRA Magazine With You!\n\nJoin The NRA And Our Growing Commu"},{"text":"nity\n\nDiscover Everything That The NRA Has To Offer\n\nAttend A Friends Of NRA Event Near You"}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.ssusa.org/content/new-mdt-one-piece-night-vision-bridge/","version_id":"154009e503ea72f9f4e7321c04b365252bf5f2676aa1be831c7241f65755f7d4","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"nra","title":"New: MDT One-Piece Night Vision Bridge","url":"https://www.ssusa.org/content/new-mdt-one-piece-night-vision-bridge/","published_at":"2026-09-16T04:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"commercial","notes":"NRA publication. 'New:' product posts are close to press releases (mounts, bags, ballistic apps); treat as manufacturer claims. Mostly firearm content, so filter."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this new night-vision mounting accessory offers citable manufacturer specifications, including a claimed 5.9-ounce carried weight and a spacer configuration MDT says is unsuitable for thermal or night-vision use. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require treating this commercial announcement as MDT's claims, flagging MDT's Mammoth sponsorship, and withholding conclusions about alignment or wear resistance without independent testing; it supports Piece 01's new-gear coverage and Piece 03's compatibility considerations.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"text":"Explore The NRA Universe Of Websites\n\nNRA.ORG\n\nManage Your Membership\n\nNRA Near You\n\nFriends of NRA\n\nState and Federal Gun Laws\n\nNRA Online Training\n\nPolitics, Policy and Legislation\n\nClubs and Associations Affiliated Clubs, Ranges and Businesses\n\nCompetitive Shooting NRA Day\n\nCompetitive Shooting Programs\n\nAmerica's Rifle Challenge\n\nCompetitor Classification Lookup\n\nShooting Sports USA\n\nAdaptive Shooting\n\nGreat American Outdoor Show\n\nNRA Whittington Center\n\nEvents and Entertainment Women's Wilderness Escape\n\nNRA Whittington Center\n\nFriends of NRA\n\nGreat American Outdoor Show\n\nNRA Annual Meetings & Exhibits\n\nNRA Day\n\nNRA Country\n\nCompetitive Shooting Programs\n\nNRA Gun Gurus\n\nFirearms Training NRA Gun Safety Rules\n\nFirearm Training\n\nBecome An NRA Instructor\n\nBecome A Training Counselor\n\nNRA Range Safety Officers\n\nShooting Sports Coach Development\n\nAdaptive Shooting\n\nNRA Gunsmithing Schools\n\nNRA Online Training\n\nNRA Program Materials Center\n\nNRA Marksmanship Qualification Program\n\nFind A Course\n\nNRA CCW\n\nNRA Training Course Catalog\n\nGiving Friends of NRA\n\nRing of Freedom\n\nInstitute for Legislative Action\n\nNRA Whittington Center\n\nNRA Firearms For Freedom\n\nHistory History Of The NRA\n\nN"},{"text":"RA Museums\n\nI Have This Old Gun\n\nNRA Gun Gurus\n\nHunting Hunter Education\n\nYouth Hunter Education Challenge\n\nNRA Whittington Center\n\nGreat American Outdoor Show\n\nHunters for the Hungry\n\nAmerican Hunter\n\nHunting Legislation Issues\n\nState Hunting Resources\n\nNRA Institute for Legislative Action\n\nAmerican Rifleman\n\nAdaptive Hunting Database\n\nOutdoor Adventure Partner of the NRA\n\nLaw Enforcement, Military, Security Law Enforcement, Military, Security\n\nMedia and Publications NRA Blog\n\nAmerican Rifleman\n\nAmerican Hunter\n\nShooting Illustrated\n\nNRA Family\n\nShooting Sports USA\n\nNRA All Access\n\nNRA Gun Gurus\n\nMembership Join The NRA\n\nNRA Member Benefits\n\nManage Your Membership\n\nNRA Store\n\nNRA Whittington Center\n\nNRA Endorsed Member Insurance\n\nNRA Membership Recruiting\n\nNRA State Associations\n\nNRA Membership For Women\n\nNRA Life Membership\n\nRenew or Upgrade Your Membership\n\nNRA Junior Membership\n\nNRA Business Alliance\n\nNRA Industry Ally Program\n\nPolitics And Legislation NRA Institute for Legislative Action\n\nNRA-ILA Gun Laws\n\nRegister To Vote\n\nCandidate Ratings\n\nWrite Your Lawmakers\n\nNRA-ILA FrontLines\n\nNRA Political Victory Fund\n\nNRA State Associations\n\nRecreational Shooting America's Rifle Chal"},{"text":"lenge\n\nNRA Whittington Center\n\nWomen's Wilderness Escape\n\nNRA Day\n\nThe NRA Range\n\nHome Air Gun Program\n\nAdaptive Shooting\n\nRange Services\n\nNRA Member Benefits\n\nGreat American Outdoor Show\n\nNRA Springfield M1A Match\n\nShooting Illustrated\n\nFirearm Training\n\nNRA Marksmanship Qualification Program\n\nNRA Training Course Catalog\n\nWomen On Target® Instructional Shooting Clinics\n\nSafety and Education NRA Gun Safety Rules\n\nEddie Eagle GunSafe® Program\n\nEddie Eagle Treehouse\n\nWhittington University\n\nFirearm Training\n\nNRA Online Training\n\nBecome An NRA Instructor\n\nHunter Education\n\nNRA Gunsmithing Schools\n\nRefuse To Be A Victim®\n\nScholarships, Awards and Contests Scholarships, Awards & Contests\n\nShopping NRA Store\n\nNRA Country Gear\n\nNRA Program Materials Center\n\nNRA Member Benefits\n\nNRA Whittington Center Store\n\nVolunteering Volunteer For NRA\n\nGet Involved Locally\n\nVolunteer At The Great American Outdoor Show\n\nInstitute for Legislative Action\n\nVolunteer at the NRA Whittington Center\n\nWomen's Interests NRA Membership For Women\n\nWomen's Wilderness Escape\n\nNRA Women's Network\n\nWomen On Target® Instructional Shooting Clinics\n\nSybil Ludington Women's Freedom Award\n\nWomen's Wildlife Management / Con"},{"text":"servation Scholarship\n\nBecome An NRA Instructor\n\nYouth Interests Eddie Eagle Treehouse\n\nScholarships, Awards & Contests\n\nNRA Day\n\nNRA Marksmanship Qualification Program\n\nYouth Education Summit\n\nAdventure Camp\n\nYouth Hunter Education Challenge\n\nNational Junior Shooting Camps\n\nYouth Wildlife Art Contest\n\nHome Air Gun Program\n\nNRA Junior Membership\n\nNRA Family\n\nEddie Eagle GunSafe® Program\n\nNRA Gun Safety Rules\n\nCollegiate Shooting Programs\n\nNational Youth Shooting Sports Cooperative Program\n\nRequest for Eagle Scout Certificate\n\nNew: MDT One-Piece Night Vision Bridge\n\nNew: MDT One-Piece Night Vision Bridge\n\nA 13-inch, 20-MOA rail for MDT’s ACC and LSS Gen3 chassis keeps clip-on thermal and night vision optics lined up with the riflescope.\n\nSSUSA Staff\n\nPredator hunters adopted clip-on thermal and night vision units for a solid reason—they let a shooter keep the same riflescope, reticle and zero after dark. A sloppy mount is what undermines them. Since a clip-on sits directly in front of a scope’s objective lens, any rail that isn’t truly in line with the scope base leaves the image and the reticle telling slightly different stories.\n\nMDT’s answer is the One-Piece NV Bridge, a 13-inch,"},{"text":" 20-MOA rail that bolts along the top of existing MDT fore-ends. It matches the elevation built into a 20-MOA scope base, which puts the clip-on and riflescope on a common line of sight. The bridge fits the ACC Elite, ACC Premier Gen2 and LSS Gen3 Competition fore-ends.\n\nAs the name implies, the bridge is a single machined part rather than a string of rail sections, so there are no joints between segments that could drift out of line. MDT starts with 6061-T6 aluminum, applies Type III hard anodizing and then adds Cerakote on top. That finish is meant for heavy wear. Over a season, these units tend to go on at dusk and off at dawn, with every cycle another round of clamping.\n\nComplete, the bridge weighs 5.9 ounces and measures 13.01 x 1.96 x 0.95 inches. It attaches with 6-32 flat-head screws torqued to 15 inch-pounds with a T10 driver.\n\nMDT calls for a 20-MOA scope base or integrated rail and scope rings at least 1½ inches tall. Barrels must be longer than 15 inches to clear the front of the bridge, with a muzzle diameter less than 1.1 inches.\n\nTwo included spacers lift the bridge 0.2 inches to clear straight-contour barrels up to 1¼ inches in diameter. MDT’s compatibility notes ar"},{"text":"e candid about the trade-off. With the spacers installed, the bridge’s Picatinny rail no longer sits in line with the scope base, and the company does not recommend that setup for night vision or thermal use. Shooters who go that route will likely need rings taller than 1½ inches (MDT suggests 1.7 inches or more) or a scope with a smaller objective bell.\n\nThe One-Piece NV Bridge is available now in black or flat dark earth for $224.99 through MDT dealers and at mdttac.com.\n\nRelated Stories\n\nPreview: Armasight Contractor 640 2.3-9.2X 35 mm Thermal Optic\n\nNew: Trijicon REAP-IR-50 Thermal Riflescope Series\n\nNew Accuracy X Multi-Sight MAX Takes Your Red Dot Lower\n\nNew: Trijicon SRO (Specialized Reflex Optic)\n\nWATCH: Swarovski Optik Z5(i)+ Riflescopes\n\nMost Popular\n\nMore like this from around the NRA\n\nLatest\n\nNew: ZeroTech Trace X 5-25X 56 mm FFP RMG-H MRAD Illuminated Riflescope\n\nZeroTech’s Trace X 5-25X 56 mm pairs an illuminated FFP MRAD reticle with 0.1-MRAD turrets and a zero stop for long-range competitors.\n\nBeretta and La Vallée Build a Shotgun You Can See Into\n\nBeretta and watchmaker La Vallée have unveiled the SO10 Kronos, a one-off sidelock with sapphire-windowed sideplates bu"},{"text":"ilt for Beretta’s 500th anniversary.\n\nChad Heckler Wins Limited Title at 2026 IPRF World Championship\n\nChad Heckler hit 171 of 200 shots to edge JC Robertson by two impacts and win the IPRF Limited world championship.\n\nNew: Springfield Armory Echelon Pro 9 mm Pistols\n\nSpringfield Armory’s Echelon Pro Comp brings an integral compensator, laser-sculpted grip texture and a steel magazine well to the 9 mm Echelon.\n\n2026 IPSC Shotgun World Shoot: U.S. Women’s Results\n\nLanny Barnes and Lena Miculek each won three world titles while Dianna Muller took silver at the 2026 IPSC Shotgun World Shoot.\n\nKentucky’s Braden Peiser Goes Perfect, and the NCAA Rifle Streak Rolls On\n\nKentucky’s Braden Peiser fired a perfect 600-53X in air rifle against Murray State, the sixth 600 of the young NCAA season.\n\nInterests\n\nSportsmen Expected to Reap $40 Million in Sales Tax Savings in Florida | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nWhy This DOJ Opinion on Handgun Ownership Matters | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nPelican Air Carry-on Case Review | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nSportsmen Expected to Reap $40 Million in Sales Tax Savings in Florida | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nA Summer Of Legends: 1999 Natio"},{"text":"nal Matches | An NRA Shooting Sports Journal\n\nA Summer Of Legends: 1999 National Matches | An NRA Shooting Sports Journal\n\nA Miracle After The Tornado: 1998 National Matches | An NRA Shooting Sports Journal\n\nRecords Fall, Legends Rise: 1997 National Matches | An NRA Shooting Sports Journal\n\n#SundayGunday: Springfield Armory Model 2020 Boundary | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nPelican Air Carry-on Case Review | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\n#SundayGunday: Springfield Armory Model 2020 Boundary | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nReviewed: Sitka’s Blizzard Pro Parka | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\n#SundayGunday: CZ 457 American | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nGun Of The Week: Tisas PX-57 FO Raptor | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nFreedom is On the Ballot in Virginia | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nGun Of The Week: TriStar Arms Raptor II | An Official Journal Of The NRA\n\nGet the best of Shooting Sports USA delivered to your inbox.\n\nGET IN TOUCH\n\nContact Us\n\nAdvertising\n\nCONNECT WITH US\n\nSECTIONS\n\nNews\n\nComing Events\n\nNational Matches\n\nReviews\n\nVideos\n\nIN THE SPOTLIGHT\n\nNRA Institute For Legislative Action\n\nNRA Explore\n\nNRA MEMBERSHIP\n\nNRA Membership\n\nExclusive Member Benefits\n"},{"text":"\nMembership FAQ\n\nManage Your Membership\n\nEXPLORE MORE NRA PUBLICATIONS\n\nTake Your NRA Magazine With You!\n\nJoin The NRA And Our Growing Community\n\nDiscover Everything That The NRA Has To Offer\n\nAttend A Friends Of NRA Event Near You"}]}
{"item_id":"youtube:Ry_3zPBi1J4","version_id":"ad59dabd0a4ecc19c752ef509dd565f73b4fa0a99b1edc7470c6f94adf76baf7","kind":"youtube","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK GR2 34L Review: Gear Bag or Rucking Bag?","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ry_3zPBi1J4","published_at":"2026-10-03T22:26:29Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Same publisher as ruckauthority_feed. Pack, vest, and boot reviews."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this recent review adds specific field observations from carrying a 20-pound plate plus equipment: separate compartments improved access, but the reviewer reported lower-back numbness after about 90 minutes and low plate positioning, supporting Pieces 01, 02, and 03. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require disclosing the free GORUCK pack and affiliate links, giving praise less independent weight, and limiting conclusions to the described two-week experience rather than assuming multi-day durability or comfort.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.02,"end_seconds":30.41,"text":"Hey, I'm Joel from Ruck Authority, and\nthis is the GORUCK GR2. It's GORUCK's travel bag. GORUCK sent me this bag, and the links\nbelow are affiliate links. But GORUCK did not sign off on this video,\nand they didn't tell me what to say. When I took it rucking, the organization\nwas the best part about it. So I've had it for about two weeks now. So this is the 34 liter in black,\nobviously. It's built like the GR1 but with a second full compartment up\nfront."},{"segment_id":"s-0002","start_seconds":30.86,"end_seconds":67.51,"text":"And no, it's not the GR1 version 2. This is a separate product altogether. They have different features that suit\ndifferent needs. But I understand that the naming can be a\nbit confusing based off the 4.0 to the 5.0. Both compartments open flat. The shell is 1,000 denier CORDURA. YKK zippers with cord pulls you can find\nwith gloves on. Webbing inside and out, and of course a\npatch that you can swap."},{"segment_id":"s-0003","start_seconds":72.75,"end_seconds":103.37,"text":"Today we're at Round Lake. Jase and I are going to explore it. I did my testing with the 20-pound GORUCK\nplate. And I also carried my drone case, a water\nbottle, dog bottle, safety equipment, and of course bear spray. The second compartment turns out to be the\nwhole story. In the main compartment, I had the plate\nin the sleeve, my drone case, and a first aid kit. In the front compartment I had my drone\nout as well as the batteries and the\ncontroller."},{"segment_id":"s-0004","start_seconds":103.61,"end_seconds":134.2,"text":"So when I wanted to fly, I opened one\nzipper and I didn't have to dig past the 20-pound\nplate to do it. On the outside, the webbing carried a\nwater bottle holder, bear spray, and Jase's leash. There's a separate laptop compartment\nagainst your back with a removable frame sheet to keep the bag's shape. So you don't necessarily need to ruck. It\ndoes work as just a travel bag. It's a carry-on size bag. That's what\nGORUCK built this for. One bag travel. Now I don't think I'd have my laptop and a\nweight plate in at the same time."},{"segment_id":"s-0005","start_seconds":134.57,"end_seconds":164.75,"text":"Height-wise, it's only about a half an\ninch taller than the GR1. The extra space comes in the depth. It's about nine inches deep versus seven\nwith the GR1. That's where the second compartment lives. At 34 liters, this is a lot of bag for a\nday walk. Room is great until things have room to\nmove. But I think that problem is partly solved\nby having the separate compartments. Just pack it snug. Just like the GR1, this one doesn't have a\nsternum strap and there's no hip belt in the box. And like the GR1, I didn't find myself\nmissing the sternum strap."},{"segment_id":"s-0006","start_seconds":165.0,"end_seconds":195.63,"text":"They pulled my shoulders back a bit and my\nposture seemed to appreciate it. I did miss the Rucker's big lumbar pad. After using it nonstop for about an hour\nand a half, I started to notice that my lower back was feeling a little bit\nnumb. Nothing dramatic, just something that I\nnoticed. The plate sleeve works, it's just not\nas refined as the Rucker's. Just like the GR1, it's an open sleeve\nwith a couple of straps. It's not a pocket that locks the plate in\nup high. In a bag this tall, a 20-pound plate can\nfall a bit lower than what you'd"},{"segment_id":"s-0007","start_seconds":195.63,"end_seconds":218.77,"text":"like, and that's where I felt it. Of course, the GORUCK's lifetime guarantee\nis behind it. For a day out with the drone kit, the GR2 is excellent. The front compartment alone is the reason\nto pick it up over a single-compartment pack. As a dedicated ruck, it's a lot of bag. The exact bag's linked below with the\nwritten review. If you want more reviews,\ncomparisons and field tests, be sure to like and subscribe\nto Ruck Authority."}]}
{"item_id":"youtube:SlW6o3cO2rk","version_id":"8dc6ddadaee9daac8d079ff5a89e70839084d84baa998a3573b1574edf167815","kind":"youtube","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK GR1 26L Review: An Everyday Bag That Can Ruck","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SlW6o3cO2rk","published_at":"2026-10-02T16:28:24Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Same publisher as ruckauthority_feed. Pack, vest, and boot reviews."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, the described trail outing with a 20-pound plate plus gear provides citable observations about load organization, strap comfort, and limited lumbar support relevant to Mammoth pack selection. Commercial incentives requires flagging the free GORUCK bag and affiliate links; Uncertainty and Freshness and novelty limit this newly reviewed experience to two weeks of use, without evidence establishing multi-day durability or independently testing water resistance. It supports Piece 01's newly reviewed gear and Piece 03's pack-selection tradeoffs.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":32.22,"text":"Hi, I'm Joel with Ruck Authority,\nand this is the GORUCK GR1. One bag meant to ruck, travel,\nand carry every day. Quick disclosure: GORUCK sent me this bag. And the links below are affiliate links. Here's which ones it does well and where a dedicated ruck\ndoes it better. Now this is the 26 liter\nin Ranger Green. I've had it for about two weeks. There's one big main compartment and\nwebbing on just about every"},{"segment_id":"s-0002","start_seconds":32.22,"end_seconds":64.52,"text":"surface. It's a tall bag, about 20 inches, and it feels it. I'm 6'2\"\nso it works for me and it's a lot of room. If you're shorter there's a 21 liter version that's\nabout two inches shorter. For this bag my big outing was a lake trail up north with my dog\nJase. In the bag I had a 20 pound plate, my drone in its case, a water bottle, and a first aid kit. I\nkept all of that in the main compartment. On the outside I kept Jase's leash and his bowl.\nI also brought bear spray. There are a couple"},{"segment_id":"s-0003","start_seconds":64.52,"end_seconds":94.71,"text":"smaller pockets on the top which you can\nput some pretty small items in. And then you've got this front storage pouch. But beyond that, it's\nbasically just one big room. That's great when your load is just a plate\nand a hard case. Plenty of MOLLE webbing on these. You got it on the front, the sides, and then along the back on the\ninside. It's got a whole ecosystem of different attachments, storage options, gear, pretty much\nanything you can think of. So that makes up for any lack of"},{"segment_id":"s-0004","start_seconds":94.71,"end_seconds":125.32,"text":"dedicated water bottle holders. The GR1\ndoes appear to have a laptop sleeve on the back here. And then if you open this little Velcro\npart here, that's where you'll see the back plate that provides the support. So if you've got a\nfull bag, you could easily have it stand up. This doesn't have the handles on all sides like the\nRucker 5.0, but it does have the standard handle at the top here. The zippers are large, quiet, and\nreliable. Definitely have no problem grabbing them. There's no chest strap and there's no hip belt in\nthe box. That's on purpose."},{"segment_id":"s-0005","start_seconds":125.32,"end_seconds":156.44,"text":"GORUCK built this to ride high on your back and stay stable without a\nwaist belt. Also, you can add both later if you decide to buy those separately. Ultimately, I didn't\nmiss the chest strap. Without one, the straps pulled my shoulders back a little bit. My posture is\na work in progress, so it actually felt like it was helping. Plenty of experienced Ruckers will tell\nyou that a sternum strap matters when the weight goes up. I'm not arguing with them. That's just how\nit felt on me with 20 pounds. I did find myself missing the lumbar support a bit. The Rucker 5.0\nhas a massive lumbar support,"},{"segment_id":"s-0006","start_seconds":156.44,"end_seconds":186.87,"text":"so going from that to this felt like a bit of a downgrade. Out of the\nbox, the straps might feel a bit stiff, but they quickly break in after some use. The plate goes in\nthis elastic sleeve against your back. It works fine. It's just not quite as refined as the\nRucker 5.0. This is an open sleeve with a couple of straps. Here's the 20 pound plate in the much\nlooser pocket. The sleeve takes any of GORUCK's plates up to the 45 pound, and they recommend the\n30 for this bag because it's thicker and it fills out"},{"segment_id":"s-0007","start_seconds":186.87,"end_seconds":219.81,"text":"the sleeve. Whereas the Rucker 5.0 has two\nplate sleeves and takes up to 75 pounds, and it holds the plate still for workouts. GORUCK calls\nthe GR1 highly water resistant. Now, that's not quite the same as waterproof. You'll see\nthe price on screen. It's not cheap. For that, you'll get a bag that GORUCK will repair or replace under its lifetime\nguarantee. So here's where I land.\nFor pure rucking, the Rucker 5.0 takes the win. It's built for exactly that. If I'm carrying a lot of\ngear, the GR2's extra"},{"segment_id":"s-0008","start_seconds":219.81,"end_seconds":245.47,"text":"compartment wins. The GR1 is everything in between. It's smaller and\nit's lighter than the GR2, and it works as an everyday bag. In my opinion, this is the best looking\nbag that I own, and I love the color. It's a solid ruck too, it's just not quite as refined as the\ndedicated Rucker. The bag is linked below, along with the written review. Be sure to like and subscribe for more reviews down the line. That's all for now. Keep rucking."}]}
{"item_id":"youtube:jVz1KOKvi-I","version_id":"21ec9121eed29ffe19c5c9436e6fb37aea5b6f887f750c12e65180de26ca96fc","kind":"youtube","publisher_id":"ruckauthority","title":"GORUCK Rucker 5.0 Review: My First Week on the Trail","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVz1KOKvi-I","published_at":"2026-09-24T02:51:16Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Same publisher as ruckauthority_feed. Pack, vest, and boot reviews."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this first-week trail review provides citable observations from carrying a 20 lb plate, including stable loading, no chafing, and some trapezius soreness, supporting Pieces 01 and 02. Commercial incentives requires noting the free GORUCK pack and affiliate commissions; Uncertainty limits these findings to short-term use, with rain resistance, long-term durability, and the optional hip belt untested.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":4.24,"end_seconds":35.44,"text":"Hey, I'm Joel with Rock Authority and this is the Go Ruck Rucker [music] 5.0. Go Ruck was kind enough to send this over, but they haven't told me what to talk about. For the last week, I've been testing this on the trails [music] up in the Northwoods of Wisconsin. Go Ruck built this Rucker as a training bag. So, the main idea is to keep a weight plate high against [music] your back instead of letting it sag like a normal backpack would. I don't actually have a Rucker [music] 4.0, so I can't do a direct comparison. So, the Rucker 5.0 is an upgrade over the 4.0. And the main"},{"segment_id":"s-0002","start_seconds":33.92,"end_seconds":65.16,"text":"differences are that it includes a larger lumbar support. [music] It's 30 mm thick. It looks enormous when you actually see the bag, but once I actually used it, it was very [music] comfortable and I didn't really notice it after a bit. This is the larger 25 [music] L version, but they do make a 20 L version. Just depends on your size and where the lumbar support falls on your back. I'm 6'2\", [music] so they sent me the larger one, but everybody's back size is different. The main body is 1,000 [music] denier"},{"segment_id":"s-0003","start_seconds":63.52,"end_seconds":94.44,"text":"Cordura. The fabric against your back and underneath the shoulder straps is a smoother 210 denier Cordura. So, it isn't as abrasive [music] against clothing or bare skin. The shoulder straps are thick and they're well padded. The sternum strap [music] can move up or down or come off entirely. There are no load lifters and the padded waist belt [music] is optional. I didn't have one, so I didn't test with it. Inside, you'll see [music] that there's two elevated plate pockets. Got one in the back, one in [music] the"},{"segment_id":"s-0004","start_seconds":93.12,"end_seconds":124.28,"text":"front. I did my testing with this 20 lb plate. The shorter pocket fits the Go Ruck standard [music] 10, 20, and 30 lb plates. The longer pocket fits its long 30 and 45 lb plates. You can hold the standard plates too. [music] Together, they're rated up to 75 lbs, although that's a capacity figure, not a suggestion for where to [music] start. And the built-in frame sheet keeps the bag from around it when you tighten the straps here. The bag opens flat. You'll see that"},{"segment_id":"s-0005","start_seconds":122.4,"end_seconds":152.4,"text":"these internal [music] pockets now close with magnets. Found this a good place to throw my keys and any [music] assorted little items, snacks, whatever. It's also a place to hang a hydration bladder. You'll see the D-ring, and then there's an opening up here. The zippers are YKK with 550 cord pulls. These are easy to grab, and they don't rattle around when you walk. Outside, you'll get the MOLLE [music] webbing. Got it on the front and on the sides."},{"segment_id":"s-0006","start_seconds":151.0,"end_seconds":182.4,"text":"If you're familiar with MOLLE, there's a whole [music] ecosystem of attachments. There's four padded handles on the top, the sides, [music] and the bottom. The two holes on the bottom are there to drain water. GoRuck describes this bag as highly water resistant. So far, I've been fortunate [music] enough to avoid the rain, so I haven't actually tested that yet. Haven't tried to destroy [music] it, but the stitching around the straps, the handles, and the plate pockets does [music] look substantial. And this bag is protected by the SCARS Lifetime Guarantee. Apparently, some people don't"},{"segment_id":"s-0007","start_seconds":180.8,"end_seconds":213.12,"text":"like the big [music] GoRuck branding. They want it more discreet. I don't care. I'm fine to show it. And this has been rock solid. [music] I don't feel it wobbling. It's not shifting weight around. I might have had a bit of a sweaty back, but I'm a bigger [music] guy, so that's going to happen. I didn't have any hot spots or chafing, numbness, or any lower back discomfort. [music] I will say that my traps were a little bit sore, but I think maybe if I were to use the hip belt, then I could alleviate some of the"},{"segment_id":"s-0008","start_seconds":210.28,"end_seconds":242.04,"text":"stress up high. Overall, I didn't really notice it while I was walking. This is just kind of [music] an after-the-fact thing. I found there's plenty of space for my extra gear along with the plate. [music] I had a drone case, and I had some camera equipment. If you mainly want an everyday backpack that can occasionally carry [music] some weight, this may be more specialized and more expensive than you need. You give up things like a laptop [music] compartment, an outside bottle pocket, and quick access storage. Another consideration is price. This is"},{"segment_id":"s-0009","start_seconds":239.76,"end_seconds":265.33,"text":"obviously a [music] premium bag, and the optional hip belt costs extra. If you want to see the current [music] sizes, colors, and price, the QR code and the link below are affiliate links. I may earn a commission [music] if you buy from the links, but that doesn't change my opinion on the bag. So, if you do want a dedicated [music] bag for carrying plates and doing ruck workouts, I think the Rucker 5.0 does make a lot of sense. >> [music]"}]}
{"item_id":"youtube:MA1GwzmgRYA","version_id":"8ec3d4e6978d7b5d5bc13fef255e5d766270d54b7ce6669a0612c02d1741f025","kind":"youtube","publisher_id":"goruck","title":"You Need To Be More Professional","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MA1GwzmgRYA","published_at":"2026-10-14T15:35:30Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"commercial","notes":"Pack and ruck manufacturer; counterpoint to Ruck Authority's reviews. Low posting volume."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance and Making the decision, the demonstrated use of a carabiner to secure an unused pack hip belt offers a specific, citable way to manage loose gear while rucking, supporting Piece 03. Under Credibility and Commercial incentives, GORUCK is a pack manufacturer, so its preference for shoulder carry and occasional hip-belt use is attributed advice, not independent evidence of comfort or endurance benefits.","excerpted":false,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":30.0,"text":"Here's what I hate. I hate it when I see I'll have to take this off cuz I don't I don't live like this. Some of you savages, you live like this and I I find it offensive and disgusting. So, you don't have a carabiner on your rucks sack. You put your rucks sack on and you you decide you don't want to use the hip belt and what you well you real savages, what you do is you don't even clip it, right? You don't need it. It's just, you know, you're just going about your day."},{"segment_id":"s-0002","start_seconds":28.16,"end_seconds":58.64,"text":"you're at public shopping or whatever and it's just flapping around. It's terrible. It's disgusting. Got to get yourself right. So, what you can do instead, it's like, yeah, then I see some of you, you know, you'll buckle it cuz you're mostly professional, right? That's good. Mostly professional is better than not professional. Okay, that's good. But what happens over time is against your butt while you're rucking, which I hate. I hate it when stuff like that happens. Like I don't"},{"segment_id":"s-0003","start_seconds":55.28,"end_seconds":85.6,"text":"want to get that vibe when I'm out rucking. I like it clean and professional. I might even be able to do this from here. This would be a herculean effort. So if you're going to store the ruck and you can't reach your hands behind your back like this, you need to practice more. You need to get more limber. I need to get more dexterity."},{"segment_id":"s-0004","start_seconds":83.28,"end_seconds":114.56,"text":"Did I get it through? I did. It didn't. All right. Let's see if I can actually do it without All right. There we go. It's in there, right? >> Yep. >> Not going anywhere. That's prolevel stuff right there. So, the carabiner keeps it attached like that. You don't always need or want a hip belt. You only need or want a hip belt when you"},{"segment_id":"s-0005","start_seconds":111.52,"end_seconds":142.16,"text":"actually want to transfer the load. I I'll walk you through that. This is bonus material. This is like old school when they used to make the bands put those good bides on the album. Like then you get a an a song like Stara. Anyway, so if you actually want to transfer the load from your shoulders down to your hips, it needs to actually transfer onto your hips. This is not"},{"segment_id":"s-0006","start_seconds":140.56,"end_seconds":173.68,"text":"really something that you want to, you know, just keep around your stomach unless you're doing bear crawls or your cadray tells you to. But here, I've got it like this. That's cool. On a 50 mileer, I might use this a couple times. That's it, right? I like it on the shoulders. So, I like it really high and really tight, but then I don't want this flapping around. So, the carabiner and when you store it, you never store it like that. That's disgusting. Okay? You"},{"segment_id":"s-0007","start_seconds":169.2,"end_seconds":180.24,"text":"store it like a pro like that. Everything in its right place."}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:c219e3c1-b26c-4c37-882b-759af3f892df","version_id":"706d57c181a240186c295b4643878e60ca99444061848ae779b70d1546c83cba","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"vortex","title":"#10MinuteTalk | Down Insulation Explained with Argali’s Brad Brooks","url":"http://www.vortexoptics.com","published_at":"2026-10-06T11:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"commercial","notes":"Same publisher as vortex_yt. Optics and layering episodes; many episodes off topic. Posts ~2 episodes/week, so max_items is 12 to cover 30 days."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, Brooks's described temperature-logger field experience and comparative treated/untreated down submersion test provide citable manufacturer evidence about sleep-system warmth, moisture, and carried weight that transfers to Mammoth, supporting Pieces 02 and 03. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require identifying Brooks's Argali product interest and publisher Vortex's Mammoth sponsorship: the reported tests lack supplied numerical results or independent confirmation, so claims about treatment-related loft loss, durability, and superior performance remain attributed claims rather than established findings.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":36.24,"text":"What is up, everybody? Mark on the mic, my good friend Ryan Muckinhern to my right, and we are about to get down with Brad Brooks. That's right. We got him, everybody. Mr. Brad Brooks from Argali across from us, and he is about to fill us in on all the great properties and considerations a person needs to be thinking about when they're purchasing a down product. Down is a fiber that's commonly used in technical outdoor apparel, sleeping bags, things of that"},{"segment_id":"s-0006","start_seconds":171.1,"end_seconds":205.3,"text":"is, you know, oftentimes, right. The amount of loft, there's a correlation between the amount of loft and the amount of air that can get trapped by that down. And that's what creates that, the warmth that down provides. And, you know, the difference between like feathers, like feathers don't have a lot of loft to them necessarily. That's why they can, they can compress really easily. Um, as opposed to the, the down under layer, um, like you would see on geese, right. That's what provides that loft and that's what traps the warm air and creates, creates warmth. I'm trying to"},{"segment_id":"s-0013","start_seconds":390.5,"end_seconds":426.96,"text":"get a lot of warmth without the bulk of a down material, um, and synthetics. If you have the right fabrics around that synthetic, it can move moisture and air really efficiently. You can also use quieter fabrics because you don't have to use what are called down proof fabrics to retain the feathers. Um, so, you know, for, if you're like tree stand bow hunting, for example, like, um, the little bit of tree stand bow hunting that I do, I, I won't, I don't wear down, but I do wear a lot of synthetic active insulation jackets for insulation because you, you don't need those likes, um, what"},{"segment_id":"s-0021","start_seconds":662.66,"end_seconds":698.98,"text":"800 is generally considered to be like, you're in the inferior, uh, fill power category. And the, the lower the fill power, the more down you need in order to achieve a given like warmth rating. So let's talk about sleeping bag specifically. So if you have a 20 degree bag filled with 700 fill power, you're going to need, I don't know, let's call it like three pounds of down in that sleeping bag, as opposed to a 900 fill power, 950 fill power, you might only need, um, let's call it a pound and a"},{"segment_id":"s-0041","start_seconds":1319.88,"end_seconds":1353.84,"text":"because it feels like we've all been like lied to for so long. And, and we're accustomed to sleep, to sleeping bag temperature ratings, not being, uh, accurate in terms of, of being reliable, uh, being reliably able to keep you warm at that specific rating. Right. Um, so I just want everybody to know if you're not aware, um, there are standardized tests for testing, uh, temperature ratings, but there is no requirement that any company, uh, has to, uh, uh, like abide by a certain rule for"},{"segment_id":"s-0055","start_seconds":1785.78,"end_seconds":1819.44,"text":"So like people do have different metabolisms, you know, they, uh, you, you know, I'm sure they have a standard or I would think they have a standard, but like, you know, my sleep pad, you mentioned our values, which like you said, different discussion, but mine might have a higher R value than the one that you're using. That's going to come into play. And then again, that's a standardized test, but depending on how you're layering in, are you wearing a puffy jacket and you're, you know, are you not, you know, things like that, what clothing do you have on there? There are so many viral variables at play there in the field, but like you said, you kind of have"},{"segment_id":"s-0061","start_seconds":1987.24,"end_seconds":2018.36,"text":"structure these baffles? Um, and you won't know that. Um, but yeah, I think it is in terms of like getting your down your, your sleeping bag out, like just give it a quick shake just to help it like kind of reloft real quick and then lay it down. And it should, if it's good quality down, it should just naturally reloft on its own. I want to talk about baffle structuring. Cause this is something I'm kind of fascinated with. Unlike, you know, subtractive manufacturing or additive manufacturing, like in, in, in the metal world, like I can build a part because I can"},{"segment_id":"s-0072","start_seconds":2344.46,"end_seconds":2375.38,"text":"Like even before we do, uh, we'll do a lab test simultaneously to me being like, I'm going to go out for a few nights and see what I think. And I have enough knowledge, you know, thankfully of being like upset and pissed off by other products where I'm like, I can compare to, uh, you know, subjectively to how like perceived warmth, this is kind of a fun, this is, I love nerding out on this stuff. When we were making our sleeping bags, um, this is what a nerd I am. I was carrying a temperature logger. Um, so a little puck that I bought off Amazon that would, that would map out and I had"},{"segment_id":"s-0082","start_seconds":2673.84,"end_seconds":2707.08,"text":"It's like, like you said, it, it can kill your desire. You're like, dude, at some level. And even on a hardcore backpacking, you know, uh, you know, quotation mark, extreme country, variable weather from, from snow to sleet, to rain, to sun, whatever, whatever you're encountering. But if you're in some of that nastier stuff and you're not having fun anymore, like if you're, if you're just miserable, you kind of get to a point, you're like, why am I, like I said, at some point, at some level, this is supposed to be fun. Even if it's really"},{"segment_id":"s-0089","start_seconds":2904.54,"end_seconds":2937.5,"text":"for our company at all, but like the reality is when we were working on it, that's what really got me started down doing my own research and treated versus untreated down my bias prior to making a sleeping bag was a treated down DWR water resistant down was the way to go. It's superior because it's going to not allow down to get wet, which again, as we talked earlier about that is the Achilles heel of down. If it gets wet, it's useless. And so why not use a treated DWR down seems like common sense,"},{"segment_id":"s-0090","start_seconds":2937.56,"end_seconds":2968.96,"text":"right? The more research I did and the more I actually talked to, um, some of the companies that, that we buy are down from, I became a little suspicious of the marketing around it and started to wonder, is this something that I have been successfully marketed as a necessity? Is it in what are the pros and the cons of treated versus untreated down? And what I learned from some of these companies that actually make the down, not the, not the companies that sell the product,"},{"segment_id":"s-0093","start_seconds":3036.92,"end_seconds":3068.66,"text":"look super lofty and plush. Well, to me it should, but what's it going to look like two years from now, after you put it in your pack, taking it out, put it in your pack, taking it out. Um, that's the true measure of a good quality bag. How is it going to re loft over time and treat treatments on down DWR treatments can have, can absolutely cause down clumping. That's when it just like sticks to each other and doesn't re loft, uh, very well. And so when you think about the temperature rating of a bag"},{"segment_id":"s-0094","start_seconds":3068.66,"end_seconds":3102.62,"text":"over the life of that bag, let's say it's an honest rating. Like it's an actual honest 20 degree bag. Well, after you use it, maybe a handful of times, maybe it's now a 25 degree bag because it's, it has treated down in it. Um, so that's the downside of treated down. Now there are, there is one coating that I'm aware of that doesn't, is not paraffin based. Now there may be others that I'm not aware of at this point in time, but that's, there's only one I'm aware of. And I don't have enough data in my personal experience yet. And what I've been able to, to test to say that it doesn't have the"},{"segment_id":"s-0097","start_seconds":3166.62,"end_seconds":3198.74,"text":"this, uh, 850 fill power down from our supplier. And I took exact same down one that was treated, one that was untreated. And we did a water submersion test. I took a vase of water, weighed the down before weight, uh, both sets it down. I took it in the water and I pushed it down and spun it in the water on a top with a timer. And I can't remember if I did two or three minutes, but whatever it is, like I just stirred it in water, right? Really trying to get that down soaking wet. And then I took it out, kind of like shook it out and then reweight it. And the interesting part, uh,"},{"segment_id":"s-0098","start_seconds":3198.74,"end_seconds":3231.98,"text":"that I found is that the treated down actually held onto a little bit more moisture than the untreated down. It weighed more than the untreated down. So what do I learn from that? Geese float and ducks float naturally. They, there are a lot of natural oils that are in down, uh, down feathers or down, um, that are naturally resistant to, uh, to, to holding on to water. And so do you need treated down is really the question. Are there, is it better to be safe than sorry? And I think better being safe"},{"segment_id":"s-0099","start_seconds":3231.98,"end_seconds":3263.64,"text":"than sorry. We have all been told that it's better to be safe than sorry. Right. But is it worth having a treated down, uh, in your, in your bag or your jacket? That's up to consumers to decide. We don't use treated down in our sleeping bags. And there are, um, I think two other very reputable companies in the sleeping bag space, Feathered Friends and Western Mountaineering, who also don't use treated down. And so the, the thing that, uh, so just on the moisture thing, what can really, the thing that"},{"segment_id":"s-0102","start_seconds":3328.32,"end_seconds":3360.02,"text":"uh, earlier this year and we had almost five days of just torrential dumping rain. And I had our sleeping bag untreated down and my bag was fine. Now, is there a situation where you could fall into a Creek and be tumbling down it for 15 minutes? I don't know. You're probably getting a helicopter ride out. You know what I mean? If that's at that point in time, uh, or, you know, there are situations where your bag can absolutely get wet. Um, but my point is, and I hope everybody understands is that the, the difference between treated and untreated down, there is a downside,"},{"segment_id":"s-0142","start_seconds":4673.32,"end_seconds":4683.74,"text":"we'll see you. We'll see you out there. Let's get down. Let's get down. Uh, yeah. Nope. Until next time, happy hunting and shooting. We'll catch on the next one. Bye everybody. See ya."}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:https://www.rokslide.com/?p=29810","version_id":"b72bd34adcc4b6911e3e801455d7eaa6fea305b1cadd452c3f94141b7921aabd","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"rokslide","title":"Gear Reviews: Lathrop Super Wides, Peak Refuel, KUIU Icon 6, and Patagonia Nano-Air","url":"https://www.rokslide.com/gear-reviews-lathrop-super-wides-peak-refuel-kuiu-icon-6-and-patagonia-nano-air/","published_at":"2026-10-05T12:00:45Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Same publisher as rokslide_feed. Gear review episodes (packs, layers, food)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, Freshness and novelty, and Making the decision, the new Icon 6 pack's measured weight, multi-day capacity limitations and reported rain performance, plus the Nano-Air Ultralight's winter hiking experience, provide citable evidence transferable to Mammoth and support Pieces 01–03. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, flag Lathrop & Sons' longstanding Rokslide boot sponsorship, the Patagonia employee connection used to order the jacket, Peak Refuel's manufacturer interview and embedded advertisements; attribute observations to this single publisher rather than treating them as independent corroboration, and omit unrelated hunting politics and promotional claims.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":37.46,"text":"the back of the heel, things like that. And the skin's thinner, and it's more prone to blistering. And when you really notice it, you won't notice it in an athletic shoe or a more flexible boot, right? It's when you get into footwear that does have structure. It does have some torsional stability to it. This season, Onyx Hunt is making it easier to stay connected"},{"segment_id":"s-0006","start_seconds":164.4,"end_seconds":194.9,"text":"So, our retailers are a super high priority for us when it comes to those options for our customers as well. Well, I think the thing about Peaks, too, is that you've really focused on what's important to, you know, the backpacker to the backcountry hunter, and that's, you know, keeping everything light, compact, and then not using a lot of water. Yes. So, our calories per ounce is actually higher than the industry average. So, you're getting a lot of calories for the weight that you're actually having to pack. You mentioned it, water, right?"},{"segment_id":"s-0019","start_seconds":596.92,"end_seconds":627.18,"text":"People definitely have their favorites. Yeah. That's just something that I've learned too, is that if you find something that you like, you better buy two of them and save one later. And you know, a mountain house lasts for 25 years or some ridiculousness. I mean, you guys don't go that long, but they do last. They have a five or six year shelf life for sure. Do you know offhand what they last typically? Yep. So our shelf life is 10 years and truthfully they're packaged to where they're fully freeze dried. They've gotten oxygen's over in there."},{"segment_id":"s-0030","start_seconds":955.64,"end_seconds":987.64,"text":"A lot of people still don't even realize it until they open the pouch. But we put two full buttermilk biscuits in there. A lot of people just put little kind of like, you know, croutons or tiny pieces. And by putting the two in there, you have to break them up first. But you can kind of choose how crunchy or how... Some people like crunched biscuits. I like mine in bigger pieces so that there's a little bit of that texture. But yeah, that very quickly became a top seller after we launched it. People absolutely love that one. People want to go check out some Peak Mills or reach out to your team there. Where can they find you guys at? They can go to peakrefuel.com."},{"segment_id":"s-0038","start_seconds":1204.92,"end_seconds":1238.26,"text":"So it's similar to that, but it's more kind of sculpted. And there's more sort of design features that are thrown into when the arc frame is. It certainly has design features, but it's kind of just a flat sheet that gets cut into the shape. This one has different channels and relief into it that that was all sort of put there, it seems, with a purpose. But another big change from the prior frames, the prior icon frames, is that they made it both taller and wider. And so I don't know if you've used any of those prior icons, Sam."},{"segment_id":"s-0042","start_seconds":1334.24,"end_seconds":1368.4,"text":"there. And I really like the 3500. I do a ton of day hunting in our area, especially for elk and the rifle season. But I do like to do overnights and multi-days. I just got back from a multi-day trip and I used the Icon 3500 and it just was, it was difficult to get all of the stuff I needed into that 3500 cube pack. But I mean, it worked. And I did some overnights during scouting season. And that worked fine, actually, for overnights. It was fine, especially for scouting because you don't have your weapon system and some"},{"segment_id":"s-0053","start_seconds":1691.92,"end_seconds":1722.22,"text":"Yeah, I am. I'm glad they did it, too. But it is if there's a give and take there for existing Kuyu customers for sure. All right. I'll link up your review and you have an ongoing thread on Rockside. People can reach out there with any questions and you can get back to them. Yeah. Sounds good, Sam. All right. Moving on. We're lucky enough to catch up with Mike Moore again, and he has the Patagonia Nano Air Review. And all of these super lightweight insulation pieces are just the fabrics that they keep coming out with are just amazing."},{"segment_id":"s-0056","start_seconds":1788.66,"end_seconds":1818.72,"text":"Patagonia. And he's the one that told me, he said, you're going to be pretty excited, you know. And I said, oh, is the Nano Air coming back out? And he goes, no, but there's a Nano Air Ultralight. It's got lighter insulation yet. And anyways, as quick as I found out, I had him order one up for me. Yeah, I was pretty excited about it and it hasn't disappointed at all. So why don't you tell us kind of how you use this piece? Because it's like a mid-layer, but it's ultralight."},{"segment_id":"s-0058","start_seconds":1854.8,"end_seconds":1885.36,"text":"for if you're sitting in class and eating lunch or whatever the heck, just bebop around camp. So it kind of ticks a couple different boxes. Yeah, this is like a nine-ounce jacket. So ultra lightweight, super compressible there. And just you can throw it in your pack. It's like one of those lightweight rain gear things that's just always in there, ready to go, whether you think you're going to need it or not. I got to use it on this winter, hiking almost daily on our local single tracks."},{"segment_id":"s-0067","start_seconds":2152.08,"end_seconds":2183.76,"text":"Stephen, are you there? Yes, sir. Thanks for having me, Robbie. You bet, man. Thanks for taking the time. So just a little history for our listeners. Lathrop & Sons, they've been the longest boot sponsor we've ever had on Rockslide. You guys started with us, I think, in 2012 or 13. Been here ever since. Really appreciate it. Get tons of feedback on the forum because you've been there for so long. I'm very familiar with everything that you guys do. But I had never tried your wides until this year. Robbie, that's a super wide we use."},{"segment_id":"s-0070","start_seconds":2246.94,"end_seconds":2279.42,"text":"I don't want to throw them under the bus. But, you know, they were a hard boot. And then, I think in 2022, I ran the Crispy Summit IIs GTX for a couple years. And they were fine. I went with the widest that they had. They were a little bit softer boot. They were more comfortable than the Canetrex. But they were still a little tight on the width. Then I made them work. And, you know, unless I did a big day or something. I wasn't getting blisters or anything like that. It was just, they were just snug. So I got a hold of Stephen this spring when they got their super wides in and just said,"},{"segment_id":"s-0072","start_seconds":2310.72,"end_seconds":2342.18,"text":"I had been buying tens the last four or five years and everything, tennis shoes and stuff like that. My foot has just gotten bigger. And so Stephen wanted me to go to a 10 and a half in these super wides, which I was still a little like, gosh, I'm going to be wearing a freaking clawed hopper here. So I got the boots. I've had them since July. And this is what I found. The other boots that I was wearing, they were still too tight. I didn't realize it until I got into your super wide. And it wasn't that there's slop in there at all."},{"segment_id":"s-0074","start_seconds":2374.0,"end_seconds":2404.08,"text":"I don't do 10 mile days, but, you know, I do three mile days. And I love them. They fit my feet so much better that I thought, man, I got to get Stephen on here. I know there's a lot of big footed guys out there that probably think if they're just buying a 4E and going up a half a size, they're doing fine. But Stephen, something you explained to me is that there's some technology behind the wides. They're not just a bigger boot, right? Right. Yeah, that's correct. Why don't you take that from there? So the big thing is..."},{"segment_id":"s-0081","start_seconds":2601.56,"end_seconds":2631.94,"text":"So they really have still got that performance fit, right? And a lot of these brands, they make them wide in the front. They're wide in the arch and they're wide in the heel. It's like just sliding their foot in it. And there's really no anatomical padding, this padding around the back to really hold that boot securely on the foot. And that's what you meant when you said, look, this isn't just a bigger boot. It's designed to still fit the foot everywhere it needs to fit and accommodate the super wide. Yeah."},{"segment_id":"s-0085","start_seconds":2732.0,"end_seconds":2771.66,"text":"But that's not always the best boot for that person, right? And that's why I wanted to pull you on here because I think there's a lot of guys that, and I was one of them, we used to buy them off the shelf when there's brick and mortar stores. Now it's even worse. We just buy them off of the internet. Right. And you can skate doing that. You can get by with that for years. And I did. I did. But that was why I wanted to bring people on this journey a little bit that while those other boots worked and they fit well enough, these super wides, because of going through the custom boot process and just the way you've built the super wides to still anatomically fit the foot, not just blow out a boot, make it bigger."},{"segment_id":"s-0087","start_seconds":2802.5,"end_seconds":2833.74,"text":"That there's a lot more that goes into this than just getting your boot off of what size you've been buying for the last 20 years. A hundred percent right. And we have some people that don't necessarily need to go through the custom process. They just want to pick up the phone and have a person actually try to help them. I mean, let's get real. $500 to $600 investment for a pair of boots is a lot. I don't know about you. I want to pick up the phone and talk to someone to kind of help guide me through it. And at that point, they may realize, you know what?"},{"segment_id":"s-0092","start_seconds":2960.46,"end_seconds":2995.8,"text":"Then there's the use. Like, if we have a job or our hobbies are really hard on our feet, orthopedic injuries, things like that, those can also cause flattening of the foot. But as we get older, we lose fat pad on the foot. Fat pad fills the void within a boot. So, a boot fit will change, right? And more than what we see is the elongation. The foot just, the joint's starting to settle. So, that's really what happens over time. And I know a lot of people will say, I'm just going to throw an arch support in the boot."},{"segment_id":"s-0129","start_seconds":4201.58,"end_seconds":4213.48,"text":"Music Music Music Music Music Music  "}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:huntbackcountry.podbean.com/d9e12821-70f0-3d9b-8475-5a569ae5530f","version_id":"35697010ceefae0df17bfe2aa16730cb71c03b46fb864f8ad9a141eae1715aa9","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"exomtngear","title":"MM 311 | Gear Q&A (Solo with Mark)","url":"https://huntbackcountry.podbean.com/e/mm-311/","published_at":"2026-10-05T15:30:41Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"commercial","notes":"Run by Exo Mtn Gear (pack maker); practitioner gear Q&A but promotes its own brand."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the recent 10-day Alaska trip supplies specific new practitioner observations on wet-weather insulation, merino durability, and boots after roughly a year of use that transfer to Mammoth conditions, supporting Pieces 01, 02, and 03; the packraft-supported extra gear limits carry-weight comparisons. Under Commercial incentives and Uncertainty, Exo Mtn Gear produces the podcast, the host received the Argali hoodie, and he reports no sponsorship, affiliate income, or kickbacks for the linked gear; treat these experiences as one practitioner's attributed evidence, not independent corroboration or established medical advice.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.26,"end_seconds":31.8,"text":"Welcome to the Monday Minute of the Hunt Backcountry Podcast. These are shorter and more informal episodes where we answer your listener questions. And today I am running solo to answer some of your listener questions. Steve is out and we had some questions, you know, really piling up in email, which is a great thing. But also some specifically either to me or just that I could talk about fully. Um, so I just thought, hey, Steve's out. Let's still do this Monday Minute and get to your questions."},{"segment_id":"s-0002","start_seconds":31.98,"end_seconds":66.18,"text":"Most of these will be gear related. Um, some of them are, I would say, gear specific on recommendations, but also there's like strategies on the best way to use gear or deal with certain conditions as well. Before we dive into the first one, if you have any questions for us for a future episode, send an email to podcast at exomountaingear.com. It'd be great to hear from you. We can cover anything from gear to tactics, strategy, whatever, whatever you want to hear. Let us know. Send that question in and we'll get to it on a future Monday Minute."},{"segment_id":"s-0003","start_seconds":67.14,"end_seconds":97.16,"text":"All right, let's just get into it. The first one's from Camden and he said, did you use down or synthetic on your recent Alaska hunt? And how about merino or synthetic? I'm curious what you choose for Alaska conditions in general, and if a trip with packrafting also influences your decision. Any other tips you have for staying warm and dry on a trip like that would be appreciated. Good questions, Camden."},{"segment_id":"s-0004","start_seconds":97.16,"end_seconds":133.3,"text":"And for context for listeners, we were in Alaska for 10 days at the beginning of September. And as Camden mentioned, it was a packraft trip. So we covered about 50 river miles, floated that basically in three different stretches, but then spent plenty of days not floating and just hiking and hunting. So he mentioned Alaska in general, and then also packrafting in particular, which is important to consider. You know, the more we've hunted Alaska, we just realized Alaska isn't Alaska."},{"segment_id":"s-0006","start_seconds":164.56,"end_seconds":195.2,"text":"And on my first read, I thought, well, I don't know if he's talking about clothing or sleep systems. So I'll touch on both. I think he's getting at clothing though, because he also mentioned merino or synthetic, which I'm assuming, you know, is for base layers. So sleep system, just to get that one out of the way, I took my Katabetic Alsec quilt, which is a down quilt. And I mean, if you guys have been listening to the podcast, I've been using this thing for years. Absolutely love it. It's pretty much what I always take on everything."},{"segment_id":"s-0010","start_seconds":291.56,"end_seconds":327.38,"text":"that we weren't always carrying on our back. We're pack rafting. And then also having this idea of, hey, we know we're going to leave the rafts and some extra gear at the river, even if we go backpack for a few days. And so it just created an opportunity to carry a bit more, much like you would if you were hunting with pack animals, for example, on a horseback hunt. So that is a unique aspect of just having the pack raft, as we did take a few extras with us. The synthetic piece that I took for the puffy was a Sitka Kelvin light."},{"segment_id":"s-0012","start_seconds":359.26,"end_seconds":391.62,"text":"But it's, as I mentioned, synthetic. So in my head, it was like, man, this would be a great layer for pack rafting when it's cold. And then also to take on the mountain as well. I'm just looking at the specs. It has 60 grams of insulation in the body, 40 grams of insulation in the sleeves, in the hood. And then basically the side panels and underneath your arms, isn't an insulated synthetic insulation."},{"segment_id":"s-0017","start_seconds":522.48,"end_seconds":552.78,"text":"and then trying to dry out wet insulation in the hours or days to come. It was nice. So it's pretty, pretty rare that I'm packing synthetic insulation either to sleep in or to wear. But this trip in particular, man, it was just the perfect choice to have that Kelvin light. Camden also mentioned merino versus synthetic. Again, I'm just assuming he's talking about"},{"segment_id":"s-0019","start_seconds":583.46,"end_seconds":614.1,"text":"over at Argali. And I just went to look it up and it must not be out yet. I thought it was, but it's not on his clothing page currently. So it must be coming. But I wore an Argali merino hoodie for the duration of the trip. And it was, it was really good. Um, it did not lose shape, which is a problem I've had with a bunch of merino pieces. It was not fragile. It didn't get any holes. It didn't get any pilling. Um,"},{"segment_id":"s-0022","start_seconds":676.7,"end_seconds":706.82,"text":"And then obviously merino itself, they're not all the same types and they're not all the same qualities. But, um, yeah, so warmer merino for a base top on this trip and it did stay, um, scent free, essentially. I mean, it didn't get funky or nasty. It was warm in the times that I got a bit wet. There was days that we were hiking and I kind of purposely was hiking without rain gear just because we were exerting"},{"segment_id":"s-0023","start_seconds":706.82,"end_seconds":737.16,"text":"and climbing and I would have been wet from the inside out if I was wearing rain gear and I purposely just stayed in that base layer top knowing I was going to get a little bit wet. Um, I did find that going back to having that synthetic puffy, if my base layer got damp, not maybe completely soaking wet but damp, my strategy was always when I stop hiking or moving, especially if we're fully stopping for a bit to glass, even if I'm not cold,"},{"segment_id":"s-0034","start_seconds":1044.84,"end_seconds":1074.98,"text":"the boot without the insoles yeah it definitely makes a difference we talked about didn't do it on this trip but doing more again it's rare we pack these boot dryers but on a hunt like this they're great and then next time we would also bring some hot hands packets and then try to accelerate okay now we can move some warm air through the boots with a hot hands packet and that would be even more effective so that's feet you know boot moisture and socks and I'll talk more about boots in a"},{"segment_id":"s-0035","start_seconds":1074.98,"end_seconds":1105.48,"text":"second because we had a question on it and then on hands obviously just thinking of gloves you know gloves are tricky with what type of glove what's the context do you need something with durability for thorns and brush not uncommon in Alaska do you need something with dexterity maybe maybe not depending on what you're doing can you go maximum warmth with something like a down glove because you're sitting in glassing but it doesn't have dexterity or durability so there's lots of"},{"segment_id":"s-0041","start_seconds":1259.14,"end_seconds":1289.68,"text":"feet they work in all conditions I never tape I never blister I never do anything they're awesome I have had some issues with durability on them and then mediocre to mixed customer service from Hanwag on an issue with durability it really goes back to 2023 I had a new pair of Oliver stones that I purposely had done minimal minimal use with because I don't find that for me these boots need much break-in and I took them on a sheep hunt backpack sheep hunt and they"},{"segment_id":"s-0044","start_seconds":1351.28,"end_seconds":1382.18,"text":"alternatives I haven't talked much on the podcast about it because I hadn't really found something that I loved until actually pretty recently the Mindel what they call their air revolution alpine light hiker it's a mouthful but if you look it's a Mindel alpine light hiker essentially has been a really good boot that I've used I used on this recent Alaska trip I used last spring"},{"segment_id":"s-0046","start_seconds":1412.4,"end_seconds":1444.18,"text":"know it's been great from a durability perspective for me the outsole's pretty good with grip the leasing systems really nice on it yeah it's been waterproof I mentioned earlier getting boots wet you know there's times where the boot itself was dry from an external boot perspective but because of going back to like my strategy I talked about hiking in rain gear there's times I didn't have it and then got wet pants and then the wet pants seep down and eventually"},{"segment_id":"s-0055","start_seconds":1688.64,"end_seconds":1719.3,"text":"slightest touch was almost non-existent of dealing with this problem because those insoles so I'll leave a link to the ones I use there's several out there I actually would say these insoles are not nearly as comfortable as a lot of standard insoles I've used like we've talked a lot about the hike cushion insoles from super feet they're not that comfortable so my feet do get more tired with these metatarsal insoles but it solves that Morton's neuroma issue for me so it's been worth the"},{"segment_id":"s-0064","start_seconds":1968.42,"end_seconds":1998.86,"text":"it should be to sharpen an edge that is beginning to lose its edge think of sharpening in the field as maintenance not like rehab right it's I'm gonna take care of my knife as I'm working on an animal not completely beat the crap out of my knife and then try and you know work a miracle in the field so if you do that and actually did use this sharpener on this recent Alaska trip for example and I killed one bear but you know we"},{"segment_id":"s-0067","start_seconds":2061.26,"end_seconds":2095.74,"text":"too it was genuinely really really good so check that out if you'd like for all this stuff I know I mentioned like the sharpening video I was leaving links to but pretty much all this gear I've mentioned for the most part everything from let's see the boots to the nitrile gloves to the boot dryers to the jackets whatever I'll leave links to all that stuff so check out the links in the description and as always nothing sponsored or affiliate or kickbacks or whatever just our genuine takes all right"},{"segment_id":"s-0077","start_seconds":2389.4,"end_seconds":2398.92,"text":"Monday minute question if you have a guest suggestion something we should do a full length episode on someone we should have on the show reach out let us know it'd be great to hear from you and we'll talk to you soon"}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:huntbackcountry.podbean.com/1321502f-db9d-3a78-abb1-6eadc982978b","version_id":"a2eb9b873e1beaeb2f6382b91dc1adce7bee16611d510f4ac2b66912a8aa4f42","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"exomtngear","title":"MM 310 | Hunting Burnout, Short Rifles, Tall Packs, & Bad Water","url":"https://huntbackcountry.podbean.com/e/mm-310/","published_at":"2026-09-28T15:46:55Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"commercial","notes":"Run by Exo Mtn Gear (pack maker); practitioner gear Q&A but promotes its own brand."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the newly reported listener experience of persistent bladder taste despite multiple cleaning attempts, contrasted with the hosts’ reported years of HydraPak use without that problem, supplies attributable field evidence relevant to multi-day hydration and supports pieces 01, 02, and 03. Commercial incentives and Conflicting evidence require identifying Exo Mtn Gear as a pack manufacturer promoting its own K4 frames and preserving the differing hydration experiences without treating the hosts as independent confirmation of pack performance; Exclusions rules out using the barrel-modification, chassis, and suppressor discussion.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":35.18,"text":"Welcome to the Monday Minute of the Hunt Back Country podcast. These are shorter and more informal episodes where we answer your listener questions. Steve, how is the knee feeling? We mentioned because of talking about the Alaska trip recently, you had knee issues, and I meant to ask you this offline, but I'll ask you now. Any progress with the knee? Subtle, yeah. So we had a boating accident-ish issue. I don't know how you would call that. Incident. Um, where Justin got pinned into a tree and then was getting flipped in his raft,"},{"segment_id":"s-0007","start_seconds":202.28,"end_seconds":234.16,"text":"But I just want to mention that and again, say thank you. And then we will, we plan to talk a bit more about the trip. So kind of a recap coming from that. It's some other great podcasts coming. We just, I was able to speak with the winner of the experience challenge about his sheep hunt last week, which we'll probably put that episode out this week. So a fun one coming there and just more interviews and a lot to come with the podcast. So thanks guys, as always, for the support with that."},{"segment_id":"s-0009","start_seconds":265.88,"end_seconds":298.08,"text":"I'll let you pick order-ish. We have one, semi-related to potential hunting burnout. Two, packs for tall people. Three, an issue with water bladders. And four, a question on rifle barrel length. What piques your interest first? Packs for tall people. All right. Shocker. I feel like we're on the wheel of fortune here. Packs for tall people. Tall people. 1,000 please, Mark. Guess what? It's the Daily Double. No way, that's Jeopardy, not wheel forger."},{"segment_id":"s-0010","start_seconds":298.58,"end_seconds":335.76,"text":"All right. So this question came through from Trent. He basically is saying he's 6'7\", 220. He's had a pack since 2022 from another hunting company. It doesn't feel like it performs well for his build. He basically is saying and looking at the market, it doesn't seem like there's too many packs on the market designed specifically for someone like him. It's 6'7\". And then he also says he's seeing a pack out there that has frame extensions, but he's worried"},{"segment_id":"s-0011","start_seconds":335.76,"end_seconds":371.0,"text":"about the, you know, reliability and performance of frame extensions under heavy load. So long story short, let's start here because you did ask us, will your 26.5 inch K4 frame be a good option for me? And what is a reasonable maximum height for someone with that 26.5 inch frame? I don't even know what torso measurement, to be honest with you. Like to throw a number on there. So just because you're 6'7\", does not necessarily mean you need a super tall frame."},{"segment_id":"s-0021","start_seconds":661.54,"end_seconds":692.62,"text":"go on where I wear a 25 inch frame. And there's certain hunts I go on where I wear a 26 and a half inch frame. And you can put 50 pounds on my back with either frame height and I'm not going to immediately necessarily feel a distinct black and white difference. And one isn't going to be right. And one's not going to be wrong. It's just some nuance over time, especially with time and especially with weight where you begin to feel the difference. But I do like what you said there of like, yes, it is frame height is about effective load"},{"segment_id":"s-0027","start_seconds":853.94,"end_seconds":884.42,"text":"of time, it becomes pretty much non drinkable just with a strong rubbery taste. He said, I've tried cleaning. I've tried lemon juice. I've tried vinegar. I've tried hot water. I've tried freezing. I've tried denture cleaning cat cleaning tabs, but water from my bladder still tastes terrible. What am I missing? And what do you guys do to prevent this? I haven't had this issue. I don't know. I would tell me that I would turn that like buy or call hydropack and return it."},{"segment_id":"s-0031","start_seconds":975.26,"end_seconds":1006.8,"text":"You know? So go to the source, contact hydropack. Hopefully they're just going to replace that thing for you or have a good answer for you. But, uh, in a situation like this, I'd hope they just would replace it for you. I, I haven't seen it be a common issue, not only in our experience, Steve, but I mean, we just know a ton of other hunters, um, who are using those bladders and hasn't been an issue. Uh, if for some reason there's something weird with hydropack, other good bladders out there, there's a company called CNOC, uh, C-N-O-C."},{"segment_id":"s-0033","start_seconds":1038.18,"end_seconds":1068.88,"text":"Hoser, platypus hoser three liter. It was a bladder I used to use and it was clear. And that had no, no added flavor at all. Um, and I remember it was either camelback or hydropack or maybe there's some other brand I'm confusing it with, but it was more of that kind of, it's like a different looking rubber material. Um, and yeah, they absolutely had taste, but the, said the last, I don't know, I haven't had that thought in a good while, man. Yeah. Of six, seven, eight years of like using these hydropacks and not on issue. Yeah."},{"segment_id":"s-0042","start_seconds":1347.66,"end_seconds":1380.06,"text":"Um, but also I'm out there for adventure, uh, and, and to make memories. Um, and I'm out there to bring meat home to the family. Uh, so those are, those are my why, you know, my reasons why I'm hunting. And maybe for this guy, he just needs to reevaluate that. Maybe like he might be more where I'm at. Like, I don't care if this buck is 180 or 220. I really, really, really don't care. Um, and I know that's, it's a, um, obviously that's trophy hunting is looked at in a lot of different ways,"},{"segment_id":"s-0056","start_seconds":1804.04,"end_seconds":1837.48,"text":"It's awesome. All right, guys. Oh, there's one more. This question was on barrel length. A recent question from Steven. And I somewhat wanted to answer it, but then also just point to a resource just for guys. Because this question does come up quite a bit. But Steven basically wrote in and said, on a recent Monday minute, I believe I heard you guys mention you were using a 16 inch barrel on one of your rifles. He said, I'm currently shooting a Tika. That's a 6.5 Creedmoor. And in the next year, I plan to get an airlock suppressor."},{"segment_id":"s-0058","start_seconds":1872.38,"end_seconds":1906.58,"text":"But there's potential in the future with more practice and developing skills as a shooter that I may consider to shoot out to 500. He said, with all that said, what considerations do you guys take when choosing a barrel length? And have you ever regretted going too short? We can talk about that in one second. Just want to quickly mention, I have an article that I wrote on this very topic, essentially to answer a different listener question that was almost identical. So in the article, and then also a podcast, it was podcast 519."},{"segment_id":"s-0060","start_seconds":1938.62,"end_seconds":1968.68,"text":"I mean, you even said it again in Alaska. Even though we've both been hunting with short barrels and suppressors and folding chassis for years at this point, you're still just like very organic. You're like, man, I just love this type of setup. Like, you know, like it's so nice. So do you have any regrets? No, zero. Absolutely zero regrets. There's no, I mean, from, I like that he mentioned the distances that he's going to shoot at. That's a huge part of all this, right?"},{"segment_id":"s-0062","start_seconds":2005.84,"end_seconds":2044.84,"text":"I think recently, you're now with the new stuff, you can go even shorter, maybe. Yeah, with basically like the changes with NFA and SBRs, more guys are going shorter because of the process of having a technical short barrel rifle is easier. Yeah, easier. Yes. Gotcha. In my mind, the reason I want a short barrel and a folding chassis is so when the rifle is on my pack, maybe a little bit like when it's in your hand, it's easier to maneuver, but it's also, it's in your hand and it's already easy to maneuver."},{"segment_id":"s-0063","start_seconds":2044.84,"end_seconds":2078.04,"text":"So whether it's 16-inch barrel or 22-inch barrel, like these aren't like make or break things. But if you're hunting really brushy country and, you know, I mean, anyone who's, you know, hunted Alaska, Kodiak, whatever, with a long barrel, long suppressor, it's freaking miserable. You get stuck in alders all day long. And so that was like the first, you know, catalyst of like, okay, I need something different. This, this is miserable. And I was like, like our first trip to Kodiak in 2018 or 19, I can't remember what it was. Very shortly after that, the next time we went back, so I had a freaking folding chassis and they'd also just kind of come out at that time."},{"segment_id":"s-0064","start_seconds":2078.04,"end_seconds":2112.84,"text":"But that solves that problem. Like, and so right now for me, I'm running my mountain gear chassis with a 16-inch barrel and the airlock, whether, you know, their five-inch suppressor, the gravity, zero gravity one on the six millimeter. And I have the nano on the 22. That is mounted on the side of the pack and it's like completely within the profile of the pack. It's not sticking up higher than my backpack. So with the, therefore it's not getting caught on more brush. So I had like a friend who's like chopped his 16-inch down to 12 already."},{"segment_id":"s-0066","start_seconds":2143.66,"end_seconds":2183.5,"text":"Going back to frame, my low lifters earlier discussion, um, that, uh, if I was short and run a 23-inch, half-inch frame, maybe I would want it a little bit shorter. Um, I, in my opinion for me and what the distance is that I shoot big game at, uh, none of it really has any impact on my ability to make clean ethical shots and, and, you know, accurate shots, whether it's 20, 16, or 12 inches. Yeah. Yeah. Yeah, I agree. I don't have any, any regrets. I mean, in the past, uh, 10 years, I've gone from 24-inch barrel to a 22-inch barrel to a 20-inch barrel to an 18-inch barrel and also have a 16-inch barrel."},{"segment_id":"s-0070","start_seconds":2286.78,"end_seconds":2319.54,"text":"there are other things that we didn't accommodate for in this conversation. With length, for example, like density altitude, it can make a difference if you're hunting at sea level versus 5,000 feet versus 10,000 feet. So just, you know, if you want the best broad picture of truly calculating what type of speeds do I need at the muzzle for this bullet, for this, you know, downrange impact velocity, your max hunting distance, and then equating that to barrel length and how short can you go."}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:huntbackcountry.podbean.com/cff3484d-abad-3a86-9780-b1bc5cf1082b","version_id":"58a0ba0769f7493d632458c30030e80d2cebd25d2409a19500054d9538e936ee","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"exomtngear","title":"MM 309 | Life is too short. Solo Gear. 22 Creed. Ranging Elk.","url":"https://huntbackcountry.podbean.com/e/mm-309/","published_at":"2026-09-21T21:01:05Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"commercial","notes":"Run by Exo Mtn Gear (pack maker); practitioner gear Q&A but promotes its own brand."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Freshness and novelty, and Making the decision, the recent Alaska experience adds citable observations about sharing a spotter and shooting supports and about pack-buckle retention, supporting Pieces 01–03. Under Credibility, Commercial incentives, and Uncertainty, Exo's reported K4X versus YKK slip angles of approximately 38 versus 15 degrees are manufacturer test claims, not independent confirmation; the hosts sell these packs and disclose frequently receiving free gear or trades. Under Exclusions and Relevance, omit the firearm, ammunition, chassis, and hunting-tactics discussion.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.4,"end_seconds":36.04,"text":"Welcome to the Monday Minute of the Hunt's Backcountry podcast. These are shorter and more informal episodes where we answer listener questions. Steve, we're pretty close to being on the phone for two hours already. When the whole purpose we got on the phone was to record a Monday Minute, we're finally getting around to it. Yeah, lots to catch up on. Yeah, we are back from Alaska and other things. I actually had an email recently Andy wrote in and said, Hey, I was just wondering if the podcast is on hold or is having issues. I haven't seen a new podcast since late August."},{"segment_id":"s-0011","start_seconds":332.04,"end_seconds":362.16,"text":"And then you get that news and it, it hits different for sure. Yeah. I've, it's been interesting. And, you know, I told, I honestly didn't want to tell my wife, um, just cause she's not like a over the top warrior by any means, but you know, she's used to it at this point when I go and do things. Uh, but she's still, you know, in the back of your mind, it's like, that's there. Uh, and I didn't want to tell her, but ultimately don't, uh, I don't keep things from her either. So I told her and, um, she was super kind and understanding."},{"segment_id":"s-0022","start_seconds":707.28,"end_seconds":741.6,"text":"Um, and if you have a story or anything like that, um, feel free to share it. We, it's rewarding to say the least to, to see all that from you guys. An interesting question, um, from John. I think it was just our last Monday minute, which has been a little while now, but we were talking, Steve, about game bags, game bag choice, bone in versus deboning, et cetera. John wrote a response to that one, um, and said that he recently listened to that podcast about game bags. And we mentioned that any pack out over three miles, we would debone the meat."},{"segment_id":"s-0029","start_seconds":947.34,"end_seconds":985.32,"text":"You just have to be really intentional to do it no matter where they're your bone in or bone out, but, um, totally possible there. Yeah. All right. Joel had a question. I don't want to use this as like a, a full on deep dive Steve, uh, into this topic, but it was a recent question. You have a little bit more of experience. Uh, even recently Joel asked, he said, I'm considering a 22 creed. Um, my short answer for me personally, and I'll let you answer Steve."},{"segment_id":"s-0031","start_seconds":1017.1,"end_seconds":1049.16,"text":"I mean, it's such a, such an easy shooter, enjoyable shooting gun. And I planned to take the 22 creed to this recent trip to Alaska for myself personally. What I somewhat ran into is here in a few weeks, I'm headed to Colorado on a mule deer hunt. And, um, you cannot use a 22 creed. You cannot use a 22 caliber centerfire rifle in Colorado legally. And I just personally didn't want to have to worry about changing over rifles or loads or what have you during the fall."},{"segment_id":"s-0032","start_seconds":1049.16,"end_seconds":1088.2,"text":"So I just chose to, um, take my six creed to Alaska and then I'm going to take it to Colorado here in a few weeks. So I just want to bring that up one for me personally, but it is a point just for folks to consider if they don't fully haven't looked at this yet. If you may be hunting multiple States or you're looking at 22 creed is like a primary rifle. Just know that it isn't actually legal for all big game in all States. And then I would say after shooting the 22 creed, um, quite a bit, also having the six creed. I just feel again, if this is like a more of a intentional, not even one and done gun, but say you're like only gonna have one smaller caliber rifle."},{"segment_id":"s-0034","start_seconds":1125.46,"end_seconds":1156.32,"text":"And there's other, like you can take this rabbit hole far and you know, 22 creed is going to have a bit less barrel life. I've talked with, I haven't seen this myself as much, uh, but I've talked with quite a few people that I know and trust and have a lot more experience than I do. That would say they're more finicky in terms of loading, um, different things like that. So that's a quick take on the topic, Steve, but, um, you've been shooting one a bit, just used it in Alaska a bit. What is your, again, this isn't meant to be a deep dive, but what's your just current thoughts?"},{"segment_id":"s-0036","start_seconds":1186.56,"end_seconds":1217.26,"text":"We just get to try a lot of stuff and then provide honest feedback. Um, and I would never have built a 22 creed, uh, if the, I basically got the rifle for free. Like the action was free. I don't know. Maybe not free. We did trade. I believe we trade defiance, a couple of packs, like somebody from there reached out and said, Hey, I want to get a couple of packs. Do you want to trade for an action? And I'm like, yeah, sure. That sounds great. Um, and then we got to the barrel for free. The chassis was from mountain gear, um, out of New Zealand. They just did their new chassis."},{"segment_id":"s-0037","start_seconds":1217.26,"end_seconds":1251.74,"text":"And he gave me, uh, when he came up for shot show, he gave me the sample. Cause I think it was like harder for him to try to bring a rifle chassis back home than it was just to leave it with me. Um, so essentially got this gun for free. I absolutely love my six creed. Um, that thing shot lights out, killed quite a few animals with it. This point performance has been fantastic. Um, you and Tyler were both shooting the one to wait, you know, the M there in Alaska and the performance on caribou and bear was, I mean, I have so much confidence in that six creed is like, just take it anywhere in North America, pointed at what you're aiming at,"},{"segment_id":"s-0043","start_seconds":1409.52,"end_seconds":1441.46,"text":"If you're more deeter and occasional elk go with the 22 that that's just a me throwing out some random thing. Like I need more, more time with it. Um, it's a lighter gun, so I don't think I'm shooting it as accurately. Um, in my head going like with my six creed 600 yards all day long, shot that thing lights out this gun. I just hadn't, it was so busy this year. I, you know, it was the opposite of what we preach and like get out and practice and shoot a bunch. I mean, I, I barely got to shoot at all. Um, and most of that was a shorter distances."},{"segment_id":"s-0046","start_seconds":1503.42,"end_seconds":1535.3,"text":"I've got more experience with it. And I, and I haven't, I built this 22 lighter. I will say, um, it, a six pound gun and that mountain gear chassis, a 16 inch barrel, got the airlock nano on there. Uh, it's, I, it's, I packed a rifle around almost every day. I think there's one day I didn't when we, when we went and killed your and Justin's bears. Um, cause like, oh, I'm not hunting today. You guys are the hunters. We don't, you know, we're not gonna kill three animals today. Um, but packing that thing around, it's like, it's, it doesn't exist on the backpack anymore."},{"segment_id":"s-0047","start_seconds":1535.92,"end_seconds":1565.96,"text":"Um, it's so compact and short and light that it's, it's almost like packing a toy around, you know, like you just don't, you don't have this big, heavy rifle that's sticking down below the pack or up above the pack. It's kind of non, you know, it's like going from packing like a big, heavy 90 millimeter spotter down the pack and a 55. Like it's just like two completely different things. Um, and not, not to say that my six creed is heavy or big or bulky. It wasn't. Um, but if you'd like compare like a traditional rifle to now put into super lightweight folding"},{"segment_id":"s-0051","start_seconds":1663.44,"end_seconds":1694.72,"text":"is what, what we set out to achieve. All right. This next question came through from Lauren. He said, I'm getting ready to leave for my first solo back country hunt, uh, for context, he's in Washington. He's doing a hunt there. He said it wasn't supposed to be solo, but everyone backed out. But at this point I'm committed to going. He said, I've been planning for this hunt for a year, training for it and slowly buying all new gear from pack to boots to rifle to basically everything."},{"segment_id":"s-0052","start_seconds":1695.34,"end_seconds":1727.4,"text":"He said, but now that I'm looking at going completely, completely second. Sorry. Now that I'm looking at completely going solo, it has me second guessing a few things. My main question is what changes in your pack when you're hunting solo versus with a buddy? Um, he mentioned things like his first aid kit's pretty minimal. Does he need to carry more? Does he need to carry other emergency gear? Does he need to carry more food than it normally would? Does he leave a spotter behind to save weight considering the potential of a solo pack out?"},{"segment_id":"s-0053","start_seconds":1727.9,"end_seconds":1759.52,"text":"And he basically summarizes and says, where do you add weight because you're alone? And where do you cut weight because there's nobody to split gear with? So what changes? Yeah. Um, nothing for me. Um, Oh, look, it's such a great question. The answer is so boring. No, I mean, you go back 15, 20 years ago, gear was heavier. Uh, absolutely. When I'm hunting with a partner, we're packing, I guess nothing's not correct."},{"segment_id":"s-0054","start_seconds":1759.52,"end_seconds":1792.08,"text":"Cause like on this hunt, you packed a bipod, I packed a tripod for rifle support. Um, I think that's the only difference though. Uh. Yeah. I mean, your average hunt that you and I go on in Idaho, like call it, cause he's in Washington. So we're not talking Alaska. We're not talking expedition. We're not talking like remote fly and stuff. We're just keeping this like lower 48 year backpack hunting. I do think the answer is nothing. Yeah. Yeah. You said 15, 20 years ago, gear was heavier. We absolutely, like we would pack a stove. Um, we would, I don't know."},{"segment_id":"s-0073","start_seconds":2370.62,"end_seconds":2401.56,"text":"hunt archery elk every year. Do you want to shoot more elk or do you want to kill and recover more elk? That's the question. And ranging is going to help you do one and not do the other. Yeah. Yeah. I completely agree. All right. Let's wrap it there, Steve. We will be back. We said this before, but we will with more Monday minutes, more podcasts. We've got some great stories scheduled with listeners. We've got Brian Sheep Hunt who won the experience challenge. We're talking with him. We will get around to sharing the story of our Alaska trip."}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:7e32a443-4fec-4930-bdac-c1911c5a8810","version_id":"8271205a20e99a87b6c2beb4725e3e471cb753db450b92458a25320a0723aa87","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"backcountry_hunting_podcast","title":"Essential Hunting Gear: Backcountry Backpack Hunts","url":"https://backcountryhunting.libsyn.com/essential-hunting-gear-backcountry-backpack-hunts","published_at":"2026-10-02T05:59:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Gear lists for multi-day backpack trips and optics. Publisher affiliation and sponsorships not verified."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Making the decision, the transferable backpacking material provides specific practitioner evidence: a Copper Spur UL3 surviving four days of Alaskan wind and rain, sleeping-bag zipper usability when cold and exhausted, and a shooting bag serving as support, pillow, and seat. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require attributing these single-source observations, noting the explicit Leupold sponsorship, Swift promotional segment, and retailer friendship, while recording the stated absence of Stone Glacier sponsorship and leaving other relationships unverified. Exclusions bars using the firearm, ammunition, and suppressor reviews; the substantive eligible material supports Piece 02 on field performance and Piece 03 on weight, cost, and optional gear, without establishing Mammoth requirements.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":36.02,"text":"If you're hunting out of a backpack, what are the absolutely essential items that you need in there, in this bit of equipment to carry you through, whether it's a night or 10 days worth of expedition-style hunting in the backcountry? That's what we're here to talk about today, my friends. And I have my daughter, Audrey, who spent two weeks in New Zealand in the high country with me this year, earlier this year. And she's going to actually conduct the interview. We're going to have a little bit of fun with this, and she's got a list of questions she's going to ask about how to set yourself up with the right backpack"},{"segment_id":"s-0008","start_seconds":226.42,"end_seconds":256.46,"text":"What makes a backpack truly capable for backcountry hunting, particularly when it has to transition from carrying your gear to carrying tons of meat? Mm. Okay. This is a very good question. And this, by the way, we're addressing this episode toward the DIY hunter. However, if you're guided, let's face it, in most cases, your guide or his pack animals are going to carry your meat out. I have a thing for carrying my antlers or horns out myself. I always do that."},{"segment_id":"s-0019","start_seconds":601.26,"end_seconds":636.98,"text":"This particular pack has become my go-to for expedition-type hunting. This is by Stone Glacier, and I believe the model is the Terminus, and it's a 7,000-plus cubic inch pack. You can see it opens up way big, right? It's got a big opening up the top. It's got a zipper down one side. It's got a couple of pockets, exterior pockets for gear as well as a hood. You can flip over the top, fill that with gear. This entire thing, including the frame, weighs less than four pounds, which is unbelievable."},{"segment_id":"s-0020","start_seconds":637.12,"end_seconds":672.86,"text":"That's about what that, I think that Mystery Ranch pack actually weighs a tad more. It's an expensive pack, the Stone Glacier is. It's very, very high-tech fabric and construction in the frame and so forth that enables it to accomplish that ultra-light weight. Just for reference, the internal frame pack that I grew up hunting with weighed nine pounds, something like nine and a half pounds. This is less than half that starting up the mountain. That means I can take five pounds more food. Great waist belt, great pack, very comfortable."},{"segment_id":"s-0023","start_seconds":742.3,"end_seconds":775.24,"text":"And that's my kitchen, right? Yeah. While we're on the subject of kitchen spoons. Are the single most, I'm convinced, this is the single most lost item in the backcountry. And the longer you're going, the sooner you will lose it. I've proven this many times. So I tend to take two. And all this, though this one's a beautiful titanium high-grade deal with like a little serrated edge you can use for, you know, cutting meat and all that. This is spork, spoon and fork. I like bright colored ones because I'm less likely to lose them."},{"segment_id":"s-0024","start_seconds":775.24,"end_seconds":809.98,"text":"Yeah, bright orange. Yes, oranges, reds, yellows, things like that. But I digress. What's your next question, Audrey? Okay, so once you've got the pack, what are the pieces of equipment that you consider absolutely non-negotiable for backcountry hunting? And that's the crux of the matter right here, folks, right? You're very unlikely to forget your backpack when you're going into the backcountry because that's kind of where you put all your gear as you start to head out, right? So let's just start with something that doesn't necessarily go in your pack, but I have forgotten and I know that people have forgotten."},{"segment_id":"s-0028","start_seconds":916.68,"end_seconds":952.08,"text":"But if you're a pretty savvy woodsman, you can usually find a place under a tree, under a root gnarle, or under a ledge in the rocks or something where the snow and rain isn't getting, and you can hunker in there with your sleeping bag. So those two are debatable. We'll treat them both of equal value. There are a lot of good sleeping bags on the market. The one that has become my go-to is also by Stone Glacier. By the way, we're not sponsored by them in any way. I just like their gear. So backpack and sleeping bag that are my go-tos now are Stone Glacier."},{"segment_id":"s-0041","start_seconds":1355.5,"end_seconds":1391.8,"text":"And although there are a lot of good ones out there, when getting a combination of penetration and a massive wound cavity is crucial. The near-complete weight retention and deep-driving nature of Swift's bonded core bullets beat all challengers. Which is the best of the best? Of course, that depends on whether you're hunting dangerous game or not. But in my experience, no other bullet showcases the versatility of bonded core bullets as well as Swift's new state-of-the-art Zephyr. Designed to provide reliable on-impact performance from spitting distance all the way out to 1,000 yards or more,"},{"segment_id":"s-0043","start_seconds":1423.82,"end_seconds":1467.78,"text":"Swift has engineered Zephyr bullets for nearly all calibers from .224 up to .338. These Z-bullets, as they're nicknamed, are heavy for caliber projectiles that hold on to velocity and energy and buck the wind extremely well. Weight retention on impact is superb, averaging around 88%. It's worth noting that Zephyr bullets do require fast-twist rifling, such as one turn every 8 inches to stabilize properly. Zephyrs are perfect for the PRC and Creedmoor cartridge families, as well as the 7mm backcountry, 6.8 Western, and any other rifle chambered for a classic cartridge with a fast-twist custom barrel."},{"segment_id":"s-0044","start_seconds":1467.78,"end_seconds":1500.68,"text":"Swift bullets are legendary in Africa and the far north, where dangerous game specialists rely on Swift A-frames when putting their lives on the line. It's impossible to give a bullet a better endorsement than that. The company ethos of quality and performance is embedded into the Zephyr, resulting in a projectile that is arguably the finest hunting bullet ever designed. Check them out at swiftbullets.com. So, backpack, sleeping bag, tent."},{"segment_id":"s-0045","start_seconds":1500.84,"end_seconds":1531.86,"text":"The next thing that I'm going to add is kind of a non-crucial item, unless you're old with a bad back and bad shoulder like me, right? And that's a good sleeping pad. Yeah. I like the air mattresses that you can blow up because they roll down real small and they're lightweight. There are a lot of good ones. You can use a Big Agnes. You can use, what's the, there's a Swedish company that's really good. I'm trying to remember the name, but I'm blanking. Anyway, a good air mattress."},{"segment_id":"s-0067","start_seconds":2215.72,"end_seconds":2246.88,"text":"those. It'll work for days on end whenever you put it on. You don't have to always keep reapplying DEET or something like that. So mosquito net can be nice to have. Some firearms tools. This is a little kit assembled by Leupold that I'm looking at right now. But it's actually a fix-it sticks kit with all the items necessary to reset your scope, to torque the screws on your rifle's action, whatever. Tighten screws on your bipod or tripod if they're coming loose."},{"segment_id":"s-0074","start_seconds":2443.26,"end_seconds":2476.8,"text":"I've worn synthetic base layers, some of the really expensive stuff. And man, I can get ripe in a single afternoon. The wool, I can go for days before I start smelling myself, right? It's just the best thing. God gave us an optimal material. Let's use it. Thank you, sheep. Merino wool. Next, I put on a really good layer of high-performance hunting clothing. So pants and shirt. It's a proper set that I'm going to do most of my hunting in. If the weather gets real hot, I'll take off my base layers and keep hunting in that."},{"segment_id":"s-0089","start_seconds":2963.5,"end_seconds":3004.02,"text":"Yeah. While you're dressing or boning off or quartering off your animal. Yeah. It can't hurt to have some gauze with a blood clotting agent in it. That's really good. A roll of medical tape. I like Leuko Tape, L-E-U-K-O, the best because it's so durable. You can bind a sole to a boot that's coming off and it'll hold for a couple days with this stuff. It's incredible. You can put a patch on a blister and it'll stay for the duration of your hunt. Leuko medical tape is awesome stuff. Yeah. Splints, I don't worry about because I can take a leg off of a bipod or tripod and use some clothing and some paracord, whatever, and create a splint."},{"segment_id":"s-0097","start_seconds":3237.48,"end_seconds":3273.62,"text":"And in the backcountry, when the going gets hard and all odds seem against you, do as Leupold's tagline suggests, be relentless. What's one piece of gear you've learned through experiences worth carrying, even though it might look unnecessary? Oh, man. That's a good one and a tough one. So basically, something I would always take, even though a lot of people don't think of it,"},{"segment_id":"s-0114","start_seconds":3805.84,"end_seconds":3837.82,"text":"So, once you've got a really good scope on a really good rifle, mounted in good quality, lightweight mounts. These are Leupold Backcountry mounts, right? It's one-piece base and ring. Then you need a few good quality accessories. We'll add that level bubble into an accessory level. Funny enough, a suppressor now has joined that list. For me, for a while, it was kind of a luxury. Then it became something that I really, really liked and kind of didn't want to go without. Now, here, hold that for me."},{"segment_id":"s-0121","start_seconds":4045.1,"end_seconds":4075.1,"text":"But folks, if you're going into the backcountry in, say, Alaska or New Zealand or something like that for a week to 10 days, you cannot risk your gear filling on you. Certain items. So I guess that's what we're talking about here. I'm going to leave the boots. That's kind of an obvious one and it isn't directly related to your backpack. But we'll just give them an honorable mention. Without good boots, you ain't traveling in the backcountry so your hunt's shot. It's done, right?"},{"segment_id":"s-0155","start_seconds":5169.08,"end_seconds":5172.88,"text":"I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. I'm here with the one. you as I'm here with the one."}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:9bb5a7b6-3598-4ba5-bbe3-b1ca9d4f6e59","version_id":"a7a078fa5d4d0db2c4eda4f6e785f1b6c5f715b4c281af9d2d46799bc4d8d1c1","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"backcountry_hunting_podcast","title":"Best Rifle Scopes for Backcountry Hunting: American Made & Tougher Than The Rest!","url":"https://backcountryhunting.libsyn.com/best-rifle-scopes-for-backcountry-hunting-american-made-tougher-than-the-rest","published_at":"2026-09-18T16:35:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Gear lists for multi-day backpack trips and optics. Publisher affiliation and sponsorships not verified."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, the newly reported VX4 field use, described glare comparison, and scope failure after 28 shots offer attributable evidence about optics whose weight and reliability transfer to Mammoth conditions. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require identifying the host's disclosed Leupold partnership and the guest's Leupold engineering role: their praise is not independent confirmation, and factory durability, tracking, and comparative weight claims remain manufacturer claims pending independent testing. This supports Pieces 01 and 02; under Exclusions, omit the ammunition and firearm promotions and all purchase links.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":35.86,"text":"Of all the equipment that you can carry into the backcountry, it makes sense, logically, that your scope would be the most fragile, but it doesn't really seem to be that way anymore. These things are built of glass and finely tuned gears, moving parts. It's an instrument like a fine camera lens, and yet they're so durable, it seems like you can just about pound railroad spikes with some of the good new ones these days. Today, folks, we're going to talk about the ins and outs, what really makes a premium scope what it is. Everything from optical quality"},{"segment_id":"s-0005","start_seconds":134.6,"end_seconds":169.66,"text":"answered that for me. I'm going to throw you a bit of a curveball here. What type of rifle scopes did you grow up hunting with and shooting? And how do they compare to what you're using now? Yeah, I grew up in a family that did a little bit of hunting, but in a very rural spot and right on the border of Oregon and Washington in the Cascades. And my first hunting trip, I was given a rifle and scope to use for a black tail hunt. And it was a loophole rifle scope on top of a 30 odd six."},{"segment_id":"s-0007","start_seconds":200.24,"end_seconds":233.7,"text":"it was always recommended like, hey, this is this is a brand that you can trust. This is a brand that performs day in and day out and was born in these mountains as well. And so kind of definitely got to experience that same type of atmosphere of of the birthplace of these rifle scopes and Leupold and Stevens doing this. But went off to engineering school. And then my first thought was, man, how can I combine this engineering background that I got with something like optics?"},{"segment_id":"s-0011","start_seconds":331.48,"end_seconds":366.8,"text":"what I wanted to put together. And yeah, I needed something that was rugged again, hunting out in the Northwest is no joke. So you got to have good rain gear and you got to have good equipment, you know, lots of, lots of stainless metal, lots of synthetic stocks and things was my first choice. And then absolutely a gold ring on there. I knew we'd get the job done. Yep. Well, let's dive right into some technical discussions here. One of the biggest, I guess, subjects that you hear bandied about on the internet is scope turret tracking. And I've long,"},{"segment_id":"s-0012","start_seconds":366.8,"end_seconds":400.5,"text":"said that I think that the scopes probably track a lot more consistently than the shooters behind them do. But, you know, there, there are scope tracking elements that ensure consistency. So if you wouldn't mind, take a minute, just teach us about the inner workings of Leupold's primary scope lines. And then maybe we can get a recommendation from you that works well for the really obsessive long range hunters among us. Cause I guess that's really where it, you know, becomes crucially important."},{"segment_id":"s-0017","start_seconds":535.1,"end_seconds":566.98,"text":"in the differences between your base, you know, let's call them your entry-level scopes, your freedom line and so forth. And then the VX3, the VX5 and the VX, well, now they have the four as well. And then the VX6, just in your hunting scopes. And I mean, we can throw in a discussion of your more precision, tactical, if you will, scopes as well, but how much difference is there inside? Yeah, from the differences overall, there'll be significant ones, you know, first and foremost,"},{"segment_id":"s-0026","start_seconds":829.06,"end_seconds":861.08,"text":"It's when you're out hunting, hopefully you can make that one perfect shot and that completes your season. But you certainly are carrying this thing around up and down mountains and everything else. And so those ounces matter. And when you compare this to, you know, many other hunting rifle scopes, that's absolutely a statistic, you know, people should look at is just what is the overall weight of the scope. And that's not just carrying it, but it's also just how it feels on top of a rifle, right? If you're saving ounces on your gun underneath it, if a scope's pretty heavy on top, it can kind of feel a little lopsided, right?"},{"segment_id":"s-0034","start_seconds":1092.98,"end_seconds":1129.56,"text":"And then I'll choose either a 44 or that 50 millimeter objective, but in that VX5 3 to 15. And those are just the workhorses. You're speaking my language there. The more I learn about hunting bullets and on-game terminal performance, the more I've come to preferred bonded core designs. And although there are a lot of good ones out there, when getting a combination of penetration and a massive wound cavity is crucial. The near-complete weight retention and deep-driving nature of Swift's bonded core bullets beat all challengers."},{"segment_id":"s-0042","start_seconds":1376.6,"end_seconds":1418.24,"text":"So a lot of the metal actually affects that image quality as well. So that's kind of my point there. But yeah, when you say, yeah, run-of-the-mill American scope, that really is a loophole, right? All the other scopes generally come out of the same factory in Asia. I've been in those factories, seen them slap on the different label and different turrets onto basically the same optical prescription. That is something that absolutely we dwell on day in and day out in the engineering department is how do we take the best optical design and deliver an overall scope that kind of has that ultimate performance."},{"segment_id":"s-0044","start_seconds":1454.44,"end_seconds":1486.0,"text":"What are their material types? What's their curvature, and where are they spaced within that, right? So we design that prescription, and there'll be, you know, 10 different overall pieces of glass and a rifle scope that many people don't realize. You know, you see the two in the end, but you don't realize how many are actually in there doing quite a bit of different types of work through that system. And then we pass it off to the design team, and they kind of go back and forth with the optics team."},{"segment_id":"s-0050","start_seconds":1654.04,"end_seconds":1690.18,"text":"I don't know how they figure that out, but they always seem to do that. I think that's an instinct they get with their mama's milk, you know. That's right. Absolutely. Kind of funny. Teach us a little bit about coatings and how those actually affect. And even if it's just, you know, keeping it simple, number of layers and how many surfaces are coated. Because I know a lot of companies don't coat all the surfaces. Absolutely, yeah. Yeah, a lot of, you know, just about anybody will coat maybe the outer part, you know, of your objective and then maybe the eyepiece."},{"segment_id":"s-0066","start_seconds":2209.68,"end_seconds":2244.6,"text":"We shot game with it. One shot kills out to 725 yards. It was really, really impressive, that optic was. And that's a perfect spot, you know, right in that, you know, if you're wishing you could have that VX5 to get up to that 3 to 15, like we'd mentioned, right? But that, just a 3 to 12, you know, a lot of people won't necessarily miss that 12 to 15 type of power. And really, the performance of the VX4 in that range is perfect, right, if you're kind of looking at an alternative to things in that scope line."},{"segment_id":"s-0071","start_seconds":2384.02,"end_seconds":2415.76,"text":"These are hand-built by the same gunsmiths that build all of Nighthawk Customs' fine firearms. The two are actually owned by the same company. In case you need a new premium scope or whatnot to go with your rifle, Porter's carries Leupold, Nightforce, Swarovski, Revik, Vortex, and so on. Now, best of all, mention that you're a Backcountry Hunting Podcast listener, and Porter's will give you listeners discounts. They'll give you bundle discounts if you need a scope as well, and they'll give you free shipping."},{"segment_id":"s-0077","start_seconds":2578.28,"end_seconds":2617.38,"text":"And they're dropping them off a second-story balconies and just doing some weird stuff to them. I've broken just about every optic brand that's made. I've been doing this for 30 years now, and I'm hard on gear because I'm testing it. I don't intentionally do mean things to it, but it seems like I'm, you know, our old friend Murphy follows me around and helps me damage stuff. And no matter how hard you try, sometimes you cannot protect your optic, but there are reasonable tests, and there are tests that actually evaluate what a scope is going to encounter for the most part."},{"segment_id":"s-0078","start_seconds":2617.38,"end_seconds":2651.86,"text":"And I sometimes think people running all these weird tests or just trying to break scopes and then draw conclusions because they get clicks off of broken scopes, right? The only Leupold I've ever broken was a 2.5 to 8 by 32, I think it was, 36, on a 460 Weatherby Magnum. This was a friend's rifle, and he brought the scope and the rifle to me and said, mount this for me."},{"segment_id":"s-0098","start_seconds":3330.56,"end_seconds":3364.22,"text":"That's what it was a U.S. manufacturer, American made. That's who we're competing against. Right. And so they definitely have us on their sites. They're doing the same thing. I'm sure the VX4 came out there. They're buying those. They're tearing them down and they're trying to understand how they reverse engineer this and reverse engineer it. Right. So you always got to stay ahead of them. But that's it's a great privilege to be able to do that. That's why I got into this. I'd studied manufacturing engineering and just loved engineering getting into it. And I just have always had a passion for building things here."},{"segment_id":"s-0112","start_seconds":3789.96,"end_seconds":3823.88,"text":"I'd pull them out. They've got a gold ring on them. And when I realized that, you know, I had this moment. It shouldn't have been an epiphany, but it kind of was. I looked in there and I thought, clearly, this is what I trust. It's what I favor naturally when I'm hunting without an assignment. You know, I'm taking a personal rifle. I'm going out with a scope that I trust. They're American-made. It's a great brand. I've got good friends at Leupold. And so I guess that's just a long-winded way of saying I consider it a real privilege and honor to be partnered with Leupold on the podcast."},{"segment_id":"s-0115","start_seconds":3895.84,"end_seconds":3903.42,"text":"Dustin, thanks once again for joining us. It's been a real privilege. Joseph von Benedict with the Backcountry Hunting Podcast. And we'll see you out there."}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:Buzzsprout-19950435","version_id":"c18ee5b38eabe522aad66e7e277c6378137aa3e88ada3b43ebe9bf9d8b0583c6","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"prs","title":"What the Data Says Actually Matters in Precision Rifle Shooting","url":"https://www.buzzsprout.com/2605409/episodes/19950435-what-the-data-says-actually-matters-in-precision-rifle-shooting.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-12T20:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Precision Rifle Series league podcast; league has many industry sponsors (e.g. Leica optics episode)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, Zant's newly reported comparison of groups with and without a rear tripod bag offers specific, attributable evidence about an optional shooting support, prompted by misses on small competition targets. Under Uncertainty and Necessary versus optional, the unquantified results support investigating the bag's convenience-versus-dispersion tradeoff, not a general performance verdict; this supports Pieces 01, 02, and 03. Commercial incentives requires noting the podcast's Cole-TAC tripod-accessory sponsorship, PRS's Zero Compromise marketing partnership, and EuroOptic's test-equipment loans; Exclusions keeps the firearm and reloading material out.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":30.24,"text":"All right, welcome back to the PRS podcast. Here with Cal Zant, the guru behind all of the data that you've seen published since 2012 on a Precision Rifle blog. Um now publishing on Instagram some of the really fascinating data and the uh Hall of Fame stuff of our sport. Um we got a great episode for you today. Cal, tell us about testing. Yeah, I am a data nerd, a testing nerd. Some of you guys know I have a hundred-yard underground range where the wind"},{"segment_id":"s-0002","start_seconds":30.24,"end_seconds":64.96,"text":"never blows and the mirage never shimmers. So I can test all kinds of stuff. We talk about a couple of the latest tests and what I saw that I haven't even published yet. Uh in fact, do you use a rear tripod as support? Have you ever tested your group size there? Might be something you want to listen to. A little tip Chad Heckler gave me. Maybe you want to listen. This is gonna be a good one, folks. You're ready. All"},{"segment_id":"s-0003","start_seconds":64.96,"end_seconds":94.96,"text":"right, welcome to another podcast here today with Cal Zant of the Precision Rifle Blog. Cal, thank you for joining us, buddy. Yeah, you bet. Been looking forward to it. Yeah, it's taking us a little bit to get uh get on a horn. I think uh we both had rescheduling uh of this of this podcast. Um man, uh the good ones are always hard to come by, I guess. So we're probably excited about it. Our calendars are probably too full. That's the truth of it. Uh agreed, agreed. Man, all right. Well,"},{"segment_id":"s-0012","start_seconds":337.04,"end_seconds":367.52,"text":"They kind of they look similar on forums. It's like whoever is the loudest is right. And uh it was also so early and long range back in 2009, but the shooting community is a whole kind of belief. Some some stuff that would surprise shooters today. Uh, we've learned a lot over the years. Uh let's take a brief break in the show to give a shout out to one of our partners. Coltac, official tripod accessory of the PRS. Coltac makes the kind of gear precision rifle shooters actually use. From support bags and ammo storage to suppressor covers, tripod"},{"segment_id":"s-0021","start_seconds":608.08,"end_seconds":638.24,"text":"And they were like, well, land nav is like part of the part of the competition, and uh really they just made it. There was lots of skills they were testing. I'll I'll say it that way. And it it really made me appreciate PRS matches, how they run, and where you can find targets and range them, and you know, get your dope and a plan together before the uh stage timer starts. The learning curve, those it it people can help you. You can see what uh you know, more experienced shooters do. Uh still safari"},{"segment_id":"s-0040","start_seconds":1185.04,"end_seconds":1215.04,"text":"to hey, I like that, that's what I'm gonna order, you know. Yeah, uh yeah, yeah. So so actually that's what made my views go way up. That was in uh July 2013. Uh okay. And really, I should ask a marketing person. I I'm not good at marketing or naming stuff, and so when I thought about it, I was like, well, I need to name this thing. Uh maybe accurate rifle blog. And then there's already an accurate rifle.com, and I thought that'll be confusing. And what about precision rifle blog? And I was like, Yeah, that sounds"},{"segment_id":"s-0041","start_seconds":1215.04,"end_seconds":1245.12,"text":"good. But like I said, I'd never heard of the precision rifle series. Uh it really only started uh yeah, that that same time. Yep, yep. It was in the first season and very small. Uh really is a bunch of outlaw matches, you know. Yeah, uh, but it wasn't long after that. I was on Sniper's Hide and I was scrolling through trying to figure out what rifle, what cartridge, and stuff like that. And I saw a forum post where they were talking about different brands of actions and stuff, scopes that the top PRS shooters were using. I was like, what's the PRS? And someone had"},{"segment_id":"s-0043","start_seconds":1275.2,"end_seconds":1305.44,"text":"Texan. Yeah, and uh he loved the idea, so I started crunching the data, making charts and stuff, uh really making it easier for you to know what it's saying without having to scroll and read every line. Uh and I published that uh actually September 7th, 2013. Uh so close to, I mean, that's 13 years ago, almost today. I also had to publish, so I went back and looked at this. I also had to publish what is the precision rifle"},{"segment_id":"s-0045","start_seconds":1336.4,"end_seconds":1366.64,"text":"I feel like I could do a survey and it could be it could help the data be more normalized. It'd be faster for them to take it. Uh, you wouldn't have to do it. And he was like, let's do it. So really I've been working with different PRS match directors over the years to collect that data. And uh also did a whole bunch of other like field tests, uh research projects. Some of them spent like 400 plus hours to complete and had me buying all kinds of like scientific equipment uh"},{"segment_id":"s-0046","start_seconds":1366.64,"end_seconds":1396.8,"text":"to measure stuff that has really never been done. It was a lot of fun. Well, what kind of yeah, what what in particular were you doing there for the for the bug? Like what kind of what kind of testing? Uh so I did this tactical scope field test where I bought every single optic over $1,500. Uh actually Euro Optic sent me several, but I had to buy some of them, gather them with friends, and uh I did all kinds of tests on them. You know, back then people would say things like, Well, you can't, you"},{"segment_id":"s-0059","start_seconds":1760.4,"end_seconds":1790.64,"text":"And really, I feel like that that one in particular pushed the industry. It it showed everyone what designs worked better, what the trade-offs were. I also measured like the ability to stay on target. So I hooked up lasers uh and basically would fire the rifle without any rear support. Uh, and I had a high speed camera, and I would watch how how much the muzzle would move, how far would you be off target afterwards uh on the high speed camera,"},{"segment_id":"s-0061","start_seconds":1820.96,"end_seconds":1851.2,"text":"in his eyes on a stage. Man, you can't see anymore. And the clock's going. So I mean, it affects everybody. Uh really Brian Litz and I see so eye to he he he think in one of his books he he put like, don't take anything for granted. You know, the bottom lines you got to deal in absolutes and fundamentals, and you got to measure everything, test it. And so I would try my best to try how could I control this in a way that I myself"},{"segment_id":"s-0072","start_seconds":2154.32,"end_seconds":2184.4,"text":"I I love that. And like I said, ultimately I do this for free just to try to help people because that's my heart, but that's the heart of a lot of people in this sport, lots of good people. Let's take a brief break in the show to give a shout out to one of our partners. Coltac, official tripod accessory of the PRS. Coltac makes the kind of gear precision rifle shooters actually use. From support bags and ammo storage to suppressor covers, tripod accessories, and other range essentials. Their products are well thought out, built to last, and designed around what shooters need on the firing line."},{"segment_id":"s-0081","start_seconds":2427.04,"end_seconds":2457.12,"text":"and really, as you shoot more matches, that sure helps. I I dry fired a lot more that season, too. I was shocked at how much dry fire helps. Uh I actually tracked my dry fire training, and so I could I could see the correlation between like how much I dry fired before a match and what place I got. Like it was very clear. Uh yeah. Did you ever publish that data? No, no, there's a hundred things that I've done that I just haven't published. I did this powder humidity"},{"segment_id":"s-0087","start_seconds":2608.32,"end_seconds":2639.28,"text":"me to test uh, you know, those rear admiral bags on the tripod uh that everybody's using uh like it's a bag that goes in between the rifle and a rear tripod uh that you can kind of connect to the rifle. Yep. And and WeBad has one also, but uh he was he watched me shoot one stage that was uh field fire targets, no no backdrop, and they were really small targets. And he said, Man, I"},{"segment_id":"s-0091","start_seconds":2730.56,"end_seconds":2760.56,"text":"just don't sometimes, you know, like we're just like, gosh, I could get steady faster with that. I'd actually practice so much with rear tripod. You know, without that, it's it's a skill to get on target. Yes. W when you just have the butt on on the tripod, it's a real skill, and I'd practice a ton to get that down. And then that bag came out, and I was like, well, I shouldn't have done that. Gosh, this is so easy. Uh yeah. And really, it only matters"},{"segment_id":"s-0095","start_seconds":2851.2,"end_seconds":2881.68,"text":"I'll tell you this like uh zero compromise, it was um I believe at the beginning of 2024 was the first year that we partnered with them, and our partnerships have a marketing component. And you know, we committed to doing marketing for zero compromise. And plain and simple, the rifle scope survey you did at the end of the year, plus the data points that we provided with the efforts we did, like reinforced"},{"segment_id":"s-0101","start_seconds":3032.64,"end_seconds":3062.72,"text":"single one, you know. And I have I have two impacts here that just arrived uh a week ago, and we're on serial number 14,000 and something. Yep. And I I've got some new ones. I again, some of those I just ordered online because I think they're the best action. Uh there's other good products. I'm not trying to dog anybody or uh there is. I think I heard this quote one time. It's uh uh building a great product without marketing is like winking at a girl"},{"segment_id":"s-0116","start_seconds":3491.6,"end_seconds":3521.6,"text":"Uh yeah. Yep. That makes sense. Uh what this morning and I think of the scope test. Actually, now now that I'm thinking about it, there's there's some losers in that scope test that made big claims that it's not that the most expensive one performed the best. In no test that I've ever done has has it been like clearly, everybody says you get what you pay for. Uh-huh. Uh, if you do objective tests, you will find that's not true, just on the"}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:Buzzsprout-19926608","version_id":"39069dbe30491be74f29dbcd1826fdb6474878e0f4b1ef9b66ee2062f90373a8","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"prs","title":"What the Data Says Actually Matters in Precision Rifle Shooting","url":"https://www.buzzsprout.com/2605409/episodes/19926608-what-the-data-says-actually-matters-in-precision-rifle-shooting.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-12T19:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Precision Rifle Series league podcast; league has many industry sponsors (e.g. Leica optics episode)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, Zant's newly disclosed comparison of rear tripod support with and without a bag offers a specific, transferable shooting-support observation: markedly different groups in a controlled 100-yard test, following trouble on small field targets. Under Uncertainty, this supports only an attributed preliminary finding and advice to test one's setup, since group measurements, sample sizes, and Mammoth field validation are missing; Exclusions rule out using the firearm and reloading material. Under Commercial incentives, flag PRS's Mile High advertising and Zero Compromise marketing partnership, plus Zant's retailer-supplied test loans and stated practice of paying for retained products.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.0,"end_seconds":30.16,"text":"All right, welcome back to the PRS podcast here with Cal Zant, the guru behind all of the data that you've seen published since 2012 on a precision rifle blog. Um, uh now publishing on Instagram some of the really fascinating data and the uh Hall of Fame stuff of our sport. Um, we got a great episode for you today. Cal, tell us about testing. Yeah, I am a data nerd, a testing nerd. Some of you guys know I have a hundred-yard underground range where the wind"},{"segment_id":"s-0002","start_seconds":30.16,"end_seconds":64.88,"text":"never blows and the mirage never shimmers. So I can test all kinds of stuff. We talk about a couple of the latest tests and what I saw that I haven't even published yet. Uh in fact, do you use a rear tripod as support? Have you ever tested your group size there? Might be something you want to listen to. A little tip Chad Heckler gave me. Maybe you want to listen. This is gonna be a good one, folks. All"},{"segment_id":"s-0008","start_seconds":216.0,"end_seconds":246.24,"text":"27 caliber bullet. Um I finally eventually got like a real my first accurate rifle. It was a uh a Rock River Barmiter AR-15, and that's what I started eating coyotes with. Shooting those 55 green B Max bullets. 223. Yep. Let's take a brief break in the show to give a shout-out to one of our partners, Mile High Shooting Accessories, the official rifle retailer of the PRS. We'd love to tell you about all the great products Mile High has for precision rifle shooters, but apparently YouTube gets a little nervous"},{"segment_id":"s-0009","start_seconds":246.24,"end_seconds":276.24,"text":"when we do that. So we'll keep it simple. Mile High is awesome. They carry many of the precision rifle brands you trust, plus a bunch of other stuff you need. And we highly recommend you go see for yourself. Go check them out. Milehighshooting.com. Two two three. Uh pretty heavy bull barrel, right? And a long forehand on that wasn't. Yeah, very heavy bull barrel. It was a uh Wilson combat barrel. Uh it was okay. I mean, with hand loads, they could do what everybody says online, quarter minute all day, you know, uh as long as you kept"},{"segment_id":"s-0015","start_seconds":427.44,"end_seconds":457.68,"text":"uh that's that's what I started with. I had a DPMS LR 308B that I that I go that was my first, yeah, that was my first entry into long-range stuff, you know, 308 out of an AR. Uh I will say we never hit that thousand-yard target with the 308, but uh a guy there had an old bench rifle, it was in 280 Ackley improved, and he let us shoot that and it was like magic, man. Uh uh honestly, it was a pretty small target. It was those old LaRue uh sniper targets,"},{"segment_id":"s-0021","start_seconds":609.36,"end_seconds":639.52,"text":"and uh really they just made it. There was lots of skills they were testing, I'll I'll say it that way. And it it really made me appreciate PRS matches, how they run, and where you can find targets and range them, and you know, get your dope and a plan together before the uh stage timer starts. The learning curve of those it people can help you. You can see what uh you know, more experienced shooters do. Uh still safari was hard. Hard, hard. Uh yeah, if"},{"segment_id":"s-0041","start_seconds":1214.0,"end_seconds":1244.0,"text":"of the precision rifle series. Uh it really only started uh yeah, that that same time. Yep, yep. It was in the first season and very small. Uh really is a bunch of outlaw matches, you know. Yeah, uh, but it wasn't long after that. I was on Sniper's Hide and I was scrolling through trying to figure out what rifle, what cartridge, and stuff like that. And I saw a forum post where they were talking about different brands of actions and stuff, scopes that the top PRS shooters were using. I was like, what's the PRS? And someone had pasted in there this huge"},{"segment_id":"s-0043","start_seconds":1274.08,"end_seconds":1304.08,"text":"uh he loved the idea, so I started crunching the data, making charts and stuff, uh, really making it easier for you to know what it's saying without having to scroll and read every line. Uh and I published that uh actually September 7th, 2013. Uh so close to, I mean, that's 13 years ago, almost today. I also had to publish. So I went back and looked at this. I also had to publish what is the precision rifle series. So I had an article"},{"segment_id":"s-0045","start_seconds":1334.16,"end_seconds":1364.16,"text":"feel like I could do a survey and it could be they could help the data be more normalized. It it'd be faster for them to take it. Uh you wouldn't have to do it. And he was like, let's do it. So really I've been working with different PRS match directors over the years to collect that data. And uh also did a whole bunch of other like field tests, uh research projects. Some of them spent like 400 plus hours to complete and had me buying all kinds of like scientific equipment uh"},{"segment_id":"s-0059","start_seconds":1757.92,"end_seconds":1788.16,"text":"And really, I feel like that that one in particular pushed the industry. It it showed everyone what designs worked better, what the trade-offs were. I also measured like the ability to stay on target. So I hooked up lasers uh and basically would fire the rifle without any rear support. Uh, and I had a high speed camera, and I would watch how how much the muzzle would move, how far would you be off target afterwards uh on the high speed camera?"},{"segment_id":"s-0061","start_seconds":1818.48,"end_seconds":1848.72,"text":"in his eyes on a stage. Man, you can't see anymore. And the clock's going. So, I mean, it affects everybody. Uh, but really, Brian Litz and I see so eye, he he he think in one of his books he he put like, don't take anything for granted. You know, the bottom lines, you got to deal in absolutes and fundamentals, and you got to measure everything, test it. And so I would try my best to try how can I control this in a way that I myself"},{"segment_id":"s-0072","start_seconds":2152.8,"end_seconds":2182.96,"text":"that. And like I said, ultimately I do this for free just to try to help people because that's my heart, but that's the heart of a lot of people in this sport, lots of good people. Let's take a brief break in the show to give a shout-out to one of our partners, Mile High Shooting Accessories, the official rifle retailer of the PRS. We'd love to tell you about all the great products Mile High has for precision rifle shooters, but apparently YouTube gets a little nervous when we do that. So we'll keep it simple. Mile High is awesome. They carry many of the precision rifle brands you trust, plus a bunch of other stuff you need. And we highly recommend you"},{"segment_id":"s-0081","start_seconds":2426.32,"end_seconds":2457.04,"text":"I I dry fired a lot more that season, too. I was shocked at how much dry fire helps. Uh I actually tracked my dry fire training and so I could I could see the correlation between like how much I dry fired before a match and what place I got. Like it was very clear. Uh yeah. Did you ever publish that data? No, no, there's a hundred things that I've done that I just haven't published. I did this powder humidity test that still I haven't published. I should do that soon. That is very interesting, super interesting."},{"segment_id":"s-0087","start_seconds":2609.68,"end_seconds":2639.68,"text":"using uh like it's a bag that goes in between the rifle and a rear tripod uh that you can kind of connect to the rifle. Yep. And and WeBad has one also, but uh he was he watched me shoot one stage that was uh field fire targets, no no backdrop, and they were really small targets. And he said, Man, I I didn't do well on that stage. I did well on most stages, but it kind of bitted on that one. And he said, I think you should."},{"segment_id":"s-0088","start_seconds":2640.32,"end_seconds":2671.44,"text":"Go test uh your group size with that rear admiral bag on the tripod. And I said, Yeah. And he said, Yep, I think you should. I think you'll be surprised by what you found. And I said, You're saying you think that might have been why I was missing? And he said, You should test it. And I was like, Well, okay. And I went and tested it in the 100-yard range, and I have those results to publish as well. Uh, it was very interesting. It's cool. I'm not everybody"},{"segment_id":"s-0090","start_seconds":2702.08,"end_seconds":2732.48,"text":"And my groups with with and without that bag were markedly different. Everybody should test that. Uh, but I I will say I I was surprised. Uh yeah. Chad had obviously already tested it and stuff like that. All of us, we could go test it. We just don't sometimes, you know, like we're just like, gosh, I could get steady faster with that. I'd actually practice"},{"segment_id":"s-0091","start_seconds":2732.48,"end_seconds":2762.72,"text":"so much with rear tripod. You know, without that, it's it's a skill to get on target. Yes. W when you just have the butt on on the tripod, it's a real skill, and I'd practice a ton to get that down. And then that bag came out, and I was like, well, I shouldn't have done that. Gosh, this is so easy. Uh yeah. And really, it only matters if the targets are really small. So I'm not saying nobody can hit with that. Lots of people do. Maybe on most stages, you should use it."},{"segment_id":"s-0095","start_seconds":2854.96,"end_seconds":2885.12,"text":"the first year that we partnered with them, and our partnerships have a marketing component. And you know, we committed to doing marketing for zero compromise. And plain and simple, the rifle scope survey you did at the end of the year, plus the data points that we provided with the efforts we did, like reinforced that relationship for the following year. So it was like, yeah, I I you know, I I can't I can't say enough uh good things about the survey and"},{"segment_id":"s-0101","start_seconds":3036.32,"end_seconds":3066.48,"text":"and something. Yep. And I I've got some new ones. I again, some of those I just ordered online because I think they're the best action. Uh there's other good products. I'm not trying to dog anybody or uh I think I heard this quote one time. It's uh uh building a great product without marketing is like winking at a girl in a dark room. You know what you're doing, but nobody else does, you know. Uh so if I can help readers connect"}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:Buzzsprout-19936395","version_id":"8742920b5f44dd5bcabbdb47a5728f935ce553adfa9fea4f8e99b0c6cc9fa295","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"prs","title":"Three World Titles… What’s Next for Austin Buschman?","url":"https://www.buzzsprout.com/2605409/episodes/19936395-three-world-titles-what-s-next-for-austin-buschman.mp3","published_at":"2026-10-09T17:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Precision Rifle Series league podcast; league has many industry sponsors (e.g. Leica optics episode)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, recent practitioner accounts provide citable lessons for Mammoth gear usability: Buschman entered 1,062 rather than 1,162 yards into his Kestrel, and another shooter reported correcting stale temperature data before engaging targets. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require noting the PRS league's industry sponsorships, including Leica, and treating these as attributed field experiences rather than independent product validation; the item supports Piece 01's new field lessons and Piece 03's guidance on checking ballistic inputs.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.56,"end_seconds":30.72,"text":"You know, after hundreds of thousands of rounds, 10 years of shooting, 102-day matches, 200 plus 1 day matches, and Austin Bushman finally finds out what it's like to be on the losing end of edge hits. Blake's got himself a library. Yeah. Yeah. He hasn't read any of those books. Out of one. Twice. Oh"},{"segment_id":"s-0029","start_seconds":856.0,"end_seconds":886.0,"text":"Yep. Yeah. Wow. I'll look it through the I'll go ahead. Oh, I was gonna say, so Clay, your qualifier score is 96.530, so you'll go into the finale with 296.530. This qualifier score score than 100. Oh, no, I'm sorry, that is a qualifier. So yep. It doesn't change anything. Well, it doesn't change anything. It still maths out. What were we gonna say, Austin? I'm just looking through the MPA. I didn't realize how stacked that match was gonna be."},{"segment_id":"s-0032","start_seconds":946.8,"end_seconds":976.88,"text":"about that. KRG this weekend, uh, Clay, what uh what was it like out there? And congrats on the win. Thank you. Um you know, I've said it a bunch of times. I think that the good shooters put on good matches. And um, if you haven't shot some of the outwest matches, sometimes you can get into some uh gimmicky props uh a little bit. Um I mean we shot I think we shot two stages where tripod was all you had. Like you had to shoot off the tripod, no if ands or buts about it, you"},{"segment_id":"s-0033","start_seconds":976.88,"end_seconds":1006.88,"text":"just had to shoot it off the tripod, but they were made to be uh hunting stages. You were hunting you're shooting coyotes on each one of these, which was um um you had a ton of really tiny targets, you know, a lot of two tenth targets, bunch of three-tenth targets. Um tripods could, I'm assuming, clipped in or bagged, right? Either one, you could do it any way you wanted, but but your tripod had to, no matter what stage it was, including those where you were only shooting off the tripod, it had to be collapsed and folded"},{"segment_id":"s-0044","start_seconds":1309.68,"end_seconds":1340.0,"text":"I was like, Well, that was strange, and the next target is further, but it's only two tenths worth, and it's a circle. And I was like, I'm just gonna hedge my bed here, I'm gonna die my dope because like this dope. Like the next target stope would have barely hit this target. So, like, I can't trust that that's gonna work on the next target. So I dialed that and then I held top of that target and I still hit low. I was like, and I hit that target twice. I was like, that's kind of weird, whatever. So the next target is a another coyote, and it's extremely missable. That coyote had not been fed well. And"},{"segment_id":"s-0047","start_seconds":1400.56,"end_seconds":1430.88,"text":"underneath it, and then you go low upwind underneath it, right? Yeah, that's very, very typical outcome right there. Yeah. So what did you watch prior to this that said, okay, this is this is what we've been seeing. Here's how I'm gonna re-engage this thing. You know, I've shot at this point 162 shots leading into that stage, um, including hitting a two-tenth tall coyote a thousand three. Like, my gun's fine. My gun has no issues,"},{"segment_id":"s-0058","start_seconds":1737.12,"end_seconds":1767.12,"text":"would say that it's extremely rare for me to go shoot zero um on on any of the days. Like I'll shoot it on Friday if I'm with somebody like you know Austin or Austin or whoever that I know so that we can compare, make sure something you know crazy is not going on. But otherwise, I'm not shooting the match at 100 yards. So, how much data is it really gonna give me? I'm either gonna shoot a teeny tiny group exactly where I want, or anything else happens. And if I've got 10 stages of data and I didn't drop my rifle off of a"},{"segment_id":"s-0059","start_seconds":1767.12,"end_seconds":1797.12,"text":"cliff between days, there's really nothing I'm gonna gain from that. I didn't even think of that with all those you know perfectly painted white targets that Shannon puts out for us. I should have just zeroed on those Saturday morning. I I know Austin, what do you do? So I shoot a lot of zero boards now. I didn't used to. Um since I started shooting six creed, I started cleaning my barrel a little bit between shooting days just because I feel like there's a"},{"segment_id":"s-0064","start_seconds":1917.92,"end_seconds":1948.0,"text":"may have seen the videos, but he had a uh you know a drill, and he was having to drill into the rock to set targets because you couldn't pound them in. And so that would be a nightmare to have to do it. But but but once once you're done, once that hole's there, you can do limitless things and never have to worry about like resetting a target. I mean, you can just put that post in there and walk away, and you're never gonna have to worry about you know something pushing it over, you know, rain,"},{"segment_id":"s-0068","start_seconds":2039.12,"end_seconds":2069.44,"text":"like the four of us who, you know, maybe our neighbors and be like, hey, let's invite a few more guys, get the minimum number, and just have a match in Austin's backyard, right? Like that's why the rule exists. But Nick calling me last night and saying, like, hey, can I can I just run a one day here in a couple weeks? You know, and like, yeah, absolutely. You're gonna have it's gonna be a legit match. Um, you're gonna have more than enough shooters show up for it, right? So um sounds like an awesome time. All right, let's uh let's actually back up. Let's talk"},{"segment_id":"s-0069","start_seconds":2069.44,"end_seconds":2099.44,"text":"about worlds here. Uh Austin Bushman, congrats on your third world championship. Uh read your post last night uh that you that you put out about uh about the experience and the trophy and all that. I and I guess uh for those that aren't friends with you on Facebook, um, which I'm sure most everybody isn't, right? Like you're repulsive. But anyways, why don't you explain what's explain explain? I'm kidding, of course. Uh explain what's in your post and and what you plan to do with the"},{"segment_id":"s-0072","start_seconds":2160.08,"end_seconds":2190.64,"text":"yards away, and it's like, Austin, you should you know, maybe go give her a hug. So the other thing, the other thing about uh awesome wives that support PRS shooters is I told and I just told Cale and I were talking about this. We're like, we could go win the world championship, and if our wife wasn't there, we could go in the house when we get home, and she'd be like, How'd it go? We're like, Oh, I won. And she'd be like, Oh, cool. Hey, can you take out the trash? Which"},{"segment_id":"s-0080","start_seconds":2401.36,"end_seconds":2431.52,"text":"planning on shooting plenty of PRS. It's just the world championship itself and the qualification process became more of a burden and more of like just me putting pressure on myself than it was fun, and I and uh I just don't want to do it any anymore. So I'm giving that up. But the trophy is really cool. I think it's the the coolest looking trophy that I've seen in the precision rifle world, and I think it would be cool to make the tradition of giving it to the next"},{"segment_id":"s-0081","start_seconds":2431.52,"end_seconds":2461.76,"text":"open champion at each world championship. So that's the trophy right there. That's a pretty cool trophy. That is that is a very cool trophy. So I will be, you know, Lord willing, I'm still around in two years to give it to the next champion, but I will be delivering that to the next champion and engraving their name on it uh below mine in 2028. So what um what is this made out of? Is this cast of metal? It's some kind of 3D printed nylon"},{"segment_id":"s-0095","start_seconds":2855.44,"end_seconds":2885.84,"text":"right, and I finished it at two tenths and three-tenths left. Oh boy. And I knocked the middle out of it every time and got a nine for timing out, you know. Like hit it just, you know, the 07, you know, didn't you know you get not that it matters, but you get used to 0.3, and so when you don't get it, it's like, ah man, I'd really like to have that extra, you know, two tenths of a second there. But I thought I just explained that though, at the IPRF championships, there is no extra 0.3. When the beeper"},{"segment_id":"s-0120","start_seconds":3613.44,"end_seconds":3643.92,"text":"fun about it, I was the first shooter up, first stage of the match. 1162 yards is not what you want to send your first shots at. And I put 1,062 in my kestrel instead of 1162. Oh, yeah. So I shoot my first, I shoot my first wheel. You gotta wait a second and a half for it to get there. So I'm waiting, and I just see the grass down below, a little puff. You did see it. Way, way down below. Everybody saw it. There were audible gas from the cloud. So"},{"segment_id":"s-0126","start_seconds":3795.44,"end_seconds":3825.6,"text":"we both dropped one there. I think I think I dropped two. You dropped two there, I dropped one. Then the next stage is the the one MOA squares or whatever that you're prone, eight hundred, nine hundred looking through the soupy barrage on the grass. And so I dropped two there, and Jeff dropped one. And so we're down five shots through three stages at KM, and we're both pretty sure that it's over. Like we even said, we audibly said, like, oh, better luck next year. And uh then we"},{"segment_id":"s-0144","start_seconds":4339.44,"end_seconds":4369.68,"text":"sh like, what guys, what's going on? Like, is there something wrong with your data? Like, that's why I asked the question earlier. Do you trust your data? You know, my my gun hammered the all but the first stage the day before, so what do I do? I'm right next to the zero board, but I'm like, it would depend. I would pay attention if I saw Austin and Clay ahead of me both do something like that. I would absolutely be paying attention. And the two shooters I saw go in front of me, I um I didn't trust that they were level"},{"segment_id":"s-0157","start_seconds":4731.84,"end_seconds":4762.32,"text":"for our last stage would have been really nice. I wish I could have shot stage 10 over at the end of the match because I had just come off of cleaning everything, and I'm like, man, if I could go over there and shoot those four stupid circles again, like I know I would center punch them. Yeah. Yeah. It was a fun day. I definitely did not have any kind of nerves or anything going into it because I went to bed thinking I was out of it at nine down. Literally was just trying to go. I was like, man, whatever. Get to, hey, I get to go shoot with Austin Bushman"}]}
{"item_id":"podcast:Buzzsprout-19841361","version_id":"8792cb24dcc7a443b2ea671065a5c5948c6944d31520fcb93baf6163d52c5523","kind":"podcast","publisher_id":"prs","title":"New Leica Products—and What You Can Do With Them","url":"https://www.buzzsprout.com/2605409/episodes/19841361-new-leica-products-and-what-you-can-do-with-them.mp3","published_at":"2026-09-21T20:00:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"practitioner","notes":"Precision Rifle Series league podcast; league has many industry sponsors (e.g. Leica optics episode)."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Freshness and novelty, and Credibility, the newly announced Geovid Max supplies citable manufacturer specifications and described prototype ranging observations, while display customization, Kestrel integration, and battery management address competition usability and team coordination. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require identifying Boykin as a Leica employee, Miller as a Leica contractor, and PRS as a Leica partner; their performance praise supplies no independent confirmation, and independent testing in Mammoth conditions remains missing. This best supports Piece 01 as a new-product report with specifications and field claims clearly attributed.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"segment_id":"s-0001","start_seconds":0.08,"end_seconds":35.68,"text":"Welcome back to the PRS Podcast. Here with Hamilton Boykin and Jared Miller. We're going to talk about some very cool Leica products coming to market and what we can do with those. Hey there. I'm Hamilton Boykin with Leica, and I'm here to talk a little bit about our history and the technology we're building into some great new products coming out. Hey, I'm Jared Miller. If you're looking for any new range finding binos or optics in general for any of your find range and engage activities, you're going to be stoked on what's coming out. You're ready. All"},{"segment_id":"s-0004","start_seconds":96.0,"end_seconds":126.0,"text":"so I've been involved in uh uh developing our products for a while. Um I may basically I take feedback from our market and feed it into uh the the product managers in Germany and Portugal. Um and then I take the product and take engineer speak and and sort of translate that into what that means to the to the end consumer. So a little bit of translation in both directions. Okay, okay. Jared? I'm Jared Miller. I'm uh I'm based in Montana. I'm really like a hunter first, but that kind of led me down"},{"segment_id":"s-0005","start_seconds":126.0,"end_seconds":156.32,"text":"the path of precision rifle. Like I like to say that I'm kind of a precision projectile enthusiast because I like bows too. And uh man, just uh that led me into you know shooting PRS and uh all types of precision rifle and team matches and all that stuff. And then uh now nowadays I'm doing uh a variety of stuff. I I do a lot of contracting for Leica, uh producing videos for them. I've done a lot of training with the team there. Uh fortunately get to share a lot of my uh product feedback as a non-employee."},{"segment_id":"s-0017","start_seconds":488.96,"end_seconds":519.28,"text":"little uh confusing at times, but uh yeah. Um uh we we've just before your time. Yeah. Yeah. Um I was still in high school then. Um but uh yeah we we uh for for in internally, you know, there are three there have been three different body designs. So we call the series the GeoVid 1, 2, and 3. So that that Star Wars model would be the body style Geovid one. Um the what you see is the Govid R these days um with the closed bridge single hinge design, that's uh the Giovid 2 body design. And then the open bridge uh dual hinge"},{"segment_id":"s-0033","start_seconds":973.68,"end_seconds":1004.0,"text":"uh it's uh uh to to to know when the unit's you know up to your eye and when it's not, yeah, for battery saving. Yeah, so it automatically goes off when you turn it in the vertical, put it back in your bino harness. So it's like just if if you're the type of guy that likes to save uh you know uh battery, but you want to be connected to your Kestrel, if that's what you're into, man, like it it does that without eating through battery. So yeah, pretty stoke, man. I don't do a lot of stuff, but they're pretty good. See, um see you shoot a bunch of hunter style matches, NRL Hunter, ranging engage type type stuff. What"},{"segment_id":"s-0034","start_seconds":1004.0,"end_seconds":1034.08,"text":"uh you shot uh I can see on your impact scoring shooter profile. You've done a bunch of PRS Pro Series matches. Um what's your like if if you could you could only select one preference going forward, like what kind of match would you shoot? If I'm being honest, like the my favorite matches, my my favorite match on the planet to go shoot is uh is it uh uh Brian and Seth down in Idaho? Um the Snake River match. Snake River. Yep. So and the PRS match."},{"segment_id":"s-0039","start_seconds":1156.32,"end_seconds":1186.64,"text":"yeah. I guess talk talk about what's what you can actually see in there. It is customizable, like you said, right? Yep. You want to hit that, Jonathan? Well, it's starting out at the basics, um the just the the the brightness and the um the resolution of the display is the brightest display on the market as far as I know. Um and uh that's been complete uh repeatedly uh beaten into our heads uh by the the product managers that actually go and source them the you know the the technology. Um and it's also uh"},{"segment_id":"s-0050","start_seconds":1489.92,"end_seconds":1520.24,"text":"you know, like great glass, great laser, but it would just take and it seemed like a forever, it was probably half a second, right? You know, it would just it would tell you your elevation, and then the display would would would rotate and it would tell you your your windage, you know, a range, elevation, windage, right? And it would just take a little bit to cycle through that, which in a hunting scenario, like probably not a big deal, right? Like, I mean, if you've been stalking an animal for a long time and like, okay, uh, we're getting ready to take a shot, like, you know, hopefully"},{"segment_id":"s-0057","start_seconds":1701.76,"end_seconds":1732.16,"text":"were they work really hard on these products, but uh you know, there is a uh like anything else, there they're there you have limited resources uh and you and you uh you do what you can with what you have. And we we've really focused on trying to do one product that is absolutely the best that we can um rather than trying to do a bunch of different products that that you know, I'd rather have one product that's got everything in it, uh at least to start. Um and and to talk about that. You know, we started talking about the display earlier. A big thing about this, we we talked about the speed, we talked about how clear the display is and how programmable"},{"segment_id":"s-0059","start_seconds":1762.16,"end_seconds":1792.24,"text":"is reflecting off my skin around my eye before I get the binocular up to my eye. It's that bright. It's amazing how bright this thing is. So uh yeah, that was a big customer feedback thing. You know, for my eyes in this condition, it's not bright enough. Well, we made one that it when it's as bright as it can be, it's gonna be too bright. Uh testing it in in bright sky, not at the sun, but right next to the sun out in Utah two weeks ago. You know, hot, hazy, white bright sky. As bright a place as I could find, I could still see the the display clearly um in the field"},{"segment_id":"s-0064","start_seconds":1913.68,"end_seconds":1943.76,"text":"the most uh you know safe level of laser that's been what we've always used in in laser range miners, which is a class one, not a class one M or a class three. And so we're we're we're getting this max performance out of the the out of out of a lower intensity, lower milliwattage of energy being put out um than what uh has oftentimes been seen before. Um so it's it's kind of the best of both worlds. You got a got a safe product and a uh and a and a product that's get that's getting out there. That's awesome."},{"segment_id":"s-0066","start_seconds":1974.16,"end_seconds":2004.48,"text":"for a while, but it was was less safe um uh to to the eye if it was shined on somebody. Yep, yep. So this is all class one, which is which is interesting. What uh yeah, I don't I don't know if you can really describe, but like what what are we able to do now? Like how did the technology change that's like hey, we can do this, we can go 6,000 yards on a rock face with a class one laser because there's something different about this one, right? Yeah, there it part of it is the um the the the algorithm,"},{"segment_id":"s-0072","start_seconds":2155.52,"end_seconds":2185.92,"text":"gonna be ranging the animal's foot, right? So I'm in the hillside where he's at, but I want to make sure that I'm getting through all the branches and that. But if I'm hunting antelope, a lot of time antelope wearing flat, and then there's gonna be stage brush, or they sometimes they're out in the field or whatever. And I want to make sure I want I want to be scanning across them, and when I hit that antelope, I want the first thing that I get. And then other times it's just like it's so convoluted, there's just so much stuff out there. I'm gonna I'm gonna do my best to hold my reticle right on it because I always test where your reticle hits and picks up. I'm"},{"segment_id":"s-0074","start_seconds":2216.0,"end_seconds":2246.0,"text":"for a long time and having a cut uh a heads-up display with all the data there, um, it's really nice now with the Leica because I could choose what I want to see and what I don't want to see. Uh, but it it it lets me know, like, hey, is my compass off or something? Or or that corporate correct? I want to be able to throw my wind in really quickly, and I could see right in the heads-up display that it's there. So for me with my OCD and wanting to control every little thing, these things kind of line right up with the way I like to operate. Heck yeah, heck yeah. That I I think that was the best explanation I've I've ever"},{"segment_id":"s-0089","start_seconds":2669.36,"end_seconds":2699.36,"text":"or it'll turn off automatically after 10 seconds if Bluetooth is off. Because the and the reason for this is because the display is the big drain. So if that display's on and it's powering that energy, then it that that that's what's burning up the battery. It's not the Bluetooth so much as it is the display. Um so uh the and the other thing is that e i if you set it to even just that one minute setting, the connection reliability and speed between the rangefinder and the kestrel is so good and so fast that the moment you power"},{"segment_id":"s-0099","start_seconds":2971.84,"end_seconds":3001.92,"text":"compasses in these, uh as far as I know, the plus minus for the price point of all the products on the market right now, your plus-minus uh good degree of error is is five degrees uh uh on a compass bearing. Um but you get variation in accuracy within that based on the product itself. Um but you can correct for it. So if you drop a pen and an elk across a canyon and the elk is to the left of that tree and the pen lands to the right of that tree, you can move the pen to where the elk actually is and"},{"segment_id":"s-0112","start_seconds":3364.88,"end_seconds":3395.12,"text":"time and resources to build that, but I think we're getting there. You know, I think I think the future is gonna be pretty cool in this uh this area. So um yeah, uh anything else? Uh anything else you guys want to mention about Leica in general or product development, that type of stuff. I don't know, man. For for me on the outside, I'm not I'm not, I I do some contracting for them, but I don't work for Leica. But like just seeing, like you referenced, you know, all the the manufacturers, sometimes they get in a um, you know, and they kind of live in a box and they do that. Well, Leica's"}]}
{"item_id":"feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/camping-outdoor-blankets","version_id":"21170f66f8d79e6d047f2ae7e19207116c5accd7b06e05d7a10f3accdcf682a5","kind":"feed","publisher_id":"treelinereview","title":"Best Outdoor and Camping Blankets of 2026","url":"https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/camping-outdoor-blankets","published_at":"2026-10-01T13:51:00Z","source_notes":{"perspective":"independent","notes":"Hands-on outdoor gear testing: boots, socks, gloves, layers, sleep systems, packs. Affiliate model."},"selection_reason":"Under Relevance, Credibility, and Freshness and novelty, this updated guide supplies transferable sleep-system and cold-weather evidence: timed 0.5-liter leakage tests, Backcountry Blanket use in freezing temperatures and 35 mph winds, and the Original Puffy's insufficient warmth on a night in the 40s. Commercial incentives and Uncertainty require noting Treeline's affiliate commissions and treating its findings as one publisher's evidence; Rumpl's claimed percentage improvements remain unverified, but the tests support Pieces 01 and 02 and inform optional-layer weight and warmth tradeoffs for Piece 03 under Necessary versus optional.","excerpted":true,"passages":[{"text":"Best Outdoor and Camping Blankets of 2026\n\nPuffy Blankets, Picnic Blankets, Waterproof Blankets, and Fleece and Down Blankets for Camping, Travel, Festivals, and Cabins\n\nOctober 1st, 2026, updated with four new winnersHome >Gear Reviews >Camping\n\nCamping blankets are not just about staying cozy. They are built for the dirt, mud, spills, and damp conditions that come with spending time outside. Unlike the old sheet or bed comforter many of us used to haul along, a good outdoor blanket should be easy to clean, resistant to moisture, and compact enough that you will actually bring it.\n\nI first encountered puffy outdoor blankets at a trade show in 2016 and have been using and evaluating them ever since. For this update, I took them on 40 flights, desert trips in Joshua Tree and the Mojave, a month-long summer camp in the Eastern Sierra with other testers, five-day fishing trips in Montana, and outdoor festivals in Oregon. We compared water resistance by suspending each blanket like a hammock, pouring half a liter of water onto it, and timing how long it took to leak through. Some held the water for more than six hours without fully wetting through.\n\nWe evaluated each blanket for warmth"},{"text":", water resistance, weight, packed size, durability, comfort, and ease of cleaning. We also considered whether it worked best as an insulating layer, a ground cover, or a blanket that could reasonably do both.\n\nTop Pick: The Rumpl Original Puffy Blanket earns our Best Overall award. It stood out for its water resistance, durable recycled construction, easy machine washability, and useful balance of warmth and packability. In our spill test, it did not begin leaking through the stitching for 15 minutes and was still holding most of the water three hours later. Corner loops make it easier to secure in windy conditions, while its range of sizes and designs works for solo camping, festivals, road trips, and everyday outdoor use.\n\nNote: This guide includes insulated blankets designed primarily for warmth, durable ground blankets for picnics and damp campsites, and versatile options that can handle both jobs.\n\nWe create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission — without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for su"},{"text":"pporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.\n\nComparison table\n\nThe Winners\n\nBest Outdoor Blanket: Rumpl Original Puffy Blanket\n\nBest uses: Camping, picnics, festivalsSizes available: 28\" x 40\" (mini) / 38\" x 52\" (compact throw) / 52\" x 75\" (1-person/full) / 84\" x 80\" (2-person)Insulation: 100% recycled 3D hollow fiber siliconized synthetic insulation; 240 gsmShell material: 100% recycled 30D ripstop polyester fabricPackable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: Machine washableWhat we liked: refined stitching and quality, fun designs and colors, variety of sizes, eco-friendly company, corner loopsWhat we didn't like: price, face material is shiny\n\nThe Rumpl Original Puffy Blanket is what you think of when you think of a puffy blanket for outdoor use. It's soft, comes in beautiful designs, is well-stitched, and comes with a convenient tote bag that makes it pack down to a small size. It works well to break the chill around a campfire (or on the couch!). It's a shareable alternative to a sleeping bag on warm days and adds extra warmth on top of a sleeping bag on cold nights. We even used it as a picnic blanket at festivals because it's so packable.\n\nBest "},{"text":"yet, Rumpl is one of the most eco-friendly outdoor brands and is a Climate Neutral Certified brand where 100% of the shell and insulation are post-consumer recycled materials and 100 plastic bottles are recycled for each double blanket.\n\nThere are still more color and print options with Rumpl than other brands, so we still think it's the best camping blanket.\n\nThe Rumpl Original comes in beautiful designs that support artists, like this blanket by Tlingit and Athabascan artist Crystal Worl. Photo by Kate Hoch.\n\nThe Rumpl Original Puffy blanket can be used instead of a sleeping bag on warm nights. Photo by Kate Hoch.\n\nUpdates\n\nNew for this year, the Rumpl Original is available in mini and throw sizes designed for kids or pets.\n\nSizes\n\nThe Rumpl Original Puffy blanket is available in three sizes—a junior, a single (75x52 inches) and a double (84×80 inches). In our experience, the single can easily be shared with two people when using it as a picnic blanket. If the couple is sharing a double camp chair like the Kelty Low, the single should be fine, too. But if you're looking to use the blanket at night instead of a sleeping bag, you'll want the double, no matter how well you know the "},{"text":"aterial: 88% post-consumer recycled polyesterPackable: YesWater-resistant: YesWashing: YesWhat we liked: waterproofness, dimensions, grippiness, repels sand, recycled material, designs, corner loopsWhat we didn't like: price, does not come with stuff sack or stuffable pocket\n\nThe Nomadix Festival Blanket is our winner for best picnic blanket. It's so waterproof, it never wetted out or leaked in over 3 hours of testing supporting half a liter of water. It’s made of a durable material that doesn’t stretch or warp over time and washes. It's grippy enough to use as a hot yoga mat. It has corner loops so it doesn't blow away. I had always thought an old sheet was sufficient enough for a picnic blanket until testing the Festival Blanket, and now that I've seen how good things can be, I never want to go back.\n\nWe like that the Nomadix packs down small. Like the Rumpl Everywhere Mat, it also has loops on the corner to prevent it from blowing away in the wind. Our one wish for it is that it had a stuff sack or folded into its own pocket. Still, the size, waterproofing, eco-friendly material, and price all make it a winner.\n\nThe Nomadix Festival Blanket makes a great porch for your camping t"},{"text":"that the most important feature of most outdoor blankets is keeping you warm. However, warmth is a balance between price, packability, and weight. Think about what season and where you'll be using your camping blanket. What are the average temperatures? Will you be using it overnight? We rank each blanket according to warmth so you can choose the blanket according to your needs. Pair a camping blanket with a fleece jacket for the ultimate in coziness.\n\nMachine Washable Vs Hand Wash\n\nFor blankets that you sit on, like picnic blankets and beach blankets, machine washable is a must since they are likely to encounter dirt more frequently than other camp blankets. Even for camping blankets, machine washable is nice since chances are you'll be getting campfire smoke on the blanket with some regularity.\n\nStain Resistance\n\nWhen you're outdoors, stains happen to your gear. Whether it's mud or grass stains or accidentally dropping the s'mores on the way from the campfire to your mouth (oops!), I've developed a number of stains on my camp blankets during testing. The best camp blankets have some Durable Water Repellent(DWR) treatment that prevents some of the staining, though in my experience"},{"text":"t more upfront.\n\nHow warm are camping blankets?\n\nWarmth varies widely depending on insulation weight, fill type, and blanket size. None of the camping blankets we tested carry standardized temperature ratings like sleeping bags do, so comparisons between brands rely on fill weight and real-world testing. As a general guide, lighter blankets under 20 oz work well for campfire sitting and mild summer nights, while heavier insulated options like the Rumpl Backcountry Blanket can handle cold ridgelines and frosty desert mornings.\n\nIs down or synthetic insulation better for a camping blanket?\n\nDown offers the best warmth-to-weight ratio and compresses smaller than synthetic fill, but it loses insulating power when wet and is far more difficult to wash, often requiring specialty detergent and hours of drying time. Synthetic insulation stays warm even when damp, machine washes easily, and costs less, but is bulkier for the same warmth. For blankets that will see regular outdoor use and washing, synthetic is the more practical choice. For travel or home use where packability matters most and washing is infrequent, down is worth the tradeoff.\n\nAre camping blankets waterproof?\n\nWaterproofnes"},{"text":"s varies significantly by type. Puffy camping blankets with DWR-treated shells repel light moisture and beaded water but will eventually soak through. Purpose-built ground blankets and picnic blankets, like the Nomadix Festival Blanket, are closer to fully waterproof and are designed to sit on wet grass or damp ground. In our testing, the best picnic blankets held 0.5 liters of water for over three hours without leaking through.\n\nAre camping blankets safe near a campfire?\n\nMost camping blankets are not fireproof and can melt, scorch, or develop burn holes from stray embers. Standard synthetic and down shells are particularly vulnerable. The exception is the Rumpl NanoLoft Flame Resistant Blanket, which uses a modacrylic and cotton shell specifically designed to resist sparks and flame contact, making it the only blanket in this guide we would confidently recommend for sitting close to an open fire.\n\nCan a camping blanket replace a sleeping bag?\n\nGenerally not in cold or unpredictable conditions. A camping blanket works well as a standalone sleep layer on warm summer nights or as extra warmth layered on top of a sleeping bag when temperatures drop. Unlike a sleeping bag, a blanket d"},{"text":"ed women’s rain boots for waterproofing, traction, comfort, fit, and durability through downpours, muddy trails, farm work, and everyday walking."}]}


